ent loads  |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2524:|                                   | every origin before lowering;     |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2525:|                                   | `:yin.k/binding :named` and       |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2526:|                                   | `:positional` variants with lift  |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2527:|                                   | and lower tables;                 |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2528:|                                   | `:binding-mismatch` (7.3).        |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2529:| Assertion signatures, retraction  | Agreed. Distinct signed envelopes |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2530:| binding, and replay               | for assert and retract covering   |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2531:|                                   | op, name, manifest, principal,    |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2532:|                                   | sequence; retraction bound by the |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2533:|                                   | assertion's content id;           |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2534:|                                   | per-principal monotone sequence   |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2535:|                                   | with floors, replay and           |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2536:|                                   | equivocation discarded (8.2).     |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2537:|                                   | Divergence: a sequence rather     |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2538:|                                   | than nonces, so the snapshot      |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2539:|                                   | check is stateless; timestamps    |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2540:|                                   | are audit-only.                   |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2541:| One manifest contract failed      | Agreed; r3 defect. `:yin.module/  |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2542:| stack and register links          | schema` versions the container;   |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2543:|                                   | `:yin.module/contracts` maps each |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2544:|                                   | format to its execution contract; |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2545:|                                   | step 0 compares the requested     |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2546:|                                   | format's entry only (8.1, 4.2).   |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2548:### 13.9 Revision r3 (round 2, `gpt-6-sol`)\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2554:| Item                              | Resolution                        |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2556:| Wire request example carried both | Agreed. Two mutually exclusive    |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2557:| name and identity; refusal table  | examples; both-or-neither is      |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2558:| lacked `:invalid-request`         | `:invalid-request`; reason added  |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2559:|                                   | to the table at step 0 (4.3, 6.3).|\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2560:| Module-local definitions flagged  | Agreed. `:definitions-fn` yields  |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2561:| as `:undeclared-free`             | the constant-key definition set,  |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2562:|                                   | subtracted before the join; also  |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2563:|                                   | gives an early `:export-missing`  |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2564:|                                   | (4.1, 4.2 step 5a, 5.x records).  |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2565:| VM-local counters collide on one  | Agreed. Link id is `[origin       |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2566:| response stream                   | counter]`; origin minted per task |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2567:|                                   | by the scheduler owning the pair; |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2568:|                                   | multiplexed schedulers need       |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2569:|                                   | distinct prefixes (7.2 step 3).   |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2570:| Exported closures carry child     | Agreed. `linked` is load, lift to |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2571:| code coordinates                  | UCF closure markers relative to   |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2572:|                                   | the image identity, lower against |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2573:|                                   | the parent's alias column or      |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2574:|                                   | offset (7.3). Divergence: export  |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2575:|                                   | relocation chosen over execution  |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2576:|                                   | in the owning child, which would  |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2577:|                                   | be a cross-VM function call.      |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2578:| `:fallback :verifying` could      | Agreed. `:derivation` policy is   |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2579:| accept an unverified claim        | `:verifying` or `:trusted`;       |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2580:|                                   | verifying fetches the tree and    |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2581:|                                   | re-lowers under the exact         |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2582:|                                   | per-format profile or refuses     |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2583:|                                   | `:unverified-derivation`; it      |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2584:|                                   | never downgrades (8.1, 6.2).      |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2585:| Bare `:asserted-by` honored       | Agreed. Proof required: a         |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2586:|                                   | signature over canonical bytes    |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2587:|                                   | verified by a composition-        |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2588:|                                   | supplied function, or an attested |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2589:|                                   | one-writer log bound to the       |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2590:|                                   | principal at attachment; unproven |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2591:|                                   | is `:unauthenticated`; retraction |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2592:|                                   | needs matching proof (8.2). Key   |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2593:|                                   | management stays open (12).       |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2594:| Host modules implied a sandbox    | Agreed. The host module is named  |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2595:|                                   | the trusted composition boundary; |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2596:|                                   | the profile check is a declaration|\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2597:|                                   | check; run-time confinement is    |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2598:|                                   | the capability-token discipline,  |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2599:|                                   | outside the linker (8.3).         |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2600:| Contract checked after row-local  | Agreed; r2 ordering defect. The   |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2601:| validation                        | contract check is step 0, before  |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2602:|                                   | any fetch or validator, and is    |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2603:|                                   | repeated against the manifest's   |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2604:|                                   | contract before any image fetch   |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2605:|                                   | (4.2 step 0, 8.1).                |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2607:### 13.10 Revision r2 (round 1, `gpt-5.6-sol`)\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2611:| Finding                          | Resolution                          |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2613:| P1 AST hash drops the tag        | Agreed. `:identity-fn` and          |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2614:|                                  | `:identity-matches-fn` take the     |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2615:|                                  | body as fetched; the id is          |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2616:|                                  | prepended only for step 4 (5.1).    |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2617:| P1 request carries functions     | Agreed. Closed plain-data request   |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2618:|                                  | key set; indexes, formats, name     |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2619:|                                  | environment in `link-state`;        |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2620:|                                  | `:invalid-request` (6.2, 6.3).      |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2621:|                                  | Divergence: receiver capabilities   |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2622:|                                  | are not sent either way; step 5b    |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2623:|                                  | runs at the receiver against live   |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2624:|                                  | state (4.2). A remote index is      |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2625:|                                  | observed into local state, not      |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2626:|                                  | queried inline (4.2 step 1).        |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2627:| P1 names, not profiles           | Agreed. Obligations joined from     |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2628:|                                  | manifest declarations; exact        |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2629:|                                  | profile / manifest equality;        |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2630:|                                  | `:undeclared-free` (4.2 step 5).    |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2631:| P1 wait entry incomplete         | Agreed. `:link-request` and         |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2632:|                                  | `:link-response`; cursor minted     |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2633:|                                  | before append; envelope and both    |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2634:|                                  | descriptors retained (7.2 steps 3-4)|\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2635:| P1 correlation unspecified       | Agreed. Exact id match; per-entry   |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2636:|                                  | kept cursor advancing past others;  |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2637:|                                  | duplicate, late, unknown, abandoned |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2638:|                                  | fixed (7.2 step 7). Divergence:     |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2639:|                                  | per-entry cells rather than one     |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2640:|                                  | router cursor, so a parked entry    |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2641:|                                  | stays a UCF cell and migrates.      |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2642:| P1 install cannot be synchronous | Agreed; r1's claim withdrawn.       |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2643:|                                  | Scheduler child task with phases,   |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2644:|                                  | own store and wait-set, waiter set, |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2645:|                                  | `:install` wait reason (7.3).       |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2646:| P1 four-way pairing unverified   | Agreed. `:yin.module/tree` plus one |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2647:|                                  | `derive-record` per format;         |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2648:|                                  | input/output checked; verifying or  |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2649:|                                  | trusted, named; swapped-image tests |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2650:|                                  | for all formats (8.1, M4).          |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2651:| P1 manifest name unchecked       | Agreed. `:module-name-mismatch`     |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2652:|                                  | after manifest validation (8.1).    |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2653:| P1 authority open at M4          | Agreed. Fail-closed assertion       |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2654:|                                  | policy with asserter, snapshot,     |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2655:|                                  | retraction, `:ambiguous-name`; M4   |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2656:|                                  | entry criterion (8.2, M4).          |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2657:|                                  | Authentication of principals stays  |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2658:|                                  | open and is named as such (12).     |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2659:| P2 decoupling not strict         | Agreed as stated: strict decoupling |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2660:|                                  | is H/R only, said plainly (3); the  |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2661:|                                  | pinning amendment is recorded as a  |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2662:|                                  | UCF / code-as-tuples decision (12). |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2663:| P2 unary segment-key at step 3   | Agreed. `segment-matches?` for the  |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2664:|                                  | storage-derived formats (4.1, 5).   |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2665:| P2 unbounded parts               | Agreed. `:row-defect-fn` before     |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2666:|                                  | enqueue; `:max-parts`, `:max-depth`,|\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2667:|                                  | `:max-bytes`; `:parts-limit` (4.2). |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2668:| P2 fetch's handle is a backdoor  | Agreed. `fetch` takes a link        |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2669:|                                  | runtime with a driver; no handle;   |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2670:|                                  | traffic-equivalence test (6.4, M3). |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2671:| P2 DHT is not a stream endpoint  | Agreed. DHT handle behind           |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2672:|                                  | `serve-content!`; equivalence       |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2673:|                                  | claimed at the linker boundary only;|\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2674:|                                  | stepped client out of scope (6.1).  |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2675:| P2 host modules unconstrained    | Agreed. Profile classes enforced at |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2676:|                                  | registration; `:host` refused;      |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2677:|                                  | effectful exports return effect     |\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2678:|                                  | data only (8.3).                    |\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n7:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_2\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '1,220p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"     1\\t## §7. The Universal Continuation Format (Proposed)\\n     2\\t\\n     3\\t> [!WARNING]  \\n     4\\t> **Status: Proposed / Deferred.** The Semantic VM's linear CESK state is theoretically sound as an architecture-agnostic continuation format, but true heterogeneous network migration requires addressing several critical defects identified in the 2026-09-14 architectural review. This revision (r3) incorporates r2's answers to that review's findings 1–19 and the subsequent owner ruling on §7.3's canonical form; §7.11 lists the acceptance blockers that keep this warning standing until an implementation phase closes each of them with tests.\\n     5\\t\\n     6\\tThe Semantic VM's linear CESK state (`{:segment id, :pc n, :env E, :stack S, :k K}`) is proposed as the canonical exchange format for network-transparent continuations. Because this lowered representation resolves execution-order ambiguity inherent in the Universal AST, it provides a simpler target for specialized execution engines (e.g., WebAssembly, LLVM, hardware FPGA) to participate in the `datom.world` ecosystem.\\n     7\\t\\n     8\\tTo safely \\\"lift on park\\\" and \\\"lower on resume\\\" across heterogeneous boundaries without violating host isolation or concurrency invariants, the following contracts must be fully specified before this feature is accepted:\\n     9\\t\\n    10\\t1. **Safepoint Reconstruction Metadata:** `:yin.code/source` mappings are insufficient for state reconstruction. The safepoint set must be exactly the machine's parking transitions — including effect-producing `:call`s — and reconstruction metadata must separate what is derivable from the canonical code alone (stack *effects*, lexical *requirements*) from what is per-activation state only the frame can carry (absolute depths, captured environments). Foreign engines publish per-segment safepoint maps derived from the canonical stream so arbitrary hardware states can be predictably lifted into the canonical CESK format.\\n    11\\t2. **Recursive Portable Encoding:** Section 1.1 permits host functions and local stream handles in the environment. A recursive encoding over a **disjoint tagged grammar** is required — one a program's own map literals cannot counterfeit — to encode these local references into portable descriptors, together with per-primitive **semantic profiles** (a name recovers a binding, not a meaning) and explicit, total failure modes for un-serializable resources. Cursor aliasing is observable and must survive the round trip.\\n    12\\t3. **Dependency Closure & Context:** A parked fragment is not a complete configuration. The format must carry its complete scheduler context — required primitives *with profiles*, loaded modules, referenced parked records, the fresh-name counter, and the reachable store slice — discovered by a conservative fixed point that includes explicit store operands, and must restore into an **isolated execution store** so the receiver's own state cannot change resolution.\\n    13\\t4. **Ownership Arbitration:** Emitting a continuation does not transfer ownership. Exclusion requires a **grounded authority** — a resource the grantor's boundary actually possesses, named in the value, independent of carriers — plus an **exporting state** that fences the source before publication, and **effect fencing** with authority epochs and stable operation ids, because absence of a lapse record is never evidence of tenure.\\n    14\\t5. **Code Identity (Content Addressing):** Because tempids (`:segment 123`) are local to a single machine's database, continuations must refer to code via immutable, versioned semantic profiles — a **canonical instruction vector** (positional tuples, not EAV) whose address is computed over the loader's resolved interpretation, whose addressed payload is the vector itself, and whose interpretation is pinned by a contract stamp versioning the *complete* execution contract.\\n    15\\t\\n    16\\t---\\n    17\\t\\n    18\\t# Universal Continuation Format — protocol draft\\n    19\\t\\n    20\\tStatus: Phase 5 design draft, 2026-09-14; revised 2026-09-14 (r2) per the\\n    21\\tarchitectural review `collab/1789387292000-architect-ucf-review.gpt-6-astra.findings.md`;\\n    22\\tamended 2026-09-14 (r3) by owner ruling — §7.3's canonical form is the\\n    23\\tpositional instruction tuple vector, not EAV (§7.3.2).\\n    24\\tSubordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md); builds on the Phase 0\\n    25\\tcontract in [`yin.vm.semantic.md`](./yin.vm.semantic.md) (§1–§4), the storage\\n    26\\tcontract in [`dao.jing.md`](./dao.jing.md), the stream contract in\\n    27\\t[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md), and the lease vocabulary in\\n    28\\t[`dao.lease.md`](./dao.lease.md). The five blockers above are answered in\\n    29\\t§7.3–§7.7, one section each, in dependency order: code identity first, because\\n    30\\tevery other part names code.\\n    31\\t\\n    32\\tEvery sentence in §7.2–§7.9 is a rule of the proposed protocol. §7.11 records\\n    33\\twhat is deliberately left open, as acceptance blockers. Nothing below is\\n    34\\timplemented; the warning at the top stands until an implementation phase\\n    35\\tresolves each blocker with tests.\\n    36\\t\\n    37\\t## §7.1 Stance: a continuation is a value, not a session\\n    38\\t\\n    39\\tThe semantic VM already parks as pure data (`yin.vm.semantic.md` §3.5): a\\n    40\\twait entry or a `:parked-continuation` record is `{segment pc env stack k}`\\n    41\\tplus a reason and resource ids, with no handle and no closure attached. That\\n    42\\trecord is *locally* complete and *globally* meaningless: its segment is a\\n    43\\ttempid, its stream ids are keys into this VM's store, its environment may hold\\n    44\\thost functions, and nothing says which primitives it assumed.\\n    45\\t\\n    46\\tThe Universal Continuation Format (UCF) is the **lift** of that local record\\n    47\\tinto a self-describing value that any conforming engine can **lower** back\\n    48\\tinto its own registers, and the discipline that governs who may lower it.\\n    49\\tThree roles participate, and they are stream roles, not parties to a call:\\n    50\\t\\n    51\\t| Role | Does | Holds |\\n    52\\t|---|---|---|\\n    53\\t| **Emitter** | lifts a parked configuration into a UCF value and appends it to a medium | nothing, once appended |\\n    54\\t| **Carrier** | any stream, file, DHT, or socket that moves the value; sees opaque data | nothing |\\n    55\\t| **Resumer** | reads the value, satisfies its declared context, obtains custody, lowers, runs | custody, for the duration of a lease |\\n    56\\t\\n    57\\tThree consequences are load-bearing. First, **a UCF value is content**: it has\\n    58\\ta content address under `dao.jing`, equal values converge on one address, and\\n    59\\tcarrying it twice is harmless (`dao.jing.md`, *Materialization rule*). Second,\\n    60\\t**a UCF value on a stream is not a claim**: a stream has no privileged reader\\n    61\\t(`datom.world.md`, *Streams*), so emission transfers nothing. Custody is\\n    62\\tarbitrated above the stream, by leases over datoms (§7.7), exactly where\\n    63\\t`dao.stream.md` *Explicitly Absent* says a take belongs. Third, **content is\\n    64\\tnot occurrence**: the same parked computation can be encoded more than once\\n    65\\t(sharing thresholds differ, §7.5.3), so the encodings address differently,\\n    66\\twhile remaining one *checkpoint occurrence*. Content identity is for\\n    67\\tdeduplication and materialization; occurrence identity is what custody names\\n    68\\t(§7.3.4, §7.7). Conflating them was a defect of the first draft; this draft\\n    69\\tnever does.\\n    70\\t\\n    71\\tThe reference engine for every rule below is `yin.vm.semantic`. Its\\n    72\\tconfiguration ⟨C, E, S, K⟩ with C = ⟨seg, pc, val, St⟩ (`yin.vm.semantic.md`\\n    73\\t§4.1) is the **canonical CESK**. A foreign engine conforms by lifting into and\\n    74\\tlowering from this configuration, never by extending it.\\n    75\\t\\n    76\\t## §7.2 Vocabulary and envelope\\n    77\\t\\n    78\\tUCF facts live under `:yin.k/*` (K for the continuation component of CESK).\\n    79\\tA UCF value is one open map dispatching on `:yin.k/type`; a consumer ignores\\n    80\\tqualified keys it does not understand, as for every DaoStream envelope. The\\n    81\\tvalue must survive the host codec structurally unchanged: no function, host\\n    82\\tobject, or live handle anywhere inside it (`dao.stream.md`, *Envelopes*).\\n    83\\tEvery value it contains is encoded in the disjoint tagged grammar of §7.5 —\\n    84\\tnever a bare marker map.\\n    85\\t\\n    86\\t```clojure\\n    87\\t{:yin.k/type        :yin.k/continuation\\n    88\\t :yin.k/contract    {:yin.code/contract \\\"v2\\\" :yin.k/version 0}   ; §7.3.3\\n    89\\t :yin.k/id          :segment/sha256-…          ; §7.3.4  content address of this map minus :yin.k/id\\n    90\\t :yin.k/occurrence  :yin.k/o-…                  ; §7.7    the checkpoint occurrence — the lease subject\\n    91\\t :yin.k/origin      {:yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/o-…                        ; predecessor, or absent on a first park\\n    92\\t                    :dao.lease/lease   :dao.lease/l-…                     ; the grant it ran under\\n    93\\t                    :yin.k/emitter     <attribution>}\\n    94\\t :yin.k/arbitration {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}     ; §7.7.3  the authority medium, named in the value\\n    95\\t :yin.k/frame       {…}                        ; §7.4  the canonical CESK at a safepoint\\n    96\\t :yin.k/values      {…}                        ; §7.5.3  shared subvalues, by content address\\n    97\\t :yin.k/cells       {…}                        ; §7.5.3  cursor cells: logical identity + position\\n    98\\t :yin.k/scheduler   {…}                        ; §7.6.3  fresh-name state and referenced parked records\\n    99\\t :yin.k/requires    {…}                        ; §7.6  dependency closure, with discovery status\\n   100\\t :yin.k/store       {…}                        ; §7.6.2  the store slice restored as an isolated store\\n   101\\t :yin.k/carried     [ … ]                      ; §7.3.4  canonical instruction vectors shipped inline\\n   102\\t :yin.k/policy      :yin.k/exclusive}          ; §7.7  or :yin.k/fork\\n   103\\t```\\n   104\\t\\n   105\\tThe parts answer four questions a resumer must be able to ask of the data\\n   106\\talone: *what code* (`:yin.k/requires`, `:yin.k/carried`, and every segment\\n   107\\taddress inside the frame), *what state* (`:yin.k/frame`, `:yin.k/values`,\\n   108\\t`:yin.k/cells`, `:yin.k/store`, `:yin.k/scheduler`), *what world*\\n   109\\t(`:yin.k/requires`, `:yin.k/arbitration`), and *who may run it*\\n   110\\t(`:yin.k/policy`, `:yin.k/occurrence`, with the custody facts of §7.7 on\\n   111\\ttheir own medium).\\n   112\\t\\n   113\\tA halted computation travels as the sibling value:\\n   114\\t\\n   115\\t```clojure\\n   116\\t{:yin.k/type      :yin.k/result\\n   117\\t :yin.k/contract  {…}\\n   118\\t :yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/o-…     ; the occurrence that halted\\n   119\\t :yin.k/value     <encoded>}      ; the final accumulator, in the §7.5 grammar\\n   120\\t```\\n   121\\t\\n   122\\tBoth envelope kinds take their identity by the same rule: `:yin.k/id` is the\\n   123\\t`dao.jing` segment address of the map with `:yin.k/id` removed (§7.3.4).\\n   124\\t\\n   125\\tOutcomes of every UCF operation are data: one map dispatching on\\n   126\\t`:yin.k/status`, in the DaoStream result convention. Where an underlying\\n   127\\tstream outcome is involved, it is preserved unchanged under its own dispatch\\n   128\\tkey `:dao.stream/outcome` — UCF never renames or nests it under a private\\n   129\\tkey. The full algebra is in §7.9.\\n   130\\t\\n   131\\t## §7.3 Code identity — content-addressed semantic profiles (blocker 5)\\n   132\\t\\n   133\\t### 7.3.1 The problem restated as data\\n   134\\t\\n   135\\t`:segment 123` is an entity id minted by one transactor; another machine's\\n   136\\t`123` is different code or no code. A continuation naming code by tempid is\\n   137\\ttherefore not a value, it is a pointer. UCF names code by **what it is**.\\n   138\\t\\n   139\\t### 7.3.2 The canonical instruction vector and its address\\n   140\\t\\n   141\\tThe segment attribute `:yin.code/hash`, reserved in `yin.vm.semantic.md`\\n   142\\t§2.2, becomes required on any segment a continuation may reference. Its\\n   143\\tvalue is the `dao.jing` segment address of the segment's **canonical\\n   144\\tinstruction vector**. The canonical form has had two predecessors, each\\n   145\\tnamed so no later plan reinvents it: the first draft's sorted `[e a v]`\\n   146\\ttriples, which were neither total nor execution-faithful; and the r2\\n   147\\trevision's attributed-map record (`{pc → {attr → value}}`), which was sound\\n   148\\tbut carried datom vocabulary into the identity layer — attribute names and\\n   149\\tadmissibility rules where position and the opcode table suffice. The r2\\n   150\\tform is superseded by two owner rulings: tuples are the correct way to\\n   151\\trepresent a semantic AST, though the datom `[e a v t m]` need not be the\\n   152\\ttuple shape when content-hashing code; and `dao.space.query` assumes no\\n   153\\tEAV — its `relation` is an arbitrary mixed-dimensional tuple collection\\n   154\\t(`src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc:153-158`) — so Datalog-over-code does not\\n   155\\trequire `[e a v t m]` and UCF must not assume it anywhere for code. The\\n   156\\tcanonical form is therefore the **positional instruction tuple vector**:\\n   157\\tone tuple per instruction, pc is the index, and the §2.4 opcode table fixes\\n   158\\teach mnemonic's tuple arity and operand kinds. Nothing else names a slot.\\n   159\\t\\n   160\\t**Input.** The vector is computed from a well-formed batch (§2.6) by first\\n   161\\tapplying the loader's *resolved interpretation* — the same\\n   162\\t`index-batch` collapse the loader and the validator perform, where a repeated\\n   163\\tsingle-valued attribute keeps its **last** value. This is the load-bearing\\n   164\\tchoice. Two batches whose constants arrive as `1, 2` versus `2, 1` resolve to\\n   165\\tindexed values `2` and `1`; they *execute differently*, and over the resolved\\n   166\\tinterpretation they canonicalize differently and address differently. Over\\n   167\\traw triples they sorted identically — the collision the review demonstrated.\\n   168\\tCanonicalization and execution now read the same value by construction; there\\n   169\\tis nothing left to collide. The last-value-wins rule itself is part of the\\n   170\\tcontract stamp (§7.3.3) and is versioned with it.\\n   171\\t\\n   172\\t**The vector.** With the resolution applied, the canonical instruction\\n   173\\tvector of a segment is (the §2.7 worked segment, excerpted):\\n   174\\t\\n   175\\t```clojure\\n   176\\t[[:closure [x] 6]           ; mnemonic, params, resolved body pc\\n   177\\t [:push]\\n   178\\t [:const 10]                ; §2.5 literal: data only\\n   179\\t [:call 1 false]            ; argc, tail?\\n   180\\t …]\\n   181\\t```\\n   182\\t\\n   183\\tRules, each replacing heavier machinery from the attributed-map record:\\n   184\\t\\n   185\\t- **pc is the index.** No entity ids and no attribute names appear anywhere\\n   186\\t  in the form: position fixes meaning, the §2.4 table fixes shape, and a\\n   187\\t  query over code is a query over tuples of the vector, not over EAV.\\n   188\\t- **Saturation.** Defaults the loader applies are materialized in the tuple —\\n   189\\t  `:gensym` absent prefix becomes `\\\"id\\\"`, `:ffi-call` absent argc becomes\\n   190\\t  `0`, `:stream-make` absent buffer becomes the default capacity — so a batch\\n   191\\t  that omitted a defaulted operand and one that stated it canonicalize\\n   192\\t  identically, because they execute identically.\\n   193\\t- **Refs are resolved pcs**, exactly as the loader resolves them.\\n   194\\t- **The header folds away.** `:yin.code/length` is `(count vector)`,\\n   195\\t  `:yin.code/type :segment` is implied by the form. Provenance\\n   196\\t  (`:yin.code/derived-from`, every `:yin.code/source`), entity ids, batch\\n   197\\t  order, `t`, `m`, and `:yin.code/hash` itself are excluded — the address is\\n   198\\t  computed *over* the vector, which therefore cannot contain it without\\n   199\\t  hashing the hash being verified.\\n   200\\t- **Admissible slots.** Each tuple is its mnemonic plus exactly the operands\\n   201\\t  the §2.4 table gives it, in the table's order. A batch that resolves to\\n   202\\t  anything else — a mnemonic outside the table, an operand the mnemonic's\\n   203\\t  tuple has no slot for, a segment-entity attribute beyond the folded and\\n   204\\t  excluded set — is **non-canonicalizable**, and canonicalization refuses\\n   205\\t  naming the instruction (the lifted value is never minted; the refusal is\\n   206\\t  `:yin.k/non-portable` with `:yin.k/kind :non-canonicalizable`).\\n   207\\t- **Ordering: none is imposed.** The vector's order is its positions;\\n   208\\t  canonicalization sorts nothing and compares no operand against another.\\n   209\\t  Literal operands are values and hash under the same encoder as everything\\n   210\\t  else, so equal literals address equally however their maps were built.\\n   211\\t  The first draft's `[:segment …]`-versus-`[0 …]` triple sort — which throws\\n   212\\t  `Keyword cannot be cast to Number` in the very host it must run on — has\\n   213\\t  no successor here because there is no sort at all.\\n   214\\t\\n   215\\t**The address** is `(dao.jing/segment-key vector)` — a\\n   216\\t`:segment/sha256-<hex>` keyword. The vector *is* the resolved\\n   217\\tinterpretation, so r2's collision-freedom argument carries over unchanged\\n   218\\tand needs only restating: canonicalization and execution read the same\\n   219\\tvalue by construction; there is nothing left to collide. The address fixes\\n   220\\tthe resolved instruction stream — mnemonics, operands, control graph,\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n9:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '621,820p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"   621\\t```\\n   622\\t\\n   623\\tThe profile record — `{name class arities effects host-state}` under a\\n   624\\tcontent address — is what a name must be *checked against*, not merely\\n   625\\tpresent as. Rules:\\n   626\\t\\n   627\\t- **Ambiguity refuses the lift.** A function object that reverse-lookups to\\n   628\\t  more than one name in the emitter's `:primitives` is refused\\n   629\\t  (`:yin.k/non-portable`, kind `:ambiguous-primitive`) unless the\\n   630\\t  composition declares a canonical-name table resolving it to exactly one.\\n   631\\t- **A host function under no name is refused** (kind `:unnamed-function`) —\\n   632\\t  a bridge handler's return value, a module-constructed function: it has no\\n   633\\t  declared semantics to travel under.\\n   634\\t- **`:host`-class functions are refused** (kind `:host-state-primitive`). A\\n   635\\t  function whose behavior depends on undeclared host state is not a value\\n   636\\t  any other host can honor; profiles that declare host state may not be\\n   637\\t  lifted, and a profile claiming `:host-state :none` is a publication's\\n   638\\t  warranty, versioned with the contract stamp's primitive-profile registry.\\n   639\\t- **On lowering, the resumer's binding is checked by profile, not by\\n   640\\t  presence.** For each required name, the resumer must hold a primitive\\n   641\\t  whose profile address is equal; a same-named function of different profile\\n   642\\t  is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the symbol and the expected profile — it is\\n   643\\t  a routing decision for whoever chose this resumer, not a silent\\n   644\\t  substitution.\\n   645\\t- The standard map `yin.vm/primitives` must be published with profiles\\n   646\\t  (note `yin/def` is `:effectful` with effect `:vm/store-put`, and `require`\\n   647\\t  is `:effectful` with `:module/require`); that publication is an acceptance\\n   648\\t  blocker (§7.11).\\n   649\\t\\n   650\\t### 7.5.3 Sharing, cells, and cycles\\n   651\\t\\n   652\\t**The value table.** A configuration is a DAG, not a tree: a closure's\\n   653\\tcaptured environment is the frame's environment; a value on the stack is\\n   654\\talso bound in `E`. Encoding naively duplicates, and a deep K makes\\n   655\\tduplication quadratic. The encoder therefore builds a **value table**:\\n   656\\t\\n   657\\t- On encountering a collection, closure, or continuation it has already\\n   658\\t  encoded (host identity during one lift), it emits\\n   659\\t  `{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/ref :yin.k/id addr}` where `addr` is the `dao.jing`\\n   660\\t  address of the encoded subvalue, and places the subvalue in\\n   661\\t  `:yin.k/values {addr → encoded}`.\\n   662\\t- A subvalue is placed in the table when it is referenced more than once or\\n   663\\t  its encoded size exceeds a composition-supplied threshold; otherwise it\\n   664\\t  stays inline. Both choices yield equal semantics; only the table is needed\\n   665\\t  for correctness of sharing.\\n   666\\t- A cycle (a value reachable from itself through host identity) is refused\\n   667\\t  as `:yin.k/non-portable` with `:yin.k/kind :cyclic` naming the path. The\\n   668\\t  reference machine cannot produce one — `E` is extended by `merge`, never\\n   669\\t  mutated — so the refusal guards against foreign engines and bridge-returned\\n   670\\t  host structures. Cycles *through ref data* cannot be minted at all and are\\n   671\\t  rejected at decode (§7.5.1); this single representation retires the first\\n   672\\t  draft's two competing ones.\\n   673\\t\\n   674\\tBecause table entries are content-addressed, a carrier that already holds an\\n   675\\tentry (a `dao.jing` intake pool, a DHT replica) need not carry it again, and\\n   676\\ttwo continuations parked from the same closure share its encoding. This is\\n   677\\t`streams-all-the-way-down.md` §6.3's checkpoint-as-published-fold applied to\\n   678\\ta single frame. It is also why §7.3.4 separates `:yin.k/id` (which varies\\n   679\\twith these choices, harmlessly) from `:yin.k/occurrence` (which does not).\\n   680\\t\\n   681\\t**Cursor cells.** A cursor is *observable state with aliasing*: two distinct\\n   682\\tcursor refs may sit at the same position and yet be independently\\n   683\\tadvanceable store entries, while repeated refs to one cursor ref must\\n   684\\tadvance together — `check-wait-set` advances a shared cursor ref before\\n   685\\tpolling the next waiter, so waiters on one ref read successive values, and\\n   686\\twaiters on two refs at the same position read the same next value\\n   687\\t(`yin.vm.engine`, `check-wait-set`). A position-only encoding erases\\n   688\\texactly this distinction: two cells at one position would collapse, and\\n   689\\ttheir independent advancement would be lost. The encoding therefore carries\\n   690\\t**logical cursor cells** separately from position content:\\n   691\\t\\n   692\\t```clojure\\n   693\\t:yin.k/cells {:yin.k/c-17 {:yin.k/stream   {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/stream …}\\n   694\\t                           :yin.k/position <opaque, exactly as the transport minted it>}}\\n   695\\t;; values reference cells, never positions:\\n   696\\t{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/cursor-ref :yin.k/cell :yin.k/c-17}\\n   697\\t```\\n   698\\t\\n   699\\tCell ids are minted by the emitter (stable within one lift; remapped on\\n   700\\tlowering) and are *not* content-addressed — a cell is a local identity, like\\n   701\\ta tempid, and deduplicating cells by their contents is precisely the aliasing\\n   702\\terasure this structure exists to prevent. On lowering, each cell becomes a\\n   703\\tfresh store cursor entry seeded with its carried position; every\\n   704\\t`:yin.k/cursor-ref` to it remaps to that entry; two refs to one cell share\\n   705\\tone entry and one ref per cell keeps its own. The FFI response cell of\\n   706\\t§7.4.3 is a cell like any other.\\n   707\\t\\n   708\\tA stream reference is portable iff its transport produces a descriptor whose\\n   709\\t`attach!` can succeed somewhere other than the emitter's host. The encoder\\n   710\\tcannot know that; it encodes the descriptor and lets the resumer's `attach!`\\n   711\\treport `not-found` as data. What the encoder *can* refuse is a handle whose\\n   712\\t`descriptor` call fails, which cannot happen under the contract, and a raw\\n   713\\tin-memory handle admitted by an in-process stream's admission declaration\\n   714\\t(`dao.stream.md`, *Creation and Attachment*), which it treats as a host\\n   715\\tobject.\\n   716\\t\\n   717\\t### 7.5.4 Failure is total and names its place\\n   718\\t\\n   719\\tEncoding a continuation with one refused leaf refuses the whole\\n   720\\tcontinuation:\\n   721\\t\\n   722\\t```clojure\\n   723\\t{:yin.k/status :yin.k/non-portable\\n   724\\t :yin.k/path   [:yin.k/env 'conn]         ; where in the frame\\n   725\\t :yin.k/kind   :host-object               ; the closed kind set below\\n   726\\t :yin.k/hint   \\\"java.net.Socket\\\"}         ; classified, never the object\\n   727\\t```\\n   728\\t\\n   729\\tThe kind set is closed:\\n   730\\t`:host-object | :unnamed-function | :ambiguous-primitive | :host-state-primitive\\n   731\\t| :cyclic | :in-memory-handle | :non-canonicalizable | :foreign-parked-ref`\\n   732\\t(the last two from §7.3.2 and §7.6.3). There is no partial UCF value and no\\n   733\\tplaceholder for a missing leaf. A program that wishes to be migratable keeps\\n   734\\thost resources behind stream descriptors and named, profiled primitives,\\n   735\\twhich is the same discipline the host boundary rules already impose on\\n   736\\tportable code (`datom.world.md`, *Host Boundaries*). The refusal is the\\n   737\\temitter's outcome, returned as data to whatever asked it to lift; the\\n   738\\tmachine itself is unaffected and remains parked locally.\\n   739\\t\\n   740\\t## §7.6 Dependency closure and context (blocker 3)\\n   741\\t\\n   742\\t### 7.6.1 Declared, not hand-written — and discovered conservatively\\n   743\\t\\n   744\\tA resumer must be able to decide *before lowering* whether it can run the\\n   745\\tvalue. `:yin.k/requires` carries that decision's inputs, and every set in it\\n   746\\tis **computed by a conservative fixed point**, not hand-written and not\\n   747\\tguessed from the current environment:\\n   748\\t\\n   749\\t```clojure\\n   750\\t:yin.k/requires\\n   751\\t{:yin.k/segments        #{:segment/sha256-3f1a… :segment/sha256-9c02…}  ; every address in frame, k, closures, values, parked\\n   752\\t :yin.k/primitives      {sym → profile, §7.5.2}                        ; every {:yin.k/primitive …} encoded, plus every free :var\\n   753\\t :yin.k/modules         {my.mod :segment/sha256-…}                     ; module name → manifest address\\n   754\\t :yin.k/effects         #{:stream/next :stream/put :module/require}    ; effect kinds the segments can raise\\n   755\\t :yin.k/ffi-ops         #{:op/add}                                     ; :yin.code/ffi-op values present\\n   756\\t :yin.k/streams         #{<identity> …}                                ; logical-stream identities referenced anywhere\\n   757\\t :yin.k/cursor-profiles #{:dao.stream/file-v1}                         ; transports that must accept kept cursors, §7.4.3\\n   758\\t :yin.k/discovery       :complete}                                     ; :complete | :incomplete | :blocked — computed, §7.6.5\\n   759\\t```\\n   760\\t\\n   761\\tThe fixed point starts from the frame, its K frames, the parked records of\\n   762\\t§7.6.3, the store slice, and the pending wait. Its unit of analysis is a work\\n   763\\titem `[code-address context]`, not an address: `context` is the finite\\n   764\\tabstraction of the captured environment with which a closure enters that\\n   765\\tcode. Code is fetched, validated, and projected once per address, but every\\n   766\\tnewly discovered address-context pair is analyzed. Two closures over the\\n   767\\tsame address with different captured environments are therefore distinct\\n   768\\twork items. A full pass repeats whenever it adds a work item or any dependency\\n   769\\tfact, and convergence is reached only when a full pass adds neither:\\n   770\\t\\n   771\\t- **Values:** every encoded closure contributes its segment address and its\\n   772\\t  captured env's values and the work item\\n   773\\t  `[segment-address captured-env-context]`; every stream and cursor marker\\n   774\\t  contributes a stream identity; every `:yin.k/primitive` marker contributes\\n   775\\t  a name. A captured environment contributes reachable values, never name\\n   776\\t  discharge.\\n   777\\t- **Code:** every reachable segment's instructions are walked — as datoms\\n   778\\t  where the emitter holds the batch, as canonical tuples where only a vector\\n   779\\t  was fetched (§7.3.4) — including **`:store-get` and `:store-put`\\n   780\\t  operands**, which name store\\n   781\\t  keys directly and which the first draft missed entirely; every `:var`\\n   782\\t  name; every effect op (`:stream-*`, `:ffi-call`, and the effects of\\n   783\\t  `:effectful`-class primitives the walk finds); every `:resume` operand\\n   784\\t  (a parked id, §7.6.3). Discovering a new work item continues the fixed point\\n   785\\t  with that address and context even when the address was already walked for\\n   786\\t  another context.\\n   787\\t- **Names:** only a free `:var` name is an obligation. An obligation is\\n   788\\t  discharged by a matching key in the reachable store slice, or retained as\\n   789\\t  a required primitive or module export and checked by profile (§7.5.2). An\\n   790\\t  environment never discharges a name: captured environments contribute\\n   791\\t  values to their work item's reachability analysis, while lexical binding\\n   792\\t  determines whether a `:var` is free before it becomes an obligation. If no\\n   793\\t  store key or profiled primitive/module requirement can discharge an\\n   794\\t  obligation, discovery is `:incomplete`; after lowering, an unavailable\\n   795\\t  retained requirement is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` (§7.6.5).\\n   796\\t- **Modules:** a module's manifest declares its exported primitive profiles\\n   797\\t  and its **store footprint** (store keys and effect kinds its handlers may\\n   798\\t  touch). Until manifests carry footprints, any required module whose\\n   799\\t  footprint is undeclared sets `:yin.k/discovery :incomplete` — the honest\\n   800\\t  statement that closure could not be computed, never a silent\\n   801\\t  under-approximation. A module effect handler may read store keys the\\n   802\\t  static walk cannot see; a composition may supply extra slice keys\\n   803\\t  explicitly, and the value records that it did.\\n   804\\t\\n   805\\tBecause the computation is over data, the same query answers \\\"what does this\\n   806\\tprogram need?\\\" for a segment that has never run. And because discovery can\\n   807\\t*fail to terminate successfully* — a missing segment cannot be walked, an\\n   808\\tundeclared footprint cannot be closed — the result distinguishes\\n   809\\t`:complete` from `:incomplete` from `:blocked` (§7.6.5); a fixed point that\\n   810\\tcould not finish is reported as such, not passed off as a satisfied closure.\\n   811\\t\\n   812\\t### 7.6.2 The store slice, restored as an isolated store\\n   813\\t\\n   814\\tThe store S holds `def` results, stream handles, cursor entries, and the FFI\\n   815\\tpair (§4.1). None of it is global in the UCF sense; it is the emitter's. A\\n   816\\tcontinuation carries the **reachable slice**: every store key the fixed\\n   817\\tpoint of §7.6.1 can name — every free `:var` obligation for which the\\n   818\\temitter's store has a matching key, every `:store-get`/`:store-put` operand,\\n   819\\tevery stream/cursor cell — with values encoded by §7.5:\\n   820\\t\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n11:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '421,620p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"   421\\ta foreign engine lowers the canonical frame directly.\\n   422\\t\\n   423\\tWhat *is* a function of the canonical vector alone, and what such an engine\\n   424\\tmust publish — **per segment and per engine profile**, derived from the\\n   425\\tcanonical stream at its own load time — is the static half:\\n   426\\t\\n   427\\t```clojure\\n   428\\t[[sp :yin.safepoint/segment      :segment/sha256-3f1a…]  ; the code, by address\\n   429\\t [sp :yin.safepoint/engine       :wasm32/v1]             ; the engine profile that produced this layout\\n   430\\t [sp :yin.safepoint/pc           5]                      ; canonical resume pc\\n   431\\t [sp :yin.safepoint/stack-effect -1]                     ; Δ|St| across the parking instruction (static)\\n   432\\t [sp :yin.safepoint/lexically-required [x conn]]         ; names this pc can read from E (static)\\n   433\\t [sp :yin.safepoint/layout       {…}]]                   ; engine-owned: physical slot → canonical slot\\n   434\\t```\\n   435\\t\\n   436\\tRules:\\n   437\\t\\n   438\\t- `stack-effect` and `lexically-required` are **derived from the canonical\\n   439\\t  vector alone**, by the same static walk the linearizer performs (§5.3).\\n   440\\t  They are identical for every engine and checkable against the reference\\n   441\\t  machine by a conformance test that runs the segment to each safepoint and\\n   442\\t  compares the observed deltas and E-reads. Absolute depth, activation\\n   443\\t  bases, captured environments, and physical layouts are **not** claimed,\\n   444\\t  not derived, and not in this table; they are the frame's business.\\n   445\\t- `:yin.safepoint/layout` is opaque to the protocol. It is a memo the engine\\n   446\\t  keeps so that its `lift` is a table lookup. It never travels in a UCF\\n   447\\t  value; a resumer that is a different engine has its own.\\n   448\\t- `lift(engine-state, layout) → canonical frame` and\\n   449\\t  `lower(canonical frame, layout) → engine-state` are total on safepoints and\\n   450\\t  undefined elsewhere. The conformance obligation is `lift ∘ lower = id` on\\n   451\\t  canonical frames, tested against the reference machine's own frames —\\n   452\\t  which is possible precisely because the dynamic half rides in the frame.\\n   453\\t- A request to lift at a pc that is not a safepoint of the segment returns\\n   454\\t  `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` naming the pc. An engine that wants finer\\n   455\\t  interruption granularity must lower the program to a segment that\\n   456\\t  *contains* a `:park` where it wants one: that is a different instruction\\n   457\\t  record, a different address, and a different profile. An engine may not\\n   458\\t  invent safepoints a record does not have, and two engines that disagree\\n   459\\t  about the safepoint set of one address are not both conforming.\\n   460\\t\\n   461\\tSafepoints are datoms, so a query can ask \\\"where can this program be\\n   462\\tmigrated?\\\" without running it, and a debugger's breakpoint set is a subset of\\n   463\\tthe safepoint set by construction.\\n   464\\t\\n   465\\t### 7.4.3 The pending wait travels as data — complete, and resumable elsewhere\\n   466\\t\\n   467\\tA parked machine was waiting for something. That something is a resource of\\n   468\\tthe emitter's host, and the resumer cannot poll it. `:yin.k/pending` records\\n   469\\tthe wait in resource-independent terms so the resumer can re-establish an\\n   470\\t*equivalent* wait, or decide it cannot. The first draft carried sketches;\\n   471\\tthe set here is exhaustive over §7.4.1's reasons, and each variant carries\\n   472\\teverything a *foreign* resumer needs — including the state the first draft\\n   473\\tleft implicit on the emitter's heap:\\n   474\\t\\n   475\\t```clojure\\n   476\\t;; blocked read (:stream-next)\\n   477\\t{:yin.k/reason   :next\\n   478\\t :yin.k/cell     :yin.k/c-17}          ; the cursor CELL id, §7.5.3 — not a bare position\\n   479\\t\\n   480\\t;; blocked write (:stream-put, or an effectful call's :stream/put)\\n   481\\t{:yin.k/reason   :put\\n   482\\t :yin.k/stream   {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}   ; the target\\n   483\\t :yin.k/value    <encoded>}            ; the retained value — a retry appends THIS, and\\n   484\\t                                       ; without it the wait is not re-establishable\\n   485\\t\\n   486\\t;; FFI call, sent (:ffi-call → ok)\\n   487\\t{:yin.k/reason     :ffi\\n   488\\t :yin.k/call-id    :call-7             ; the :dao.stream.apply/id correlation\\n   489\\t :yin.k/op         :op/add\\n   490\\t :yin.k/request    {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}   ; where the request went\\n   491\\t :yin.k/response   {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}   ; where the answer lands\\n   492\\t :yin.k/cell       :yin.k/c-23         ; the response cursor cell, §7.5.3, at its KEPT position}\\n   493\\t\\n   494\\t;; FFI call, retained (:ffi-call → full): the request is still in hand\\n   495\\t{:yin.k/reason     :ffi-request\\n   496\\t :yin.k/call-id    :call-7\\n   497\\t :yin.k/request-op :op/add\\n   498\\t :yin.k/request-args [<encoded> …]     ; enough to rebuild the envelope verbatim\\n   499\\t :yin.k/request    {identity/descriptor as above}\\n   500\\t :yin.k/response   {identity/descriptor as above}\\n   501\\t :yin.k/cell       :yin.k/c-23}        ; response cell at its kept position\\n   502\\t```\\n   503\\t\\n   504\\tThree rules make these resumable somewhere other than where they were minted:\\n   505\\t\\n   506\\t- **The response position is a kept cursor, never an anchor.** Reattaching\\n   507\\t  at `:oldest` replays responses that belong to other calls; attaching at\\n   508\\t  `:newest` skips past this call's own. The response cell therefore travels\\n   509\\t  at its *kept* position, and the whole task's waiters on that cell travel\\n   510\\t  with it (§7.6.3), so the round-order semantics of `check-wait-set` —\\n   511\\t  waiters sharing a cell consume successive values — are preserved on the\\n   512\\t  resumer exactly. A first poll that observes `gap` is the honest outcome\\n   513\\t  the contract already promises a kept cursor (`dao.stream.md`, *Retention\\n   514\\t  and Gaps*). This requires the response transport to accept a kept cursor\\n   515\\t  from another host, which `dao.stream.md` leaves transport-owned and TBD;\\n   516\\t  UCF therefore declares, per stream, a **portable cursor profile** in\\n   517\\t  `:yin.k/requires` (`:yin.k/cursor-profiles`), and a transport that does\\n   518\\t  not declare one cannot be a pending-response endpoint — the lift refuses\\n   519\\t  it as `:yin.k/unsatisfied` before the value is ever minted.\\n   520\\t- **Outstanding calls route to the emitter's pair; the resumer's pair is its\\n   521\\t  own.** The reference machine restores a `:request-sent` entry into a\\n   522\\t  response wait through its fixed local store keys (`vm/call-out-stream-key`\\n   523\\t  and friends). A resumer must *not* do that: the outstanding call's\\n   524\\t  response will arrive on the **emitter's** response stream, because that is\\n   525\\t  where the request was sent. The resumer re-establishes the wait against\\n   526\\t  the carried response descriptor and the cell's fresh local key (the cell\\n   527\\t  id remapped per §7.5.3), while calls the resumed program makes *after*\\n   528\\t  lowering use the resumer's own pair under its own keys. Pending-call\\n   529\\t  routing and future-call routing are separate stores of state; conflating\\n   530\\t  them strands the migrated call exactly as the review described.\\n   531\\t- **On resume, the resumer attaches and re-mints.** Attach to every\\n   532\\t  descriptor in the frame, pending, and cells; an attachment resolving to\\n   533\\t  `not-found` is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream identity, never a\\n   534\\t  silent `nil`; a `:put` wait retries its retained value and resumes with it\\n   535\\t  on `ok` (the engine, a woken writer's retry appends and stamps the\\n   536\\t  value); a `:next` wait polls its cell. No wait is silently dropped and no\\n   537\\t  retained value is recomputed — what parks is what resumes.\\n   538\\t\\n   539\\t## §7.5 Recursive portable encoding (blocker 2)\\n   540\\t\\n   541\\t### 7.5.1 A disjoint tagged grammar\\n   542\\t\\n   543\\tEncoding is one total function `encode : value × path → outcome`, applied\\n   544\\trecursively to every value reachable from the frame: accumulator, operand\\n   545\\tstack, environment values, every frame in K, parked records, and the store\\n   546\\tslice of §7.6.2. The outcome is either the portable form or a refusal naming\\n   547\\tthe path.\\n   548\\t\\n   549\\tThe first draft encoded markers as ordinary maps — `{:yin.k/primitive '+}`,\\n   550\\t`{:yin.k/ref addr}` — while passing program map literals through in their\\n   551\\tnatural shape. That is ambiguous to everyone: §2.5 admits map literals,\\n   552\\tso a program can *contain* `{:yin.k/primitive '+}`, and a decoder cannot tell\\n   553\\tan encoder's marker from a program's data. The grammar here is disjoint by\\n   554\\tconstruction: **every map at an encoded-value position is a UCF marker**, and\\n   555\\ta program's own maps are always wrapped.\\n   556\\t\\n   557\\t| Encoded form | Tag | Contents | Decodes to |\\n   558\\t|---|---|---|---|\\n   559\\t| scalars: nil, boolean, number, string, keyword, symbol | — | themselves (never a map; cannot forge a marker) | themselves |\\n   560\\t| vector, list, set | — | same shape, elements recursively encoded (never a map) | same shape, elements decoded |\\n   561\\t| literal map | `:yin.k/literal` | `:yin.k/entries [k₁ v₁ k₂ v₂ …]`, keys and values recursively encoded | the map, keys and values decoded |\\n   562\\t| closure | `:yin.k/closure` | `:yin.k/segment addr :yin.k/entry pc :yin.k/params […] :yin.k/env {encoded}` | `{:type :closure …}` (§2.4) |\\n   563\\t| reified continuation | `:yin.k/frame` | a nested frame (§7.4.1), recursively | `{:type :reified-continuation …}` |\\n   564\\t| parked continuation | `:yin.k/frame` | a nested frame plus `:yin.k/parked-id` | `{:type :parked-continuation …}` |\\n   565\\t| primitive | `:yin.k/primitive` | `:yin.k/name sym` | the named function, after profile check (§7.5.2) |\\n   566\\t| stream reference | `:yin.k/stream` | `:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …` | an attached handle, under a fresh store key |\\n   567\\t| cursor reference | `:yin.k/cursor-ref` | `:yin.k/cell cell-id` | a store cursor entry for that cell, §7.5.3 |\\n   568\\t| shared subvalue | `:yin.k/ref` | `:yin.k/id addr` | the table entry at `addr`, §7.5.3 |\\n   569\\t| anything else | — | — | **refused**: `:yin.k/non-portable` |\\n   570\\t\\n   571\\tWhy the wrapping is total rather than an \\\"escaping rule\\\" for suspicious\\n   572\\tshapes: a literal map is wrapped *wherever it occurs and whatever it\\n   573\\tcontains* — including one that happens to read `{:yin.k/tag …}` — and the\\n   574\\tentries inside a wrapper are themselves encoded, so a forged marker inside a\\n   575\\tliteral is re-wrapped one level down and peeled back exactly one level on\\n   576\\tdecode. No rule asks whether a map \\\"looks like\\\" data; no decoder decision\\n   577\\tdepends on program content. Scalar and collection values pass through only\\n   578\\tbecause no marker is ever a non-map, which the closed tag table fixes.\\n   579\\t\\n   580\\tMap keys are values and are encoded as such — inside `:yin.k/entries`\\n   581\\talternately with their values, and nowhere else; the frame's own `:yin.k/env`\\n   582\\tkeys are the program's symbols and are structural, not encoded values.\\n   583\\t\\n   584\\t**Decoding validates, independent of hashing.** A hash check confirms what\\n   585\\twas encoded; it does not confirm the encoding is well formed. The decoder\\n   586\\twalks the grammar and rejects, as `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the path: a map\\n   587\\twith no `:yin.k/tag` at an encoded-value position (impossible from a\\n   588\\tconforming encoder); a tag outside the closed set; a marker missing its\\n   589\\trequired keys; a `:yin.k/ref` whose address is absent from the value table; a\\n   590\\tref graph that is cyclic *through ref data* (content-addressed table entries\\n   591\\tcannot mint such a cycle — a cycle can only be written by hand, and it is\\n   592\\trejected); and a value table entry not referenced by anything reachable.\\n   593\\tMalformed-but-correctly-hashed input fails here, before any lowering.\\n   594\\t\\n   595\\t### 7.5.2 Primitives: a name is a binding, not a meaning\\n   596\\t\\n   597\\t§1.1 admits host functions into the environment because `:var` resolves a\\n   598\\tprimitive symbol to the function value and a program may then bind it\\n   599\\t(`(def f +)`). At lift time the emitter recognizes such a value by reverse\\n   600\\tlookup in its own `:primitives` map — identity on the function object. The\\n   601\\tfirst draft treated the recovered symbol as the whole story. It is not:\\n   602\\t\\n   603\\t- one function object may sit under **several names**, making reverse lookup\\n   604\\t  ambiguous;\\n   605\\t- two composition-supplied primitive maps may bind the **same symbol to\\n   606\\t  different functions** — name presence at the resumer proves nothing about\\n   607\\t  behavior;\\n   608\\t- identity stability says nothing about **purity or captured host state**.\\n   609\\t\\n   610\\tUCF therefore binds every portable function to a **primitive profile**,\\n   611\\tpublished data, and the binding is checked on both ends:\\n   612\\t\\n   613\\t```clojure\\n   614\\t;; in :yin.k/requires — computed, not hand-written (§7.6.1)\\n   615\\t:yin.k/primitives {+\\n   616\\t                   {:yin.k/profile :yin.k.pp/sha256-…   ; content address of the profile record\\n   617\\t                    :yin.k/class    :pure               ; :pure | :effectful | :host\\n   618\\t                    :yin.k/arities  [2]\\n   619\\t                    :yin.k/effects  #{}                 ; for :effectful — declared effect kinds\\n   620\\t                    :yin.k/host-state :none}}           ; :none, or refused\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n13:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_5\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '221,420p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"   221\\tsaturations — and nothing else.\\n   222\\t\\n   223\\t**What equal addresses do and do not mean.** Two segments address equally\\n   224\\t**iff** their loader-resolved interpretations are equal; this includes\\n   225\\tsegments that differ only in entity ids, batch order, provenance, or omitted\\n   226\\tdefaulted operands. No equivalence beyond that is claimed: different pc\\n   227\\tlayouts, different `:gensym` prefixes, different but *equivalent* instruction\\n   228\\tsequences, and compiler-renamed artifacts address **differently**. The\\n   229\\taddress is a fingerprint of one instruction stream, not a quotient of a\\n   230\\tsemantics.\\n   231\\t\\n   232\\tThe canonical encoding beneath `segment-key` is transitional\\n   233\\t(`dao.jing.md`, *Open items*). UCF inherits that limit unchanged: addresses\\n   234\\tare portable exactly as far as `dao.jing` addresses are, and when the pinned\\n   235\\tbyte encoding lands, `:yin.code/hash` values change with every other minted\\n   236\\taddress. This is the correct dependency; UCF must not mint a second\\n   237\\taddressing scheme.\\n   238\\t\\n   239\\t### 7.3.3 The contract stamp\\n   240\\t\\n   241\\tAn address fixes *which* instructions; it does not fix what `:call` *means*.\\n   242\\tInterpretation is supplied by the loader and the step loop (§1.1,\\n   243\\t*Interpretation Creates Semantics*), and the first draft pinned only the\\n   244\\topcode table — leaving resolution precedence, effect outcomes, call\\n   245\\trestoration, and scheduling outside the stamp. UCF therefore versions the\\n   246\\t**complete execution contract**: the stamp names one published revision of\\n   247\\t\\n   248\\t- the tuple grammar of §7.3.2 — the mnemonic set, each mnemonic's tuple\\n   249\\t  arity and operand kinds, and the saturation/defaults table — plus the\\n   250\\t  §2.6 well-formedness rules and the resolved-interpretation\\n   251\\t  (last-value-wins) rule;\\n   252\\t- the §2.4 opcode table and the §4.2 transitions;\\n   253\\t- the resolution precedence of `resolve-var` (env → store → primitives →\\n   254\\t  modules);\\n   255\\t- the effect→outcome mapping of §3.3 and `engine/handle-effect`;\\n   256\\t- call restoration and the scheduler semantics of §3.5 — wait-entry shapes,\\n   257\\t  `check-wait-set`'s round order, `semantic-restore`.\\n   258\\t\\n   259\\t```clojure\\n   260\\t:yin.k/contract {:yin.code/contract \\\"v2\\\"    ; the revision above, by name\\n   261\\t                 :yin.k/version    0}       ; this envelope's own version\\n   262\\t```\\n   263\\t\\n   264\\tA resumer whose loader implements a different revision does not guess: it\\n   265\\treturns `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` (§7.9) *before* lowering. Adding an opcode,\\n   266\\tchanging a transition, or changing the resolution order is a new revision; a\\n   267\\tresumer on the old one cannot run code that observes the difference, and the\\n   268\\tstamp is how it finds out before lowering rather than at an unknown integer\\n   269\\tin `case`. Two revisions may be declared compatible only by a published\\n   270\\tstatement in `yin.vm.semantic.md` §2.4, never by an engine's own judgment.\\n   271\\tBecause interpretation is part of identity, an engine's code index is keyed\\n   272\\t**per stamp revision**: lookup is `(get-in index [stamp address])`, and the\\n   273\\tsame address under different stamps is a different image the same way the\\n   274\\tsame datoms under different loaders are a different program.\\n   275\\t\\n   276\\t### 7.3.4 References to code inside a continuation\\n   277\\t\\n   278\\tEvery place the local record says `:segment <id>` — the control, each\\n   279\\t`:return` frame, each effect frame, each closure's `:segment` — the UCF frame\\n   280\\tsays `:yin.k/segment <address>`. Entry pcs and frame pcs are unchanged: a pc\\n   281\\tis meaningful relative to the resolved stream and is stable under everything\\n   282\\t§7.3.2 normalizes away.\\n   283\\t\\n   284\\t**The addressed object is the vector.** `dao.jing` materializes the exact\\n   285\\tpayload it is handed and verifies address-equals-hash on every write and read\\n   286\\t(`src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc`, `materialize!`); it supplies no aliases. So the payload\\n   287\\tstored at a code address **is the canonical instruction vector** — nothing\\n   288\\telse hashes to that address. A raw segment batch, with tempids and\\n   289\\tprovenance, materializes under its *own* different address and is not a\\n   290\\tresolution mechanism for UCF; `:yin.k/carried` carries vectors, not batches.\\n   291\\t\\n   292\\tLoading a fetched vector has two paths, and both are specified. The\\n   293\\t**projection path** converts the vector to loadable datoms deterministically\\n   294\\t— segment entity eid `0`, instruction of pc `p` eid `(inc p)`, each tuple\\n   295\\temitted with `:yin.code/segment 0` and its saturated operands as attribute\\n   296\\tdatoms (so the round trip vector → datoms → loader is the identity), each\\n   297\\tresolved-pc operand emitted as a ref to that eid — and hands the batch to\\n   298\\tthe ordinary §2.6 loader, so every well-formedness check runs on the way in.\\n   299\\tThe **direct path** decodes the canonical vector into an image without\\n   300\\tdatoms, as a foreign engine would. The two paths are a conformance\\n   301\\tobligation: both must yield the same image, and a test in the harness (§7.11)\\n   302\\tasserts it for every corpus segment. `yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` §7.2\\n   303\\tsupersedes this section's original designation of the projection path as the\\n   304\\treference path: the direct path is primary and is the reference; the\\n   305\\tprojection path is derived from it. Both invoke the same validator, and\\n   306\\tnothing may depend on which path a conforming engine takes.\\n   307\\t\\n   308\\tOn lowering, the resumer resolves each address in this order and stops at the\\n   309\\tfirst hit:\\n   310\\t\\n   311\\t1. its own code index for the value's stamp, keyed by address, each entry\\n   312\\t   verified once by hashing and grammar-validating its vector;\\n   313\\t2. `:yin.k/carried` in the value itself, verified the same way before load;\\n   314\\t3. a `dao.jing` read by address, verified the same way.\\n   315\\t\\n   316\\tVerification is two checks, always both: the vector must hash to the address\\n   317\\tit claims (`:yin.k/hash-mismatch` otherwise), and it must satisfy the stamp's\\n   318\\ttuple grammar — mnemonic set, arity, operand kinds, saturations in place\\n   319\\t(`:yin.k/undecodable` naming the pc otherwise; a correct hash is not\\n   320\\tstructural validation, the same rule the value decoder applies, §7.5.1). A\\n   321\\tmiss is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the address (§7.6.5); nothing from a\\n   322\\tvalue that fails either check is loaded.\\n   323\\t\\n   324\\t**The address index is additive; local-id immutability stands.** The §3.1\\n   325\\trule — an id already holding a different image is a load error, an identical\\n   326\\treload accepted — is unchanged by UCF, and the first draft's claim that a\\n   327\\thash check *replaces* it was wrong: two different, correctly hashed segments\\n   328\\tcan claim the same local id, and redirecting an existing id would silently\\n   329\\trewire every frame that names it. A lowering engine therefore keeps two\\n   330\\tmaps: `:code {local-id image}` under the §3.1 rule, and a separate\\n   331\\t`{stamp {address image}}` index with an `address → local-id` alias column.\\n   332\\tSegments loaded *by address* may mint fresh local ids; they may never reuse a\\n   333\\tlive one, and the alias column is checked so one address never lands under\\n   334\\ttwo local ids in one VM.\\n   335\\t\\n   336\\t**The continuation's own identity and the occurrence.** `:yin.k/id` is the\\n   337\\t`dao.jing` segment address of the UCF map with `:yin.k/id` removed — and by\\n   338\\tthe same no-alias argument, the payload published under `:yin.k/id` is the\\n   339\\t**id-less body**; a reader reconstructs the envelope with\\n   340\\t`(assoc body :yin.k/id (dao.jing/segment-key body))`. Equal continuations\\n   341\\tconverge; an idempotent re-publication of one encoding is a no-op. But\\n   342\\tencodings legitimately differ (§7.5.3), so `:yin.k/id` is *not* what custody,\\n   343\\tchains, or grants name. They name `:yin.k/occurrence` — the checkpoint\\n   344\\toccurrence id, minted exactly once, when a park is first exported, unique\\n   345\\tunder the emitter's attribution, and carried unchanged by every authorized\\n   346\\tsnapshot variant and every retry of the same park (§7.7.2). A re-emission\\n   347\\tafter a failed publication reuses the occurrence; it never mints a second\\n   348\\trunnable checkpoint.\\n   349\\t\\n   350\\t## §7.4 Safepoints — where a canonical configuration exists (blocker 1)\\n   351\\t\\n   352\\t### 7.4.1 Only safepoints lift, and the table is the machine's\\n   353\\t\\n   354\\tThe linear machine's registers are canonical only *between* instructions.\\n   355\\tInside a `:call` that has popped its operands but not yet pushed a frame, or\\n   356\\tinside a superinstruction the loader fused (§6.4), no ⟨C, E, S, K⟩ exists\\n   357\\tthat both describes the state and is expressible in the §2.4 vocabulary.\\n   358\\tUCF resolves this by rule rather than by metadata: **a continuation may be\\n   359\\tlifted only at a safepoint**, and a safepoint is a transition of the\\n   360\\treference machine at which the machine parks. The first draft's table\\n   361\\tpresented itself as complete while omitting the parks that arrive through\\n   362\\t`:call` — a called host function that returns an effect blocks exactly as an\\n   363\\tinstruction effect does, through `apply-call` → `handle-effect` →\\n   364\\t`call-park-entries`, and both ordinary and tail calls reach that path\\n   365\\t(`yin.vm.semantic`, `apply-call`). The complete set:\\n   366\\t\\n   367\\t| Safepoint kind | Raised by | Canonical resume pc | Stack in frame (St) | Accumulator on resume |\\n   368\\t|---|---|---|---|---|\\n   369\\t| explicit park | `:park` | `pc+1` | St at park | the resume value |\\n   370\\t| blocked read | `:stream-next` → `blocked` | `pc+1` | St (cursor ref was in `val`) | the read value; `nil` on `end`, `:dao.stream/gap` on gap |\\n   371\\t| blocked write | `:stream-put` → `full` | `pc+1` | St minus the popped target ref | the written value, on retry `ok` |\\n   372\\t| FFI call, sent | `:ffi-call` → `:dao.stream/ok` | `pc+1` | St minus the popped args | the correlated response's `ok` value; its `error` raises |\\n   373\\t| FFI call, retained | `:ffi-call` → `:dao.stream/full` | `pc+1` | St minus the popped args | as sent, once the request is appended and the response correlates |\\n   374\\t| effectful call | `:call`/`:tailcall` whose operator yields a blocking effect (`require`, stream-module functions, effect handlers) | `pc+1` | St minus the popped operator and args | the effect's resume value, per its kind as above |\\n   375\\t| halt | `:halt`, `:return` with empty K | — | — | not resumable; a `:yin.k/type :yin.k/result` travels instead |\\n   376\\t\\n   377\\tTwo corrections to the first draft's table stand beside the addition.\\n   378\\t`:current-continuation` is **not** a safepoint: it does not park — it\\n   379\\treifies `val ← {seg, pc+1, E, St, K}` and continues, so a reified\\n   380\\tcontinuation is a *value class* of the encoding (§7.5.1), never a lift\\n   381\\tpoint. And `pc+1` as the resume pc is verified sound for every row above:\\n   382\\t§2.6 requires the final pc to be a terminator, so every listed instruction\\n   383\\thas a successor in its own segment.\\n   384\\t\\n   385\\tThe `:yin.k/frame` is exactly the wait-entry shape of `yin.vm.semantic.md`\\n   386\\t§3.5, with segments replaced by addresses and values by their portable\\n   387\\tencodings (§7.5):\\n   388\\t\\n   389\\t```clojure\\n   390\\t:yin.k/frame\\n   391\\t{:yin.k/segment  :segment/sha256-3f1a…\\n   392\\t :yin.k/pc       5                         ; the resume pc, already pc+1\\n   393\\t :yin.k/reason   :next                     ; :park | :next | :put | :ffi | :ffi-request | :call-effect\\n   394\\t :yin.k/val      nil                       ; accumulator; nil except where §7.4.3 pre-fills it\\n   395\\t :yin.k/stack    [ … ]                     ; St per the table above, encoded\\n   396\\t :yin.k/env      { sym → encoded value }   ; E\\n   397\\t :yin.k/k        [ {:yin.k/frame-type :return                    ; or :eval-call / :request-sent,\\n   398\\t                    :yin.k/segment :segment/sha256-…             ; which also carry :yin.k/call-id\\n   399\\t                    :yin.k/pc 5 :yin.k/env {…} :yin.k/stack-base 0} … ]   ; K, innermost last\\n   400\\t :yin.k/pending  {…}}                      ; the wait it was in, §7.4.3 — one variant per reason\\n   401\\t```\\n   402\\t\\n   403\\t**Quiescence.** The migration unit is one task: the whole blocked machine\\n   404\\t(§7.6.3). Lifting additionally requires the machine's ready-queue to be\\n   405\\tempty — a machine with runnable work queued is mid-scheduling, its next\\n   406\\tconfiguration is not the parked one, and lift answers `:yin.k/not-quiescent`.\\n   407\\t\\n   408\\t### 7.4.2 Safepoint maps for foreign engines: static facts only\\n   409\\t\\n   410\\tThe reference machine needs no metadata because its registers *are* the\\n   411\\tcanonical configuration. A foreign engine — a register allocator over the\\n   412\\timage, a WebAssembly lowering, a JIT — keeps its state in physical locations\\n   413\\tof its own choosing, and the first draft promised it reconstruction data\\n   414\\tthat is not a function of the code: absolute stack depth and environment\\n   415\\tkey sets at a pc depend on the *activation* — a closure enters with the\\n   416\\tcaller's residual operand stack and an environment formed from its captured\\n   417\\tenvironment plus arguments (`apply-call`), and §2.6 enforces no equal stack\\n   418\\theights at joins, so the same pc is legitimately reached at many depths.\\n   419\\tNone of that is derivable, and none of it needs to be: **the frame carries\\n   420\\tit.** E, St, and every K frame's `:stack-base` and env travel in the value;\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n15:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_6\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '821,1020p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"   821\\t```clojure\\n   822\\t:yin.k/store {counter 41\\n   823\\t              'log {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/stream …}}\\n   824\\t```\\n   825\\t\\n   826\\tThe FFI pair is never in the slice — a program that names the pair's store\\n   827\\tkeys directly holds handles, and the lift refuses it (§7.5.4). A resumer has\\n   828\\tits own pair; outstanding calls route per §7.4.3.\\n   829\\t\\n   830\\t**There is no merge.** The first draft said, in two places, both that the\\n   831\\tslice wins on collision and that the slice merges *under* the local store —\\n   832\\ta contradiction, and beneath it a real defect: resolution is env → store →\\n   833\\tprimitives (`resolve-var`), so a resumer whose local store happens to bind\\n   834\\t`+` would change what the migrated program resolves, without any collision\\n   835\\tat all. Merging in either order is the wrong shape. **The resumed task runs\\n   836\\tin an isolated execution store**: a fresh store whose contents are exactly\\n   837\\tthe carried slice, the remapped cells of §7.5.3, and the resumer's FFI pair\\n   838\\t— nothing else of the resumer's, nothing of any other task's. A name absent\\n   839\\tfrom the slice resolves through primitives and modules at the resumer\\n   840\\texactly as it did at the emitter, because there is no third store to\\n   841\\tintervene; a slice key cannot collide with a receiver task's key because no\\n   842\\treceiver key participates; resource entries are remapped to fresh local\\n   843\\tkeys (cursor cells per §7.5.3, stream handles per §7.4.3), and non-resource\\n   844\\tentries keep their keys verbatim. \\\"Unreachable store entries do not travel\\\"\\n   845\\tsurvives unchanged — anything the program did not observe is not the\\n   846\\tprogram's state — and nothing else shares state with the resumed task by\\n   847\\taccident.\\n   848\\t\\n   849\\t### 7.6.3 The migration unit is one task\\n   850\\t\\n   851\\tThe reference machine's scheduler state does not live in the store, and the\\n   852\\tfirst draft transported none of it. Both omissions are defects: the\\n   853\\tmachine's fresh identifiers come from `:id-counter`, and `:resume` resolves\\n   854\\tits operand against the `:parked` map — a fresh receiver could reuse an\\n   855\\texisting generated identifier, or fail to resolve a parked continuation a\\n   856\\tmigrated segment is about to name (`engine/park-continuation`,\\n   857\\t`engine/resume-continuation`, §3.5). UCF therefore states the unit and\\n   858\\tcarries the state:\\n   859\\t\\n   860\\t- **The unit is the whole blocked machine**: registers (the frame), the\\n   861\\t  wait-set (as pending variants, §7.4.3), the referenced `:parked` records,\\n   862\\t  and the fresh-name counter. The ready-queue must be empty at lift\\n   863\\t  (`:yin.k/not-quiescent` otherwise, §7.4.1) — a task with queued runnable\\n   864\\t  work has a next configuration that is not the parked one.\\n   865\\t- **Fresh-name state travels.** `:yin.k/scheduler {:yin.k/id-counter n\\n   866\\t  …}` carries the counter verbatim; the lowered machine adopts\\n   867\\t  `max(local, carried)` so generated ids never repeat, and ids already\\n   868\\t  minted (gensym results in env, stack, or store) travel as ordinary values,\\n   869\\t  unaffected by the counter.\\n   870\\t- **Referenced parked records travel.** The fixed point\\n   871\\t  of §7.6.1 collects every `:resume` operand in reachable code and every\\n   872\\t  `[:parked id]` value from reachable environments. Each must resolve\\n   873\\t  within this task to a parked record carried under\\n   874\\t  `:yin.k/scheduler :yin.k/parked {pid → frame}`. A missing id referenced\\n   875\\t  as a code operand is reported as an unsupplied requirement\\n   876\\t  (`:yin.k/unsatisfied`). A missing id referenced by an active value\\n   877\\t  refuses the lift (`:yin.k/non-portable`, kind `:foreign-parked-ref`),\\n   878\\t  as it represents corrupted state. Parked records not named by the\\n   879\\t  reachable graph do not travel.\\n   880\\t\\n   881\\t### 7.6.4 Modules\\n   882\\t\\n   883\\t`:yin.k/modules` names each module by the content address of its published\\n   884\\tmanifest, on the model of `dao.space.index/publish-index!` (`dao.jing.md`,\\n   885\\t*Publication from an agent*). A module is satisfied when the resumer holds a\\n   886\\tmodule registry entry (`yin.vm.module` — a value, supplied by the\\n   887\\tcomposition) whose manifest address is equal; it is not satisfied by a\\n   888\\tsame-named module of different content. Module *code* is segments and is\\n   889\\tcovered by `:yin.k/segments` and the manifest's own addressing; the manifest\\n   890\\tpins the effect handlers, the exported primitive profiles (§7.5.2), and —\\n   891\\tper §7.6.1 — the store footprint the fixed point needs to claim\\n   892\\t`:complete`.\\n   893\\t\\n   894\\t### 7.6.5 The satisfaction check\\n   895\\t\\n   896\\tBefore lowering, the resumer evaluates `:yin.k/requires` against what it\\n   897\\tholds and answers with one outcome:\\n   898\\t\\n   899\\t```clojure\\n   900\\t{:yin.k/status    :yin.k/unsatisfied\\n   901\\t :yin.k/discovery :incomplete                    ; or :blocked, §7.6.1 — the computed status of the closure itself\\n   902\\t :yin.k/missing   {:yin.k/segments       #{:segment/sha256-9c02…}\\n   903\\t                   :yin.k/primitives     {println <expected profile>}\\n   904\\t                   :yin.k/modules        {my.mod …}\\n   905\\t                   :yin.k/streams        #{<identity>}\\n   906\\t                   :yin.k/cursor-profiles #{:dao.stream/file-v1}}}  ; keys present only where something is missing\\n   907\\t```\\n   908\\t\\n   909\\tThe check reports everything missing in one pass *that the fixed point could\\n   910\\tsee*, and says which kind of pass it was: `:complete` (the closure is fully\\n   911\\tcomputed; the missing list is exhaustive), `:incomplete` (a module footprint\\n   912\\twas undeclared; the missing list is a lower bound), or `:blocked` (a missing\\n   913\\tsegment stopped discovery; satisfying `:yin.k/segments` and re-running the\\n   914\\tcheck is the only way forward). A missing segment may be resolved by\\n   915\\t`dao.jing` fetch (§7.3.4); a missing primitive, module, or cursor profile\\n   916\\tcannot be resolved by the resumer and is a routing decision for whoever\\n   917\\tchose this resumer. A resumer never lowers a partially satisfied value; a\\n   918\\thost with no implementation for something answers with the qualified\\n   919\\tunsupported outcome, which `datom.world.md` calls a correct outcome, not a\\n   920\\tgap to be filled.\\n   921\\t\\n   922\\t## §7.7 Ownership arbitration — custody as a lease over datoms (blocker 4)\\n   923\\t\\n   924\\t### 7.7.1 The race, named\\n   925\\t\\n   926\\tThe emitter parks, lifts, appends the UCF value to a medium, and keeps its\\n   927\\tlocal `:parked` record. Three readers see the value. The emitter's own wait\\n   928\\tset wakes because the stream it was waiting on delivered. Now four machines\\n   929\\tbelieve they may continue one computation. Nothing in DaoStream can stop\\n   930\\tthem: a stream has no privileged reader, there is no destructive read, and\\n   931\\t\\\"the hard part of a take was never the removal but the *exclusion* of\\n   932\\tcompeting takers, which is a lease over datoms\\\" (`dao.stream.md`,\\n   933\\t*Explicitly Absent*).\\n   934\\t\\n   935\\tUCF adopts that sentence as its design. **Resumption is a take. A take is a\\n   936\\twrite. Exclusion is a lease.** Nothing new is added to the stream contract,\\n   937\\tand no key is added to any result map.\\n   938\\t\\n   939\\t### 7.7.2 Custody facts, and their dispatch keys\\n   940\\t\\n   941\\tCustody is negotiated on an **arbitration medium** — a `dao.space` whose\\n   942\\tfacts are ordinary datoms — using `dao.lease.md`'s vocabulary unchanged,\\n   943\\twith the checkpoint **occurrence** as the leased subject. The UCF-authored\\n   944\\tfacts are dispatch-keyed exactly as lease facts are (`dao.lease.md`,\\n   945\\t*Vocabulary*: a reader switches on `:dao.lease/status`; a fact carrying it\\n   946\\tis a lease fact, one carrying neither is ignored):\\n   947\\t\\n   948\\t| Fact | Dispatch | Author | Required keys |\\n   949\\t|---|---|---|---|\\n   950\\t| offer | `:yin.k/custody :yin.k/offered` | emitter | `:yin.k/occurrence`, `:yin.k/id` (this snapshot variant), `:yin.k/policy`, `:yin.k/medium` (the carrier identity) |\\n   951\\t| checkpoint done | `:yin.k/custody :yin.k/resumed` | holder | `:yin.k/occurrence`, `:dao.lease/lease`, `:yin.k/result` — the address of the successor's occurrence value or of a `:yin.k/result` |\\n   952\\t\\n   953\\tLease facts themselves keep their own table verbatim — proposal\\n   954\\t(`:dao.lease/proposed`, with `:dao.lease/subject = {:yin.k/occurrence O}`),\\n   955\\tgrant (`:dao.lease/accepted`, with `:dao.lease/lease :dao.lease/holder\\n   956\\t:dao.lease/subject :dao.lease/duration`, and `:dao.lease/max` optional),\\n   957\\trefusal, release, renewal, lapse — each dispatched on `:dao.lease/status`\\n   958\\tor `:dao.lease/event`, each with `dao.lease.md`'s required keys, unchanged.\\n   959\\tA lease judge reading the same medium switches on the lease keys and\\n   960\\tignores the `:yin.k/custody` facts; a UCF reader switches on\\n   961\\t`:yin.k/custody` and reads lease facts for authority. Both `:yin.k/custody`\\n   962\\tfacts are **evidence, not authority**: an offer creates no claim, and a\\n   963\\t`:resumed` record is a holder's report — completion is the grantor's ledger\\n   964\\ttransition, not the report (§7.7.6). Authority is exactly `dao.lease.md`'s:\\n   965\\t**only grantor-authored facts establish terms.**\\n   966\\t\\n   967\\tThe subject of every grant is the **occurrence**, never `:yin.k/id`:\\n   968\\tsnapshot variants and retries of one park share one occurrence, so no pair\\n   969\\tof encodings can hold independent grants, and an equal-content copy minted\\n   970\\tby coincidence is a different occurrence and a different computation\\n   971\\t(§7.3.4).\\n   972\\t\\n   973\\t### 7.7.3 The authority: what the grantor possesses\\n   974\\t\\n   975\\t`dao.lease.md` is explicit that a judge must be composed **inside the\\n   976\\tboundary that possesses the resource** and must reclaim *by acting on what\\n   977\\tit itself holds*; a judge that possesses nothing is outside the contract.\\n   978\\tThe first draft designated \\\"the composition-designated arbiter for the\\n   979\\tcarrier medium,\\\" defaulting to the emitter — an arbiter relative to a\\n   980\\tcarrier, possessing an offer, which is a copy of content: the same\\n   981\\toccurrence carried on a second medium would acquire a second arbiter, and\\n   982\\tneither arbiter controls execution of anything. This section replaces that\\n   983\\twith a grounded design.\\n   984\\t\\n   985\\t**The possessed resource is the occurrence's execution ledger**: the set of\\n   986\\tdatoms about one occurrence id on the arbitration `dao.space` — its offer,\\n   987\\tgrants, renewals, lapses, and resumed reports, plus the occurrence's\\n   988\\t**admission epoch** (§7.7.5). The **grantor is the boundary that transacts\\n   989\\tthat space** — its transactor. Possession is exact and of the right kind:\\n   990\\t\\n   991\\t- the ledger's datom base lives inside the transactor's boundary, and\\n   992\\t  admission to it is the transactor's alone — no other boundary can write\\n   993\\t  those datoms, which is why grantor-authored facts are worth more than\\n   994\\t  holder reports;\\n   995\\t- the **reclaim** is the grantor acting on what it holds: recording the\\n   996\\t  lapse on its own base and advancing the occurrence's epoch datom. It\\n   997\\t  needs no cooperation from any holder and no reach into any carrier;\\n   998\\t- the arbiter is **not medium-relative**: the binding\\n   999\\t  occurrence → arbitration space is minted at first export and travels *in\\n  1000\\t  the value* (`:yin.k/arbitration`, §7.2), so every carrier, every snapshot\\n  1001\\t  variant, and every copy names the same one authority. The same content on\\n  1002\\t  two media does not create a second arbiter, because the authority is named\\n  1003\\t  by the occurrence, not discovered from wherever a value happened to land;\\n  1004\\t- **one holder per occurrence is the grantor's policy, enforced where\\n  1005\\t  policy lives**: `dao.lease` does not impose it, and UCF does not add to\\n  1006\\t  the vocabulary — the grantor's ledger admits at most one live lease per\\n  1007\\t  occurrence (a second grant requires the prior lease reclaimed first,\\n  1008\\t  which `dao.lease.md`'s own validity rules make the grantor the sole\\n  1009\\t  author of). Exclusive custody is thus a property of the ledger the\\n  1010\\t  grantor possesses, not an aspiration over streams it does not.\\n  1011\\t\\n  1012\\tWhat is possessed, concretely, is the *right to admit effects as this\\n  1013\\toccurrence* — the ledger is where epochs and dedup-stamped admissions are\\n  1014\\trecorded, which is what §7.7.5's fencing consults. A composition that\\n  1015\\tcannot wire a `dao.space` transactor as authority cannot offer\\n  1016\\t`:yin.k/exclusive` custody at all; it composes `:yin.k/fork` only. That is\\n  1017\\tan honest capability limit, not a gap to paper over.\\n  1018\\t\\n  1019\\t### 7.7.4 The exporting state: fencing the source before publication\\n  1020\\t\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n17:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_7\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '1021,1220p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"  1021\\tEmitter-as-candidate was the first draft's whole answer to the\\n  1022\\tsource-wakeup race, and it does not close the publication window. The\\n  1023\\tlifecycle published first and made the emitter a candidate afterward, but\\n  1024\\tnothing ever removed the emitter's *local* eligibility: its driver may\\n  1025\\tstill poll the wait set — and polling a blocked writer **performs the\\n  1026\\tappend** (the engine, a `:put` entry retries `append!` on every\\n  1027\\tpoll) — may restore a woken entry from the ready queue, may satisfy a\\n  1028\\tdirect `:resume`, and may retry a blocked write, all after the value is in\\n  1029\\tother machines' hands. Delaying register restoration is too late; the\\n  1030\\tappend is the effect.\\n  1031\\t\\n  1032\\tUCF therefore defines an explicit **exporting** transition that precedes\\n  1033\\tany publication, and the lift driver performs it as one step over the\\n  1034\\tmachine value:\\n  1035\\t\\n  1036\\t1. **Quiesce**: the ready-queue must be empty (§7.6.3).\\n  1037\\t2. **Detach the wait set**: every wait entry moves from the machine's\\n  1038\\t   wait-set into the export record. The machine's wait-set is now empty,\\n  1039\\t   so *there is nothing to poll* — no poll, no append, no effect. The\\n  1040\\t   entries are not lost: the export record retains them verbatim; they are\\n  1041\\t   the source of the `:yin.k/pending` variants of §7.4.3.\\n  1042\\t3. **Neutralize control**: the record's control and K are already absent\\n  1043\\t   (the machine is parked or blocked); direct `:resume` resolves against\\n  1044\\t   `:parked`, and the parked records referenced by reachable code travel\\n  1045\\t   inside the export record — a local `:resume` against them is refused\\n  1046\\t   while exporting (the restore function observes the exporting machine\\n  1047\\t   and declines), because the parked id now names a checkpoint that may be\\n  1048\\t   granted elsewhere.\\n  1049\\t4. **Publish, then offer**: append segments and the value body; record\\n  1050\\t   `:yin.k/offered` on the arbitration medium. Only now do readers exist,\\n  1051\\t   and the source was fenced before any of them did.\\n  1052\\t\\n  1053\\tThe exporting machine is pure data with an empty wait-set and a retained\\n  1054\\texport record — no engine change, no callback, no timer; the drivers of the\\n  1055\\tworld observe the state as they observe `ready-for-ingress?` (§3.1), and a\\n  1056\\tdriver that polls an exporting machine's wait set anyway is a host assembly\\n  1057\\tdefect of the same kind as wiring a refusing deposit destination.\\n  1058\\t\\n  1059\\t**Failure recovery and idempotent retry.** If publication fails — a full\\n  1060\\tcarrier, an unreachable arbitration medium — the machine stays exporting\\n  1061\\tand the composition retries; a retry re-encodes or reuses the encoded value\\n  1062\\tand **reuses the occurrence** (§7.3.4), so no retry mints a second runnable\\n  1063\\tcheckpoint. Abandoning the export reinstates the export record into the\\n  1064\\tmachine (wait entries return to the wait-set, parked records to `:parked`)\\n  1065\\tand the task resumes as a purely local one — the sole paths back to local\\n  1066\\texecution are *abort export* (before the offer is recorded) and *a grant to\\n  1067\\tthe emitter itself* (after); there is no third.\\n  1068\\t\\n  1069\\t### 7.7.5 Fencing effects: epochs, operation ids, and what exactly-once costs\\n  1070\\t\\n  1071\\t`dao.lease.md` states it plainly: **the absence of a `:lapsed` fact is\\n  1072\\tnever evidence of tenure.** The first draft's fencing — stamp effects with\\n  1073\\tthe lease id, let consumers consult the medium and drop effects whose\\n  1074\\tincarnation has a `:lapsed` record — fails in every direction the review\\n  1075\\tnamed: a partitioned consumer accepts stale effects (it cannot see the\\n  1076\\tlapse); a delayed legitimate effect is discarded after release; repeated\\n  1077\\teffects within one lease share one incarnation and are indistinguishable; a\\n  1078\\treplacement holder repeats an effect its predecessor committed; and apply\\n  1079\\tids correlate requests with responses, they do not suppress duplicates.\\n  1080\\tLapse-record filtering is correlation, not fencing. What UCF requires\\n  1081\\tinstead:\\n  1082\\t\\n  1083\\t**Authority epochs, checked atomically with commitment.** The occurrence's\\n  1084\\tledger carries an epoch datom, advanced by the grantor on every reclaim\\n  1085\\t(§7.7.3) — a monotone number the possessing boundary alone can write. A\\n  1086\\tconsumer that must be exactly-once (a `dao.space` transactor, an FFI callee\\n  1087\\twith side effects) admits a fenced effect only in the same act that reads\\n  1088\\tthe epoch: the check and the commit are one transition against the\\n  1089\\tadmission resource, never check-then-act. An effect whose incarnation's\\n  1090\\tepoch is not the current one is refused at commitment. This is\\n  1091\\t`dao.lease.md`'s \\\"fencing from the resource\\\" for durable resources, and it\\n  1092\\tis a composition duty that composition owes exactly once.\\n  1093\\t\\n  1094\\t**Stable operation ids, and durable dedup.** Every fenced effect carries\\n  1095\\t`:yin.k/op-id {:yin.k/occurrence O :yin.k/seq n}` — assigned by the fenced\\n  1096\\twriter in emission order. Op-ids are deterministic functions of the\\n  1097\\tcheckpoint and the operation sequence: a replacement holder resuming the\\n  1098\\t*same* occurrence replays the same op-ids, which is what makes\\n  1099\\tacross-incarnation dedup possible at all. Exactly-once consumers keep a\\n  1100\\tdurable `{op-id → committed result}` record, written in the same atomic\\n  1101\\ttransition as the epoch check; a replayed op-id returns the recorded result\\n  1102\\tand commits nothing. Within one incarnation and across incarnations, the\\n  1103\\tsame op-id is the same logical operation — the room for exactly-once\\n  1104\\tsemantics that lease identity alone cannot provide.\\n  1105\\t\\n  1106\\t**Where the stamp rides.** Program values are program data; UCF never\\n  1107\\tedits them. A resumed incarnation running under a lease emits through a\\n  1108\\t**fenced writer** the composition wires: a writer that wraps each appended\\n  1109\\tvalue in an envelope `{:yin.k/envelope … :yin.k/incarnation <lease>\\n  1110\\t:yin.k/op-id … :yin.k/value <the program value, verbatim>}`. Exactly-once\\n  1111\\tconsumers read stamped envelopes; a stream wired bare — no fence — is\\n  1112\\t*declared* unprotected, and a composition that needs exactly-once delivery\\n  1113\\tthrough it has wired the wrong thing. Fencing is opt-in per stream, exactly\\n  1114\\tas `dao.lease.md` places the burden: \\\"a holder needing exclusion obtains it\\n  1115\\tfrom the resource.\\\" No callback tells a lapsed holder to stop; it stops at\\n  1116\\tits own lease bound (`dao.lease.md`, *The holder*), and what it did past\\n  1117\\tthe bound is distinguishable after the fact by epoch and op-id.\\n  1118\\t\\n  1119\\t**Durable completion of a checkpoint.** Publication of a successor is not\\n  1120\\tcompletion. The holder's exit sequence is: append the successor value to\\n  1121\\tthe carrier, append `:yin.k/resumed` (evidence) to the arbitration medium,\\n  1122\\tappend `:dao.lease/released`. Completion is the **grantor's ledger\\n  1123\\ttransition** — observing the release (or the `:resumed` evidence followed\\n  1124\\tby release) and closing the occurrence's tenure. The crash windows are then\\n  1125\\tbounded and harmless: a crash after the successor append but before the\\n  1126\\trecords leaves an orphan successor — content-addressed, idempotent,\\n  1127\\tre-readable as evidence but granting nothing; the lease lapses by silence;\\n  1128\\tthe grantor re-grants the *last recorded* occurrence, and the orphan chain\\n  1129\\tis queryable history (§7.7.6). A crash mid-sequence never requires the\\n  1130\\tgrantor to trust a holder's report as proof of durable completion, because\\n  1131\\tthe report is evidence and the ledger is authority.\\n  1132\\t\\n  1133\\t**Partition policy, stated honestly.** A fenced consumer that cannot reach\\n  1134\\tthe authority (epoch unreadable) must **suspend protected effect\\n  1135\\tcommitment** — fail closed — or the composition has chosen at-least-once\\n  1136\\tdelivery with after-the-fact dedup reconciliation; which one is a declared\\n  1137\\tproperty of the consumer, and a consumer that neither checks nor declares\\n  1138\\tis not exactly-once and may not claim to be. A holder partitioned from the\\n  1139\\tarbitration medium runs to its own lease bound and its effects are\\n  1140\\tafter-the-fact distinguishable, which is the guarantee `dao.lease.md`\\n  1141\\tactually makes; UCF does not promise more. A grantor that lost its ledger\\n  1142\\treclaims and re-grants (`dao.lease.md`, *Restart*); **permanent** authority\\n  1143\\tloss breaks exclusive custody irreparably — the ledger was the resource —\\n  1144\\tand repair is a governance act outside this protocol. The arbitration\\n  1145\\tmedium's durability is therefore a composition duty owed before\\n  1146\\t`:yin.k/exclusive` is offered at all (`dao.lease.md`, *Composition\\n  1147\\tduties*: a judge whose durability matches the resource).\\n  1148\\t\\n  1149\\t### 7.7.6 Successors and chains\\n  1150\\t\\n  1151\\tA resumed continuation runs to its next safepoint and either halts or parks\\n  1152\\tagain. Either way it produces a successor value — a `:yin.k/continuation`\\n  1153\\tor a `:yin.k/result` — whose `:yin.k/origin` carries the predecessor's\\n  1154\\t`:yin.k/occurrence` and the lease it ran under. Each park is a **new\\n  1155\\toccurrence**: minted fresh by the running holder, chained to its\\n  1156\\tpredecessor, and itself the subject of the next custody cycle. The holder\\n  1157\\tappends the successor, records `:yin.k/resumed` with the successor's\\n  1158\\taddress, and releases. The chain of occurrences linked by `:yin.k/origin`\\n  1159\\tis the migratory computation's history, queryable on the arbitration\\n  1160\\tmedium: where it ran, under which grant, what it became. A rollback is a\\n  1161\\tre-offer of an earlier link; effects past that link need compensation, not\\n  1162\\trecomputation (`streams-all-the-way-down.md` §6.4) — and §7.7.5's op-ids\\n  1163\\tare what compensation reads to find what was done.\\n  1164\\t\\n  1165\\t### 7.7.7 Restart\\n  1166\\t\\n  1167\\tA grantor that lost its ledger reclaims and re-grants (`dao.lease.md`,\\n  1168\\t*Restart*: reclaim first, then grant afresh — a new grant alone does not\\n  1169\\tend the prior tenure). Its inventory of possessed resources is the set of\\n  1170\\toccurrence ledgers it transacts; its recoverable holder for each is the\\n  1171\\t`:dao.lease/holder` of the last grant, both of which are facts on (or the\\n  1172\\trecoverable state of) the space it possesses. Both are queries, which is\\n  1173\\twhy custody lives on a `dao.space` and not on the carrier stream.\\n  1174\\t\\n  1175\\t## §7.8 Lifecycle: lift on park, lower on resume\\n  1176\\t\\n  1177\\t**Lift** (emitter side; composition-driven, never automatic on every park):\\n  1178\\t\\n  1179\\t1. The machine is parked or blocked with a local record `r`, and its\\n  1180\\t   ready-queue is empty, else `:yin.k/not-quiescent`.\\n  1181\\t2. **Enter exporting** (§7.7.4): detach the wait set into the export\\n  1182\\t   record, neutralize direct `:resume`. From here the source is fenced; no\\n  1183\\t   poll, retry, or local resume can perform an effect.\\n  1184\\t3. `lift(export-record)`: confirm the record is at a safepoint (§7.4.1),\\n  1185\\t   else `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; canonicalize every referenced segment and\\n  1186\\t   compute its address (§7.3.2), else the refusal; encode (§7.5), else the\\n  1187\\t   refusal; compute `:yin.k/requires` and `:yin.k/store` by the fixed point\\n  1188\\t   (§7.6), recording the discovery status; stamp the contract (§7.3.3); on\\n  1189\\t   first export, mint the occurrence; mint `:yin.k/id`.\\n  1190\\t4. Append the canonical instruction vectors of every required segment to a\\n  1191\\t   `dao.jing` intake pool (the payloads *are* the vectors, §7.3.4), then\\n  1192\\t   the id-less value body to the carrier medium; observe `:dao.stream/ok`\\n  1193\\t   on each. `:yin.k/carried` may then be empty. Retry of any failed append\\n  1194\\t   reuses the occurrence (§7.7.4).\\n  1195\\t5. Under `:exclusive`, append `:yin.k/offered` to the arbitration medium.\\n  1196\\t   The export record stays for recovery; the emitter is now a candidate\\n  1197\\t   like any other, and wakes only on custody (a grant to itself), never on\\n  1198\\t   data.\\n  1199\\t\\n  1200\\t**Lower** (resumer side):\\n  1201\\t\\n  1202\\t1. Read the value; check `:yin.k/contract`, else `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`.\\n  1203\\t2. Verify `:yin.k/id` by re-hashing the body, else `:yin.k/hash-mismatch`.\\n  1204\\t3. Decode against the §7.5.1 grammar with full structural validation, else\\n  1205\\t   `:yin.k/undecodable` — a correct hash is not structural validation, and\\n  1206\\t   both checks always run.\\n  1207\\t4. Evaluate `:yin.k/requires` (§7.6.5); fetch what may be fetched (segments\\n  1208\\t   by address, verified by re-canonicalization); else\\n  1209\\t   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with the discovery status.\\n  1210\\t5. Under `:exclusive`, attach to the arbitration medium (an attachment\\n  1211\\t   failure here is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming it — the value declares its\\n  1212\\t   authority, §7.7.3), propose, then observe the grant, else\\n  1213\\t   `:yin.k/not-holder` with the lease state observed; while a proposal is\\n  1214\\t   unanswered the driver's step answers `:yin.k/awaiting-grant` — a\\n  1215\\t   grantor owes no answer and no bound applies (`dao.lease.md`,\\n  1216\\t   *Authority*).\\n  1217\\t6. Load every required segment by address into the per-stamp index,\\n  1218\\t   verifying each (§7.3.4).\\n  1219\\t7. **Restore into an isolated store** (§7.6.2): the slice, the remapped\\n  1220\\t   cells, the resumer's FFI pair. Attach to every stream descriptor in the\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n19:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_8\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '1221,1520p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"  1221\\t   frame, pending waits, and cells (§7.4.3); an attachment `not-found` is\\n  1222\\t   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream. **Any failure from this step\\n  1223\\t   onward — after custody was acquired — releases first**: the resumer\\n  1224\\t   appends `:dao.lease/released`, the occurrence returns to offered, and\\n  1225\\t   the failure outcome is returned. A resumer that dies without releasing\\n  1226\\t   is recovered by the lease's own lapse; nothing else is owed.\\n  1227\\t8. `lower(frame, layout)` into engine registers; re-mint every pending wait\\n  1228\\t   in the local wait set; seed `:id-counter` per §7.6.3; run — through a\\n  1229\\t   fenced writer under `:exclusive` (§7.7.5).\\n  1230\\t9. At the next safepoint or halt, produce the successor occurrence\\n  1231\\t   (§7.7.6), record `:yin.k/resumed`, release.\\n  1232\\t\\n  1233\\tNeither sequence contains a callback. Each is a step function a\\n  1234\\tcomposition-supplied driver repeats, as for every other interpreter in the\\n  1235\\tsystem.\\n  1236\\t\\n  1237\\t## §7.9 Outcome algebra\\n  1238\\t\\n  1239\\tEvery UCF operation returns one map dispatching on `:yin.k/status`; the set\\n  1240\\tis closed and every non-`ok` outcome carries the data needed to act on it.\\n  1241\\t\\n  1242\\t| `:yin.k/status` | Raised by | Carries |\\n  1243\\t|---|---|---|\\n  1244\\t| `:yin.k/ok` | any | the value, or the lowered VM |\\n  1245\\t| `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` | lift | `:yin.k/segment`, `:yin.k/pc` |\\n  1246\\t| `:yin.k/not-quiescent` | lift | the ready-queue depth |\\n  1247\\t| `:yin.k/non-portable` | lift | `:yin.k/path`, `:yin.k/kind`, `:yin.k/hint` — kinds at §7.5.4, cycles included as `:kind :cyclic` |\\n  1248\\t| `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` | lower | `:yin.k/contract` expected and found |\\n  1249\\t| `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` | lower | the address claimed and the address computed |\\n  1250\\t| `:yin.k/undecodable` | lower | `:yin.k/path`, the structural defect — malformed markers, missing or cyclic refs, malformed-but-correctly-hashed bodies |\\n  1251\\t| `:yin.k/unsatisfied` | lower | `:yin.k/missing`, `:yin.k/discovery` — includes attachment failures, cursor profiles, and an unreachable arbitration medium |\\n  1252\\t| `:yin.k/awaiting-grant` | lower | `:yin.k/occurrence`, the proposal — a step status while a proposal is unanswered, bounded by nothing (`dao.lease.md`) |\\n  1253\\t| `:yin.k/not-holder` | lower | `:yin.k/occurrence`, the lease state observed |\\n  1254\\t\\n  1255\\tTerminal DaoStream outcomes met during lift or lower (`transport-error`,\\n  1256\\t`closed`, `invalid-descriptor`, …) are surfaced unchanged inside the UCF\\n  1257\\toutcome under their own key `:dao.stream/outcome`, exactly as the transport\\n  1258\\tproduced them; UCF does not rename them, nest them under private keys, or\\n  1259\\ttranslate between them. FFI requests and responses inside a value use the\\n  1260\\treference machine's versioned envelope, `dao.stream.apply` —\\n  1261\\t`:dao.stream.apply/id`, `/op`, `/args`, `/ok`, `/error` — never the\\n  1262\\tdeleted v1 `dao.stream.apply` vocabulary. Every lower failure names its\\n  1263\\tcleanup obligation: before custody, none; after custody, release\\n  1264\\t(§7.8 step 7).\\n  1265\\t\\n  1266\\t## §7.10 Invariant compliance\\n  1267\\t\\n  1268\\t| Invariant | How UCF honors it |\\n  1269\\t|---|---|\\n  1270\\t| No hidden global state | Every dependency is declared in `:yin.k/requires` — *as computed by the declared fixed point, with its completeness stated* (`:yin.k/discovery`); the store travels as an explicit slice restored into an isolated store; fresh-name state and parked records travel explicitly; custody is facts on a named, possessed medium. |\\n  1271\\t| No implicit control flow | Resume happens only at a safepoint the machine's parking transitions define; the successor chain is explicit `:yin.k/origin` links; the exporting state is an observed data state, never a notification. |\\n  1272\\t| No callbacks | Lift and lower are step functions; custody is observed, never notified; a lapsed holder stops at its own bound; an unanswered proposal is a step status, not a wait. |\\n  1273\\t| No shared mutable state | A UCF value is content-addressed and immutable; occurrence identity separates content variants from the one leased computation; two resumers of one value under `:fork` diverge into distinct incarnations; the isolated execution store shares nothing with the resumer's tasks. |\\n  1274\\t| No layer collapsing | Canonicalize, encode, discover, arbitrate, load, restore, execute are separate functions with data between them. The engine's hot loop is untouched: lifting is a pure function over the blocked machine's value, and the exporting state is that value with an emptied wait-set and a retained record — driver discipline, not engine machinery. |\\n  1275\\t| No assumed graphs | Sharing is an explicit value table keyed by address; cursor aliasing is an explicit cell graph, not inferred from positions; code refs are pcs relative to an addressed vector; nothing is resolved by pointer; foreign engines reconstruct dynamic state from the frame, never from layouts they assume. |\\n  1276\\t\\n  1277\\tAnd the axioms — each claim conditioned on the obligation it depends on: the\\n  1278\\tcontinuation is a stream value (1); its meaning is supplied by the\\n  1279\\tresumer's loader and contract stamp, not carried in it (2); its code and its\\n  1280\\tstate are datoms under `:yin.code/*` and `:yin.k/*` (3); and it pauses,\\n  1281\\ttravels, and resumes anywhere that **holds what it declares, satisfies the\\n  1282\\tdeclared cursor profiles, and obtains custody where the policy demands it**\\n  1283\\t(4) — the unconditional form of that sentence was a claim the unresolved\\n  1284\\tstate closure, custody enforcement, and effect admission had not earned.\\n  1285\\t\\n  1286\\t## §7.11 Acceptance blockers for the implementation phase\\n  1287\\t\\n  1288\\tThe warning at the top of this file is retained until each of these is\\n  1289\\tclosed with a test in the parity or conformance suite. The first four are\\n  1290\\tthe review's named architectural obligations; they are blockers to\\n  1291\\t*accepting the design*, not merely work items.\\n  1292\\t\\n  1293\\t- **Authority grounding and wiring.** The arbitration `dao.space`, its\\n  1294\\t  transactor as grantor, the occurrence ledger with epochs, and the\\n  1295\\t  attribution resolver `dao.lease.md` requires, are composition duties; the\\n  1296\\t  first composition to ship UCF is `yin.repl.core`'s handoff demo, and\\n  1297\\t  its wiring becomes the worked example. A composition without a\\n  1298\\t  transactable space offers `:yin.k/fork` only.\\n  1299\\t- **Execution identity.** Occurrence minting, the `:yin.k/origin` chain,\\n  1300\\t  retry idempotence (one occurrence per park), and successor completion as\\n  1301\\t  a grantor ledger transition need conformance tests, including the crash\\n  1302\\t  windows of §7.7.5.\\n  1303\\t- **Pending-state completeness.** Every pending variant of §7.4.3 must\\n  1304\\t  round-trip and resume *elsewhere*: retained writes, sent and retained FFI\\n  1305\\t  calls with both endpoints and the kept response cursor, and cursor cells\\n  1306\\t  through the aliasing scenarios of `check-wait-set`. This depends on a\\n  1307\\t  **portable cursor profile** in at least one transport, which\\n  1308\\t  `dao.stream.md` leaves TBD — an external dependency UCF inherits and\\n  1309\\t  cannot close itself.\\n  1310\\t- **Enforceable fencing.** Epoch-checked, atomic-with-commitment admission\\n  1311\\t  and durable op-id dedup at consumers: which consumers check, and how a\\n  1312\\t  `dao.space` transactor consults the epoch without a callback, is a design\\n  1313\\t  item for `dao.space.transactor.md`. Until it lands, `:yin.k/exclusive`\\n  1314\\t  custody is at-least-once and must say so.\\n  1315\\t- **Canonical byte encoding.** `:yin.code/hash` and `:yin.k/id` are exactly\\n  1316\\t  as portable as `dao.jing/segment-key`. Canonical CBOR landed in\\n  1317\\t  3ddaa21b (recorded in `yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md` section 5); the\\n  1318\\t  cross-host address checks below still must pass.\\n  1319\\t- **Contract revision publication.** `:yin.code/contract \\\"v2\\\"` has a\\n  1320\\t  published revision history naming the complete execution contract\\n  1321\\t  (§7.3.3): the tuple grammar — mnemonic set, per-mnemonic arity and operand\\n  1322\\t  kinds, saturation/defaults table — the opcode table and transitions, the\\n  1323\\t  resolution and last-value-wins rules, the effect outcome map, and the\\n  1324\\t  scheduler semantics. The index is in `yin.vm.semantic.md` 2.4 and the\\n  1325\\t  history landed in dbae125b (`yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md`); runtime profile\\n  1326\\t  checks below remain open.\\n  1327\\t- **Primitive profile publication.** The standard `yin.vm/primitives`\\n  1328\\t  map published with profiles (§7.5.2), including the `:effectful`\\n  1329\\t  declarations for `yin/def` and `require`; reverse-lookup uniqueness\\n  1330\\t  asserted at `create-vm`.\\n  1331\\t- **Discovery completeness.** The fixed point of §7.6.1 implemented and\\n  1332\\t  tested against the `:store-get`/`:store-put`, dynamic-callee, and\\n  1333\\t  cross-activation-`E` cases; module manifests publishing store footprints\\n  1334\\t  so `:yin.k/discovery :incomplete` disappears.\\n  1335\\t- **Isolated-store lowering.** Conformance tests that the resumed task's\\n  1336\\t  store contains exactly the slice and the resumer's pair, and that a\\n  1337\\t  receiver-side binding of a name in the slice's name set cannot change\\n  1338\\t  resolution.\\n  1339\\t- **Safepoint conformance harness.** A test that runs every corpus segment\\n  1340\\t  on the reference machine to each safepoint of the complete §7.4.1 table —\\n  1341\\t  effect-producing calls included — lifts, lowers into a fresh VM, and\\n  1342\\t  checks the parity suite's result; the same harness is what a foreign\\n  1343\\t  engine runs to claim conformance, including `lift ∘ lower = id`, the\\n  1344\\t  static `stack-effect`/`lexically-required` checks, and the §7.3.4\\n  1345\\t  projection/direct-path image-equality assertion.\\n  1346\\t- **Exporting-state integration.** The lift driver's exporting transition\\n  1347\\t  and its failure/retry paths, tested specifically against the\\n  1348\\t  poll-a-blocked-writer-appends hazard of §7.7.4.\\n  1349\\t\\n  1350\\t### 7.11.1 Blocker-closure acceptance matrix\\n  1351\\t\\n  1352\\tThese are observable test contracts, not a declaration that the blockers\\n  1353\\tare closed. M3 and M4 tests run on JVM, CLJS (Node), and CLJD. A named\\n  1354\\trefusal must be a data outcome before a code image is loaded, a machine is\\n  1355\\trestored, or an effect is committed, as applicable. The M4 lift driver\\n  1356\\tmust consume the UCF table amendments in linker section 11 item 12 before\\n  1357\\tits round-trip tests run. Full UCF acceptance waits for every row,\\n  1358\\tincluding the post-M5 gates; passing a linker milestone alone is not a\\n  1359\\tclaim that all five blockers have closed.\\n  1360\\t\\n  1361\\t**Code identity (7.3; blocker 5).**\\n  1362\\t\\n  1363\\t- Invariant: A resumer executes the instruction stream named by the\\n  1364\\t  continuation's address and contract stamp, or refuses before loading it.\\n  1365\\t- Setup: On each host, make equal resolved vectors from batches with\\n  1366\\t  different entity ids, order, provenance, and omitted defaults; make\\n  1367\\t  unequal vectors by reversing repeated single-valued operands. Include\\n  1368\\t  both direct and projected loads, an index hit, carried code, and a\\n  1369\\t  `dao.jing` miss/hit path. Use a valid vector under a wrong index entry,\\n  1370\\t  a correctly addressed malformed vector, a missing address, an old\\n  1371\\t  stamp, and two valid images claiming one live local id.\\n  1372\\t- Action and expected outcome: M3 `step`/`fetch` transfers and verifies\\n  1373\\t  each format over ring buffers; the two equal vectors address equally,\\n  1374\\t  the unequal vectors do not, and a `:sha256` address still verifies under\\n  1375\\t  its named algorithm. M3 also passes linker section 9's full refusal\\n  1376\\t  matrix, staged-response, invalid-request, and traffic-equivalence\\n  1377\\t  tests without a function or handle on the link stream. M4 lower selects\\n  1378\\t  by stamp then address, verifies hash and grammar, and gives B0-equal\\n  1379\\t  execution on all three hosts.\\n  1380\\t  Wrong index content is linker `:hash-mismatch` at M3 or\\n  1381\\t  `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` at UCF lower; malformed code is\\n  1382\\t  `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the pc; a miss is `:yin.k/unsatisfied`;\\n  1383\\t  an incompatible stamp is `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`. No check may\\n  1384\\t  redirect an existing local id or bypass the common validator.\\n  1385\\t- Landing and order: M3 lands the stream and format-identity gate;\\n  1386\\t  M4 closes UCF identity after M3 and before any lifted image is run.\\n  1387\\t  The already landed `v2` history (dbae125b), canonical CBOR\\n  1388\\t  (3ddaa21b), and Phase 1 vector/stamp (f51077f2) supply the names and\\n  1389\\t  bytes, not the cross-host lowering proof. Landed linker design\\n  1390\\t  (1f7990d5, sections 5 and 9) specifies M2's four format records and\\n  1391\\t  refusal tests; the records must pass those tests before M3 starts.\\n  1392\\t\\n  1393\\t**Safepoint reconstruction (7.4; blocker 1).**\\n  1394\\t\\n  1395\\t- Invariant: At every declared parking transition, lifting and lowering\\n  1396\\t  preserve the next observable step and its wait, while other pcs refuse.\\n  1397\\t- Setup: Run the corpus to every 7.4.1 row: explicit park, blocked\\n  1398\\t  stream read, blocked write, sent FFI, retained FFI, ordinary and tail\\n  1399\\t  effectful calls, and halt; also exercise M4's `:link-request`,\\n  1400\\t  `:link-response`, and `:install` pending variants (linker section 11\\n  1401\\t  item 12). Exercise distinct activation depths and captured environments\\n  1402\\t  at one pc, a nonempty ready queue, a nonsafepoint pc, and a kept response\\n  1403\\t  cursor shared by two waiters. Omit the portable cursor profile for the\\n  1404\\t  refusal case; use a transport that publishes one for the eventual\\n  1405\\t  cross-host positive case.\\n  1406\\t- Action and expected outcome: On each host, lift a parked task and lower\\n  1407\\t  into a fresh VM, then compare its result and effect trace with the\\n  1408\\t  reference machine. For a transport with a portable cursor profile,\\n  1409\\t  repeat with the receiver on another host. Static `:yin.safepoint/kinds`,\\n  1410\\t  stack effect, and lexical reads agree with the reference trace; the\\n  1411\\t  observed parked record, wait entry, and effect outcome determine\\n  1412\\t  `:yin.k/reason` and `:yin.k/pending`, never the static kind alone.\\n  1413\\t  Test both FFI states and each call effect. `lift(lower(frame))` must\\n  1414\\t  reproduce the canonical frame. An undeclared pc gives\\n  1415\\t  `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; queued work gives `:yin.k/not-quiescent`.\\n  1416\\t  Insufficient pending evidence refuses before publication, as does a\\n  1417\\t  missing portable cursor profile. Halt yields a result, not a frame.\\n  1418\\t- Landing and order: M4 covers the table and reference-machine parity,\\n  1419\\t  after code identity; cross-host kept-cursor parity closes in post-M5\\n  1420\\t  hardening if no transport profile lands by M4.\\n  1421\\t  Before export, specify the no-wait explicit-park representation and any\\n  1422\\t  `:call-effect` pending shape; test them or remove that reason from the\\n  1423\\t  wire contract. The `:reasons` Option B ruling in\\n  1424\\t  `yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md` section 8 is landed design, not a runtime\\n  1425\\t  fix: M4 replaces it with static `:kinds` and tests dynamic reasons.\\n  1426\\t  A foreign engine claiming conformance runs the same frame parity harness.\\n  1427\\t\\n  1428\\t**Recursive portable encoding (7.5; blocker 2).**\\n  1429\\t\\n  1430\\t- Invariant: Every reachable portable value returns with its meaning and\\n  1431\\t  aliasing intact, and every unsupported or malformed value refuses as a\\n  1432\\t  whole with a precise path before execution.\\n  1433\\t- Setup: Park frames with nested literal maps that look like UCF tags,\\n  1434\\t  map keys containing values, closures, reified and parked continuations,\\n  1435\\t  profiled primitives, shared subvalues, stream refs, and two cursor refs\\n  1436\\t  that share a cell beside two independent cells at the same position.\\n  1437\\t  Add a host object, unnamed or ambiguous function, host-state primitive,\\n  1438\\t  cyclic value, forged resource ref, missing table ref, cyclic ref data,\\n  1439\\t  extra table entry, and a correctly hashed malformed marker.\\n  1440\\t- Action and expected outcome: Lift, transfer, and lower on each host;\\n  1441\\t  literals remain literals, repeated refs share one value, independent\\n  1442\\t  cursor cells advance independently, and shared cells advance in wait\\n  1443\\t  order. The lift refuses unsupported leaves as `:yin.k/non-portable`\\n  1444\\t  with path and kind and publishes no partial value; decode refuses\\n  1445\\t  malformed markers and refs as `:yin.k/undecodable` with path before\\n  1446\\t  restoring state. A same-named primitive with a different profile is\\n  1447\\t  `:yin.k/unsatisfied`, never substituted by name alone.\\n  1448\\t- Landing and order: M4 supplies the recursive codec, published standard\\n  1449\\t  primitive profiles (including effectful `yin/def` and `require`), and\\n  1450\\t  linker section 11 item 12's `:yin.k/binding`, `:yin.k/store-of`,\\n  1451\\t  private-resource, and sealed-reference variants before lift uses them.\\n  1452\\t  Cross-host kept-cursor round trips require the transport's portable\\n  1453\\t  cursor profile; if none exists at M4, that part closes in post-M5\\n  1454\\t  hardening; the lift refuses it meanwhile. Phase 1 canonicalization and\\n  1455\\t  M2 format records do not encode frames or values.\\n  1456\\t\\n  1457\\t**Ownership arbitration (7.7; blocker 4).**\\n  1458\\t\\n  1459\\t- Invariant: One occurrence has at most one admitted holder and each\\n  1460\\t  protected effect commits at most once across retries and lease changes.\\n  1461\\t- Setup: Copy one checkpoint, including two valid encodings with one\\n  1462\\t  occurrence id, to two readers and leave the source's blocked writer\\n  1463\\t  wakeable. Use a transactable arbitration space, a grantor ledger,\\n  1464\\t  epoch-checking consumer, and durable op-id results. Inject publication\\n  1465\\t  failure, retry, stale lease, consumer partition, and crashes after the\\n  1466\\t  successor append, resumed report, and release, respectively.\\n  1467\\t- Action and expected outcome: M4's exporting transition detaches waits\\n  1468\\t  before publication; polling or direct resume of the exporting source\\n  1469\\t  cannot append, and retry preserves the occurrence. With arbitration,\\n  1470\\t  only a granted holder runs; competitors get `:yin.k/not-holder` or\\n  1471\\t  `:yin.k/awaiting-grant`. Reclaim advances the epoch; stale effects fail\\n  1472\\t  at commitment, duplicate op-ids return the stored result without a\\n  1473\\t  second commit, and the grantor's ledger alone completes the occurrence.\\n  1474\\t  A partitioned protected consumer suspends admission. Without a durable\\n  1475\\t  transactable authority the composition offers `:yin.k/fork` only.\\n  1476\\t- Landing and order: M4 must land and test exporting before any lift\\n  1477\\t  publishes. Exclusive custody, epoch admission, crash recovery, and the\\n  1478\\t  `yin.repl.core` handoff composition close in post-M5 hardening, after\\n  1479\\t  code, state, and pending waits can round-trip. Until then an unfenced\\n  1480\\t  stream declares at-least-once delivery, not exclusive custody.\\n  1481\\t\\n  1482\\t**Dependency closure (7.6; blocker 3).**\\n  1483\\t\\n  1484\\t- Invariant: A resumer runs only when every reachable dependency is\\n  1485\\t  declared and satisfied, using an isolated store that cannot inherit a\\n  1486\\t  receiver task's bindings.\\n  1487\\t- Setup: Make code that reaches names through `:store-get` and\\n  1488\\t  `:store-put`, a dynamically called closure, two activations of one\\n  1489\\t  address with different captured environments, a transitive module\\n  1490\\t  closure, and a parked-id reference. Give the receiver a conflicting\\n  1491\\t  store binding and a different FFI pair. Omit in turn a segment, module\\n  1492\\t  footprint, primitive profile, stream attachment, and parked record.\\n  1493\\t- Action and expected outcome: Lift computes the fixed point over\\n  1494\\t  address-context pairs and the reachable store slice; lowering checks\\n  1495\\t  every requirement before restoring. The resumed task reads the carried\\n  1496\\t  binding, uses the receiver's pair only for future calls, routes pending\\n  1497\\t  calls to their carried endpoints, and shares no task-local module store.\\n  1498\\t  A missing segment reports `:blocked`; an undeclared footprint reports\\n  1499\\t  `:incomplete`; missing requirements yield `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with\\n  1500\\t  the computed discovery state and missing set. A missing active-value\\n  1501\\t  parked ref refuses lift as `:yin.k/non-portable` with\\n  1502\\t  `:yin.k/kind :foreign-parked-ref`. No partial lower occurs.\\n  1503\\t- Landing and order: M4 must test linker obligations, manifest store\\n  1504\\t  footprints, transitive child-install slices, and isolated lowering\\n  1505\\t  after encoding and code identity; linker section 11 criteria 15 and 23\\n  1506\\t  are the minimum module cases. The full UCF fixed point, scheduler\\n  1507\\t  parked records and fresh-name state, and all missing-dependency modes\\n  1508\\t  close in post-M5 hardening. M2 format records verify link payloads;\\n  1509\\t  they do not prove the task's transitive runtime closure.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n23:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_10\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '350,464p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"   350\\t## §7.4 Safepoints — where a canonical configuration exists (blocker 1)\\n   351\\t\\n   352\\t### 7.4.1 Only safepoints lift, and the table is the machine's\\n   353\\t\\n   354\\tThe linear machine's registers are canonical only *between* instructions.\\n   355\\tInside a `:call` that has popped its operands but not yet pushed a frame, or\\n   356\\tinside a superinstruction the loader fused (§6.4), no ⟨C, E, S, K⟩ exists\\n   357\\tthat both describes the state and is expressible in the §2.4 vocabulary.\\n   358\\tUCF resolves this by rule rather than by metadata: **a continuation may be\\n   359\\tlifted only at a safepoint**, and a safepoint is a transition of the\\n   360\\treference machine at which the machine parks. The first draft's table\\n   361\\tpresented itself as complete while omitting the parks that arrive through\\n   362\\t`:call` — a called host function that returns an effect blocks exactly as an\\n   363\\tinstruction effect does, through `apply-call` → `handle-effect` →\\n   364\\t`call-park-entries`, and both ordinary and tail calls reach that path\\n   365\\t(`yin.vm.semantic`, `apply-call`). The complete set:\\n   366\\t\\n   367\\t| Safepoint kind | Raised by | Canonical resume pc | Stack in frame (St) | Accumulator on resume |\\n   368\\t|---|---|---|---|---|\\n   369\\t| explicit park | `:park` | `pc+1` | St at park | the resume value |\\n   370\\t| blocked read | `:stream-next` → `blocked` | `pc+1` | St (cursor ref was in `val`) | the read value; `nil` on `end`, `:dao.stream/gap` on gap |\\n   371\\t| blocked write | `:stream-put` → `full` | `pc+1` | St minus the popped target ref | the written value, on retry `ok` |\\n   372\\t| FFI call, sent | `:ffi-call` → `:dao.stream/ok` | `pc+1` | St minus the popped args | the correlated response's `ok` value; its `error` raises |\\n   373\\t| FFI call, retained | `:ffi-call` → `:dao.stream/full` | `pc+1` | St minus the popped args | as sent, once the request is appended and the response correlates |\\n   374\\t| effectful call | `:call`/`:tailcall` whose operator yields a blocking effect (`require`, stream-module functions, effect handlers) | `pc+1` | St minus the popped operator and args | the effect's resume value, per its kind as above |\\n   375\\t| halt | `:halt`, `:return` with empty K | — | — | not resumable; a `:yin.k/type :yin.k/result` travels instead |\\n   376\\t\\n   377\\tTwo corrections to the first draft's table stand beside the addition.\\n   378\\t`:current-continuation` is **not** a safepoint: it does not park — it\\n   379\\treifies `val ← {seg, pc+1, E, St, K}` and continues, so a reified\\n   380\\tcontinuation is a *value class* of the encoding (§7.5.1), never a lift\\n   381\\tpoint. And `pc+1` as the resume pc is verified sound for every row above:\\n   382\\t§2.6 requires the final pc to be a terminator, so every listed instruction\\n   383\\thas a successor in its own segment.\\n   384\\t\\n   385\\tThe `:yin.k/frame` is exactly the wait-entry shape of `yin.vm.semantic.md`\\n   386\\t§3.5, with segments replaced by addresses and values by their portable\\n   387\\tencodings (§7.5):\\n   388\\t\\n   389\\t```clojure\\n   390\\t:yin.k/frame\\n   391\\t{:yin.k/segment  :segment/sha256-3f1a…\\n   392\\t :yin.k/pc       5                         ; the resume pc, already pc+1\\n   393\\t :yin.k/reason   :next                     ; :park | :next | :put | :ffi | :ffi-request | :call-effect\\n   394\\t :yin.k/val      nil                       ; accumulator; nil except where §7.4.3 pre-fills it\\n   395\\t :yin.k/stack    [ … ]                     ; St per the table above, encoded\\n   396\\t :yin.k/env      { sym → encoded value }   ; E\\n   397\\t :yin.k/k        [ {:yin.k/frame-type :return                    ; or :eval-call / :request-sent,\\n   398\\t                    :yin.k/segment :segment/sha256-…             ; which also carry :yin.k/call-id\\n   399\\t                    :yin.k/pc 5 :yin.k/env {…} :yin.k/stack-base 0} … ]   ; K, innermost last\\n   400\\t :yin.k/pending  {…}}                      ; the wait it was in, §7.4.3 — one variant per reason\\n   401\\t```\\n   402\\t\\n   403\\t**Quiescence.** The migration unit is one task: the whole blocked machine\\n   404\\t(§7.6.3). Lifting additionally requires the machine's ready-queue to be\\n   405\\tempty — a machine with runnable work queued is mid-scheduling, its next\\n   406\\tconfiguration is not the parked one, and lift answers `:yin.k/not-quiescent`.\\n   407\\t\\n   408\\t### 7.4.2 Safepoint maps for foreign engines: static facts only\\n   409\\t\\n   410\\tThe reference machine needs no metadata because its registers *are* the\\n   411\\tcanonical configuration. A foreign engine — a register allocator over the\\n   412\\timage, a WebAssembly lowering, a JIT — keeps its state in physical locations\\n   413\\tof its own choosing, and the first draft promised it reconstruction data\\n   414\\tthat is not a function of the code: absolute stack depth and environment\\n   415\\tkey sets at a pc depend on the *activation* — a closure enters with the\\n   416\\tcaller's residual operand stack and an environment formed from its captured\\n   417\\tenvironment plus arguments (`apply-call`), and §2.6 enforces no equal stack\\n   418\\theights at joins, so the same pc is legitimately reached at many depths.\\n   419\\tNone of that is derivable, and none of it needs to be: **the frame carries\\n   420\\tit.** E, St, and every K frame's `:stack-base` and env travel in the value;\\n   421\\ta foreign engine lowers the canonical frame directly.\\n   422\\t\\n   423\\tWhat *is* a function of the canonical vector alone, and what such an engine\\n   424\\tmust publish — **per segment and per engine profile**, derived from the\\n   425\\tcanonical stream at its own load time — is the static half:\\n   426\\t\\n   427\\t```clojure\\n   428\\t[[sp :yin.safepoint/segment      :segment/sha256-3f1a…]  ; the code, by address\\n   429\\t [sp :yin.safepoint/engine       :wasm32/v1]             ; the engine profile that produced this layout\\n   430\\t [sp :yin.safepoint/pc           5]                      ; canonical resume pc\\n   431\\t [sp :yin.safepoint/stack-effect -1]                     ; Δ|St| across the parking instruction (static)\\n   432\\t [sp :yin.safepoint/lexically-required [x conn]]         ; names this pc can read from E (static)\\n   433\\t [sp :yin.safepoint/layout       {…}]]                   ; engine-owned: physical slot → canonical slot\\n   434\\t```\\n   435\\t\\n   436\\tRules:\\n   437\\t\\n   438\\t- `stack-effect` and `lexically-required` are **derived from the canonical\\n   439\\t  vector alone**, by the same static walk the linearizer performs (§5.3).\\n   440\\t  They are identical for every engine and checkable against the reference\\n   441\\t  machine by a conformance test that runs the segment to each safepoint and\\n   442\\t  compares the observed deltas and E-reads. Absolute depth, activation\\n   443\\t  bases, captured environments, and physical layouts are **not** claimed,\\n   444\\t  not derived, and not in this table; they are the frame's business.\\n   445\\t- `:yin.safepoint/layout` is opaque to the protocol. It is a memo the engine\\n   446\\t  keeps so that its `lift` is a table lookup. It never travels in a UCF\\n   447\\t  value; a resumer that is a different engine has its own.\\n   448\\t- `lift(engine-state, layout) → canonical frame` and\\n   449\\t  `lower(canonical frame, layout) → engine-state` are total on safepoints and\\n   450\\t  undefined elsewhere. The conformance obligation is `lift ∘ lower = id` on\\n   451\\t  canonical frames, tested against the reference machine's own frames —\\n   452\\t  which is possible precisely because the dynamic half rides in the frame.\\n   453\\t- A request to lift at a pc that is not a safepoint of the segment returns\\n   454\\t  `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` naming the pc. An engine that wants finer\\n   455\\t  interruption granularity must lower the program to a segment that\\n   456\\t  *contains* a `:park` where it wants one: that is a different instruction\\n   457\\t  record, a different address, and a different profile. An engine may not\\n   458\\t  invent safepoints a record does not have, and two engines that disagree\\n   459\\t  about the safepoint set of one address are not both conforming.\\n   460\\t\\n   461\\tSafepoints are datoms, so a query can ask \\\"where can this program be\\n   462\\tmigrated?\\\" without running it, and a debugger's breakpoint set is a subset of\\n   463\\tthe safepoint set by construction.\\n   464\\t\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n25:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_11\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '465,538p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"   465\\t### 7.4.3 The pending wait travels as data — complete, and resumable elsewhere\\n   466\\t\\n   467\\tA parked machine was waiting for something. That something is a resource of\\n   468\\tthe emitter's host, and the resumer cannot poll it. `:yin.k/pending` records\\n   469\\tthe wait in resource-independent terms so the resumer can re-establish an\\n   470\\t*equivalent* wait, or decide it cannot. The first draft carried sketches;\\n   471\\tthe set here is exhaustive over §7.4.1's reasons, and each variant carries\\n   472\\teverything a *foreign* resumer needs — including the state the first draft\\n   473\\tleft implicit on the emitter's heap:\\n   474\\t\\n   475\\t```clojure\\n   476\\t;; blocked read (:stream-next)\\n   477\\t{:yin.k/reason   :next\\n   478\\t :yin.k/cell     :yin.k/c-17}          ; the cursor CELL id, §7.5.3 — not a bare position\\n   479\\t\\n   480\\t;; blocked write (:stream-put, or an effectful call's :stream/put)\\n   481\\t{:yin.k/reason   :put\\n   482\\t :yin.k/stream   {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}   ; the target\\n   483\\t :yin.k/value    <encoded>}            ; the retained value — a retry appends THIS, and\\n   484\\t                                       ; without it the wait is not re-establishable\\n   485\\t\\n   486\\t;; FFI call, sent (:ffi-call → ok)\\n   487\\t{:yin.k/reason     :ffi\\n   488\\t :yin.k/call-id    :call-7             ; the :dao.stream.apply/id correlation\\n   489\\t :yin.k/op         :op/add\\n   490\\t :yin.k/request    {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}   ; where the request went\\n   491\\t :yin.k/response   {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}   ; where the answer lands\\n   492\\t :yin.k/cell       :yin.k/c-23         ; the response cursor cell, §7.5.3, at its KEPT position}\\n   493\\t\\n   494\\t;; FFI call, retained (:ffi-call → full): the request is still in hand\\n   495\\t{:yin.k/reason     :ffi-request\\n   496\\t :yin.k/call-id    :call-7\\n   497\\t :yin.k/request-op :op/add\\n   498\\t :yin.k/request-args [<encoded> …]     ; enough to rebuild the envelope verbatim\\n   499\\t :yin.k/request    {identity/descriptor as above}\\n   500\\t :yin.k/response   {identity/descriptor as above}\\n   501\\t :yin.k/cell       :yin.k/c-23}        ; response cell at its kept position\\n   502\\t```\\n   503\\t\\n   504\\tThree rules make these resumable somewhere other than where they were minted:\\n   505\\t\\n   506\\t- **The response position is a kept cursor, never an anchor.** Reattaching\\n   507\\t  at `:oldest` replays responses that belong to other calls; attaching at\\n   508\\t  `:newest` skips past this call's own. The response cell therefore travels\\n   509\\t  at its *kept* position, and the whole task's waiters on that cell travel\\n   510\\t  with it (§7.6.3), so the round-order semantics of `check-wait-set` —\\n   511\\t  waiters sharing a cell consume successive values — are preserved on the\\n   512\\t  resumer exactly. A first poll that observes `gap` is the honest outcome\\n   513\\t  the contract already promises a kept cursor (`dao.stream.md`, *Retention\\n   514\\t  and Gaps*). This requires the response transport to accept a kept cursor\\n   515\\t  from another host, which `dao.stream.md` leaves transport-owned and TBD;\\n   516\\t  UCF therefore declares, per stream, a **portable cursor profile** in\\n   517\\t  `:yin.k/requires` (`:yin.k/cursor-profiles`), and a transport that does\\n   518\\t  not declare one cannot be a pending-response endpoint — the lift refuses\\n   519\\t  it as `:yin.k/unsatisfied` before the value is ever minted.\\n   520\\t- **Outstanding calls route to the emitter's pair; the resumer's pair is its\\n   521\\t  own.** The reference machine restores a `:request-sent` entry into a\\n   522\\t  response wait through its fixed local store keys (`vm/call-out-stream-key`\\n   523\\t  and friends). A resumer must *not* do that: the outstanding call's\\n   524\\t  response will arrive on the **emitter's** response stream, because that is\\n   525\\t  where the request was sent. The resumer re-establishes the wait against\\n   526\\t  the carried response descriptor and the cell's fresh local key (the cell\\n   527\\t  id remapped per §7.5.3), while calls the resumed program makes *after*\\n   528\\t  lowering use the resumer's own pair under its own keys. Pending-call\\n   529\\t  routing and future-call routing are separate stores of state; conflating\\n   530\\t  them strands the migrated call exactly as the review described.\\n   531\\t- **On resume, the resumer attaches and re-mints.** Attach to every\\n   532\\t  descriptor in the frame, pending, and cells; an attachment resolving to\\n   533\\t  `not-found` is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream identity, never a\\n   534\\t  silent `nil`; a `:put` wait retries its retained value and resumes with it\\n   535\\t  on `ok` (the engine, a woken writer's retry appends and stamps the\\n   536\\t  value); a `:next` wait polls its cell. No wait is silently dropped and no\\n   537\\t  retained value is recomputed — what parks is what resumes.\\n   538\\t\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n27:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_12\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '539,649p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"   539\\t## §7.5 Recursive portable encoding (blocker 2)\\n   540\\t\\n   541\\t### 7.5.1 A disjoint tagged grammar\\n   542\\t\\n   543\\tEncoding is one total function `encode : value × path → outcome`, applied\\n   544\\trecursively to every value reachable from the frame: accumulator, operand\\n   545\\tstack, environment values, every frame in K, parked records, and the store\\n   546\\tslice of §7.6.2. The outcome is either the portable form or a refusal naming\\n   547\\tthe path.\\n   548\\t\\n   549\\tThe first draft encoded markers as ordinary maps — `{:yin.k/primitive '+}`,\\n   550\\t`{:yin.k/ref addr}` — while passing program map literals through in their\\n   551\\tnatural shape. That is ambiguous to everyone: §2.5 admits map literals,\\n   552\\tso a program can *contain* `{:yin.k/primitive '+}`, and a decoder cannot tell\\n   553\\tan encoder's marker from a program's data. The grammar here is disjoint by\\n   554\\tconstruction: **every map at an encoded-value position is a UCF marker**, and\\n   555\\ta program's own maps are always wrapped.\\n   556\\t\\n   557\\t| Encoded form | Tag | Contents | Decodes to |\\n   558\\t|---|---|---|---|\\n   559\\t| scalars: nil, boolean, number, string, keyword, symbol | — | themselves (never a map; cannot forge a marker) | themselves |\\n   560\\t| vector, list, set | — | same shape, elements recursively encoded (never a map) | same shape, elements decoded |\\n   561\\t| literal map | `:yin.k/literal` | `:yin.k/entries [k₁ v₁ k₂ v₂ …]`, keys and values recursively encoded | the map, keys and values decoded |\\n   562\\t| closure | `:yin.k/closure` | `:yin.k/segment addr :yin.k/entry pc :yin.k/params […] :yin.k/env {encoded}` | `{:type :closure …}` (§2.4) |\\n   563\\t| reified continuation | `:yin.k/frame` | a nested frame (§7.4.1), recursively | `{:type :reified-continuation …}` |\\n   564\\t| parked continuation | `:yin.k/frame` | a nested frame plus `:yin.k/parked-id` | `{:type :parked-continuation …}` |\\n   565\\t| primitive | `:yin.k/primitive` | `:yin.k/name sym` | the named function, after profile check (§7.5.2) |\\n   566\\t| stream reference | `:yin.k/stream` | `:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …` | an attached handle, under a fresh store key |\\n   567\\t| cursor reference | `:yin.k/cursor-ref` | `:yin.k/cell cell-id` | a store cursor entry for that cell, §7.5.3 |\\n   568\\t| shared subvalue | `:yin.k/ref` | `:yin.k/id addr` | the table entry at `addr`, §7.5.3 |\\n   569\\t| anything else | — | — | **refused**: `:yin.k/non-portable` |\\n   570\\t\\n   571\\tWhy the wrapping is total rather than an \\\"escaping rule\\\" for suspicious\\n   572\\tshapes: a literal map is wrapped *wherever it occurs and whatever it\\n   573\\tcontains* — including one that happens to read `{:yin.k/tag …}` — and the\\n   574\\tentries inside a wrapper are themselves encoded, so a forged marker inside a\\n   575\\tliteral is re-wrapped one level down and peeled back exactly one level on\\n   576\\tdecode. No rule asks whether a map \\\"looks like\\\" data; no decoder decision\\n   577\\tdepends on program content. Scalar and collection values pass through only\\n   578\\tbecause no marker is ever a non-map, which the closed tag table fixes.\\n   579\\t\\n   580\\tMap keys are values and are encoded as such — inside `:yin.k/entries`\\n   581\\talternately with their values, and nowhere else; the frame's own `:yin.k/env`\\n   582\\tkeys are the program's symbols and are structural, not encoded values.\\n   583\\t\\n   584\\t**Decoding validates, independent of hashing.** A hash check confirms what\\n   585\\twas encoded; it does not confirm the encoding is well formed. The decoder\\n   586\\twalks the grammar and rejects, as `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the path: a map\\n   587\\twith no `:yin.k/tag` at an encoded-value position (impossible from a\\n   588\\tconforming encoder); a tag outside the closed set; a marker missing its\\n   589\\trequired keys; a `:yin.k/ref` whose address is absent from the value table; a\\n   590\\tref graph that is cyclic *through ref data* (content-addressed table entries\\n   591\\tcannot mint such a cycle — a cycle can only be written by hand, and it is\\n   592\\trejected); and a value table entry not referenced by anything reachable.\\n   593\\tMalformed-but-correctly-hashed input fails here, before any lowering.\\n   594\\t\\n   595\\t### 7.5.2 Primitives: a name is a binding, not a meaning\\n   596\\t\\n   597\\t§1.1 admits host functions into the environment because `:var` resolves a\\n   598\\tprimitive symbol to the function value and a program may then bind it\\n   599\\t(`(def f +)`). At lift time the emitter recognizes such a value by reverse\\n   600\\tlookup in its own `:primitives` map — identity on the function object. The\\n   601\\tfirst draft treated the recovered symbol as the whole story. It is not:\\n   602\\t\\n   603\\t- one function object may sit under **several names**, making reverse lookup\\n   604\\t  ambiguous;\\n   605\\t- two composition-supplied primitive maps may bind the **same symbol to\\n   606\\t  different functions** — name presence at the resumer proves nothing about\\n   607\\t  behavior;\\n   608\\t- identity stability says nothing about **purity or captured host state**.\\n   609\\t\\n   610\\tUCF therefore binds every portable function to a **primitive profile**,\\n   611\\tpublished data, and the binding is checked on both ends:\\n   612\\t\\n   613\\t```clojure\\n   614\\t;; in :yin.k/requires — computed, not hand-written (§7.6.1)\\n   615\\t:yin.k/primitives {+\\n   616\\t                   {:yin.k/profile :yin.k.pp/sha256-…   ; content address of the profile record\\n   617\\t                    :yin.k/class    :pure               ; :pure | :effectful | :host\\n   618\\t                    :yin.k/arities  [2]\\n   619\\t                    :yin.k/effects  #{}                 ; for :effectful — declared effect kinds\\n   620\\t                    :yin.k/host-state :none}}           ; :none, or refused\\n   621\\t```\\n   622\\t\\n   623\\tThe profile record — `{name class arities effects host-state}` under a\\n   624\\tcontent address — is what a name must be *checked against*, not merely\\n   625\\tpresent as. Rules:\\n   626\\t\\n   627\\t- **Ambiguity refuses the lift.** A function object that reverse-lookups to\\n   628\\t  more than one name in the emitter's `:primitives` is refused\\n   629\\t  (`:yin.k/non-portable`, kind `:ambiguous-primitive`) unless the\\n   630\\t  composition declares a canonical-name table resolving it to exactly one.\\n   631\\t- **A host function under no name is refused** (kind `:unnamed-function`) —\\n   632\\t  a bridge handler's return value, a module-constructed function: it has no\\n   633\\t  declared semantics to travel under.\\n   634\\t- **`:host`-class functions are refused** (kind `:host-state-primitive`). A\\n   635\\t  function whose behavior depends on undeclared host state is not a value\\n   636\\t  any other host can honor; profiles that declare host state may not be\\n   637\\t  lifted, and a profile claiming `:host-state :none` is a publication's\\n   638\\t  warranty, versioned with the contract stamp's primitive-profile registry.\\n   639\\t- **On lowering, the resumer's binding is checked by profile, not by\\n   640\\t  presence.** For each required name, the resumer must hold a primitive\\n   641\\t  whose profile address is equal; a same-named function of different profile\\n   642\\t  is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the symbol and the expected profile — it is\\n   643\\t  a routing decision for whoever chose this resumer, not a silent\\n   644\\t  substitution.\\n   645\\t- The standard map `yin.vm/primitives` must be published with profiles\\n   646\\t  (note `yin/def` is `:effectful` with effect `:vm/store-put`, and `require`\\n   647\\t  is `:effectful` with `:module/require`); that publication is an acceptance\\n   648\\t  blocker (§7.11).\\n   649\\t\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n29:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_13\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '650,739p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"   650\\t### 7.5.3 Sharing, cells, and cycles\\n   651\\t\\n   652\\t**The value table.** A configuration is a DAG, not a tree: a closure's\\n   653\\tcaptured environment is the frame's environment; a value on the stack is\\n   654\\talso bound in `E`. Encoding naively duplicates, and a deep K makes\\n   655\\tduplication quadratic. The encoder therefore builds a **value table**:\\n   656\\t\\n   657\\t- On encountering a collection, closure, or continuation it has already\\n   658\\t  encoded (host identity during one lift), it emits\\n   659\\t  `{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/ref :yin.k/id addr}` where `addr` is the `dao.jing`\\n   660\\t  address of the encoded subvalue, and places the subvalue in\\n   661\\t  `:yin.k/values {addr → encoded}`.\\n   662\\t- A subvalue is placed in the table when it is referenced more than once or\\n   663\\t  its encoded size exceeds a composition-supplied threshold; otherwise it\\n   664\\t  stays inline. Both choices yield equal semantics; only the table is needed\\n   665\\t  for correctness of sharing.\\n   666\\t- A cycle (a value reachable from itself through host identity) is refused\\n   667\\t  as `:yin.k/non-portable` with `:yin.k/kind :cyclic` naming the path. The\\n   668\\t  reference machine cannot produce one — `E` is extended by `merge`, never\\n   669\\t  mutated — so the refusal guards against foreign engines and bridge-returned\\n   670\\t  host structures. Cycles *through ref data* cannot be minted at all and are\\n   671\\t  rejected at decode (§7.5.1); this single representation retires the first\\n   672\\t  draft's two competing ones.\\n   673\\t\\n   674\\tBecause table entries are content-addressed, a carrier that already holds an\\n   675\\tentry (a `dao.jing` intake pool, a DHT replica) need not carry it again, and\\n   676\\ttwo continuations parked from the same closure share its encoding. This is\\n   677\\t`streams-all-the-way-down.md` §6.3's checkpoint-as-published-fold applied to\\n   678\\ta single frame. It is also why §7.3.4 separates `:yin.k/id` (which varies\\n   679\\twith these choices, harmlessly) from `:yin.k/occurrence` (which does not).\\n   680\\t\\n   681\\t**Cursor cells.** A cursor is *observable state with aliasing*: two distinct\\n   682\\tcursor refs may sit at the same position and yet be independently\\n   683\\tadvanceable store entries, while repeated refs to one cursor ref must\\n   684\\tadvance together — `check-wait-set` advances a shared cursor ref before\\n   685\\tpolling the next waiter, so waiters on one ref read successive values, and\\n   686\\twaiters on two refs at the same position read the same next value\\n   687\\t(`yin.vm.engine`, `check-wait-set`). A position-only encoding erases\\n   688\\texactly this distinction: two cells at one position would collapse, and\\n   689\\ttheir independent advancement would be lost. The encoding therefore carries\\n   690\\t**logical cursor cells** separately from position content:\\n   691\\t\\n   692\\t```clojure\\n   693\\t:yin.k/cells {:yin.k/c-17 {:yin.k/stream   {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/stream …}\\n   694\\t                           :yin.k/position <opaque, exactly as the transport minted it>}}\\n   695\\t;; values reference cells, never positions:\\n   696\\t{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/cursor-ref :yin.k/cell :yin.k/c-17}\\n   697\\t```\\n   698\\t\\n   699\\tCell ids are minted by the emitter (stable within one lift; remapped on\\n   700\\tlowering) and are *not* content-addressed — a cell is a local identity, like\\n   701\\ta tempid, and deduplicating cells by their contents is precisely the aliasing\\n   702\\terasure this structure exists to prevent. On lowering, each cell becomes a\\n   703\\tfresh store cursor entry seeded with its carried position; every\\n   704\\t`:yin.k/cursor-ref` to it remaps to that entry; two refs to one cell share\\n   705\\tone entry and one ref per cell keeps its own. The FFI response cell of\\n   706\\t§7.4.3 is a cell like any other.\\n   707\\t\\n   708\\tA stream reference is portable iff its transport produces a descriptor whose\\n   709\\t`attach!` can succeed somewhere other than the emitter's host. The encoder\\n   710\\tcannot know that; it encodes the descriptor and lets the resumer's `attach!`\\n   711\\treport `not-found` as data. What the encoder *can* refuse is a handle whose\\n   712\\t`descriptor` call fails, which cannot happen under the contract, and a raw\\n   713\\tin-memory handle admitted by an in-process stream's admission declaration\\n   714\\t(`dao.stream.md`, *Creation and Attachment*), which it treats as a host\\n   715\\tobject.\\n   716\\t\\n   717\\t### 7.5.4 Failure is total and names its place\\n   718\\t\\n   719\\tEncoding a continuation with one refused leaf refuses the whole\\n   720\\tcontinuation:\\n   721\\t\\n   722\\t```clojure\\n   723\\t{:yin.k/status :yin.k/non-portable\\n   724\\t :yin.k/path   [:yin.k/env 'conn]         ; where in the frame\\n   725\\t :yin.k/kind   :host-object               ; the closed kind set below\\n   726\\t :yin.k/hint   \\\"java.net.Socket\\\"}         ; classified, never the object\\n   727\\t```\\n   728\\t\\n   729\\tThe kind set is closed:\\n   730\\t`:host-object | :unnamed-function | :ambiguous-primitive | :host-state-primitive\\n   731\\t| :cyclic | :in-memory-handle | :non-canonicalizable | :foreign-parked-ref`\\n   732\\t(the last two from §7.3.2 and §7.6.3). There is no partial UCF value and no\\n   733\\tplaceholder for a missing leaf. A program that wishes to be migratable keeps\\n   734\\thost resources behind stream descriptors and named, profiled primitives,\\n   735\\twhich is the same discipline the host boundary rules already impose on\\n   736\\tportable code (`datom.world.md`, *Host Boundaries*). The refusal is the\\n   737\\temitter's outcome, returned as data to whatever asked it to lift; the\\n   738\\tmachine itself is unaffected and remains parked locally.\\n   739\\t\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n30:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_14\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"The draft makes several claims conditional on work still open: cross-host cursor recovery, dependency discovery, and effect admission. I’m checking those against the linker phase gates and the revision rulings before assigning verdicts.\"}}\n32:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_15\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '849,921p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"   849\\t### 7.6.3 The migration unit is one task\\n   850\\t\\n   851\\tThe reference machine's scheduler state does not live in the store, and the\\n   852\\tfirst draft transported none of it. Both omissions are defects: the\\n   853\\tmachine's fresh identifiers come from `:id-counter`, and `:resume` resolves\\n   854\\tits operand against the `:parked` map — a fresh receiver could reuse an\\n   855\\texisting generated identifier, or fail to resolve a parked continuation a\\n   856\\tmigrated segment is about to name (`engine/park-continuation`,\\n   857\\t`engine/resume-continuation`, §3.5). UCF therefore states the unit and\\n   858\\tcarries the state:\\n   859\\t\\n   860\\t- **The unit is the whole blocked machine**: registers (the frame), the\\n   861\\t  wait-set (as pending variants, §7.4.3), the referenced `:parked` records,\\n   862\\t  and the fresh-name counter. The ready-queue must be empty at lift\\n   863\\t  (`:yin.k/not-quiescent` otherwise, §7.4.1) — a task with queued runnable\\n   864\\t  work has a next configuration that is not the parked one.\\n   865\\t- **Fresh-name state travels.** `:yin.k/scheduler {:yin.k/id-counter n\\n   866\\t  …}` carries the counter verbatim; the lowered machine adopts\\n   867\\t  `max(local, carried)` so generated ids never repeat, and ids already\\n   868\\t  minted (gensym results in env, stack, or store) travel as ordinary values,\\n   869\\t  unaffected by the counter.\\n   870\\t- **Referenced parked records travel.** The fixed point\\n   871\\t  of §7.6.1 collects every `:resume` operand in reachable code and every\\n   872\\t  `[:parked id]` value from reachable environments. Each must resolve\\n   873\\t  within this task to a parked record carried under\\n   874\\t  `:yin.k/scheduler :yin.k/parked {pid → frame}`. A missing id referenced\\n   875\\t  as a code operand is reported as an unsupplied requirement\\n   876\\t  (`:yin.k/unsatisfied`). A missing id referenced by an active value\\n   877\\t  refuses the lift (`:yin.k/non-portable`, kind `:foreign-parked-ref`),\\n   878\\t  as it represents corrupted state. Parked records not named by the\\n   879\\t  reachable graph do not travel.\\n   880\\t\\n   881\\t### 7.6.4 Modules\\n   882\\t\\n   883\\t`:yin.k/modules` names each module by the content address of its published\\n   884\\tmanifest, on the model of `dao.space.index/publish-index!` (`dao.jing.md`,\\n   885\\t*Publication from an agent*). A module is satisfied when the resumer holds a\\n   886\\tmodule registry entry (`yin.vm.module` — a value, supplied by the\\n   887\\tcomposition) whose manifest address is equal; it is not satisfied by a\\n   888\\tsame-named module of different content. Module *code* is segments and is\\n   889\\tcovered by `:yin.k/segments` and the manifest's own addressing; the manifest\\n   890\\tpins the effect handlers, the exported primitive profiles (§7.5.2), and —\\n   891\\tper §7.6.1 — the store footprint the fixed point needs to claim\\n   892\\t`:complete`.\\n   893\\t\\n   894\\t### 7.6.5 The satisfaction check\\n   895\\t\\n   896\\tBefore lowering, the resumer evaluates `:yin.k/requires` against what it\\n   897\\tholds and answers with one outcome:\\n   898\\t\\n   899\\t```clojure\\n   900\\t{:yin.k/status    :yin.k/unsatisfied\\n   901\\t :yin.k/discovery :incomplete                    ; or :blocked, §7.6.1 — the computed status of the closure itself\\n   902\\t :yin.k/missing   {:yin.k/segments       #{:segment/sha256-9c02…}\\n   903\\t                   :yin.k/primitives     {println <expected profile>}\\n   904\\t                   :yin.k/modules        {my.mod …}\\n   905\\t                   :yin.k/streams        #{<identity>}\\n   906\\t                   :yin.k/cursor-profiles #{:dao.stream/file-v1}}}  ; keys present only where something is missing\\n   907\\t```\\n   908\\t\\n   909\\tThe check reports everything missing in one pass *that the fixed point could\\n   910\\tsee*, and says which kind of pass it was: `:complete` (the closure is fully\\n   911\\tcomputed; the missing list is exhaustive), `:incomplete` (a module footprint\\n   912\\twas undeclared; the missing list is a lower bound), or `:blocked` (a missing\\n   913\\tsegment stopped discovery; satisfying `:yin.k/segments` and re-running the\\n   914\\tcheck is the only way forward). A missing segment may be resolved by\\n   915\\t`dao.jing` fetch (§7.3.4); a missing primitive, module, or cursor profile\\n   916\\tcannot be resolved by the resumer and is a routing decision for whoever\\n   917\\tchose this resumer. A resumer never lowers a partially satisfied value; a\\n   918\\thost with no implementation for something answers with the qualified\\n   919\\tunsupported outcome, which `datom.world.md` calls a correct outcome, not a\\n   920\\tgap to be filled.\\n   921\\t\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n34:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_16\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '740,848p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"   740\\t## §7.6 Dependency closure and context (blocker 3)\\n   741\\t\\n   742\\t### 7.6.1 Declared, not hand-written — and discovered conservatively\\n   743\\t\\n   744\\tA resumer must be able to decide *before lowering* whether it can run the\\n   745\\tvalue. `:yin.k/requires` carries that decision's inputs, and every set in it\\n   746\\tis **computed by a conservative fixed point**, not hand-written and not\\n   747\\tguessed from the current environment:\\n   748\\t\\n   749\\t```clojure\\n   750\\t:yin.k/requires\\n   751\\t{:yin.k/segments        #{:segment/sha256-3f1a… :segment/sha256-9c02…}  ; every address in frame, k, closures, values, parked\\n   752\\t :yin.k/primitives      {sym → profile, §7.5.2}                        ; every {:yin.k/primitive …} encoded, plus every free :var\\n   753\\t :yin.k/modules         {my.mod :segment/sha256-…}                     ; module name → manifest address\\n   754\\t :yin.k/effects         #{:stream/next :stream/put :module/require}    ; effect kinds the segments can raise\\n   755\\t :yin.k/ffi-ops         #{:op/add}                                     ; :yin.code/ffi-op values present\\n   756\\t :yin.k/streams         #{<identity> …}                                ; logical-stream identities referenced anywhere\\n   757\\t :yin.k/cursor-profiles #{:dao.stream/file-v1}                         ; transports that must accept kept cursors, §7.4.3\\n   758\\t :yin.k/discovery       :complete}                                     ; :complete | :incomplete | :blocked — computed, §7.6.5\\n   759\\t```\\n   760\\t\\n   761\\tThe fixed point starts from the frame, its K frames, the parked records of\\n   762\\t§7.6.3, the store slice, and the pending wait. Its unit of analysis is a work\\n   763\\titem `[code-address context]`, not an address: `context` is the finite\\n   764\\tabstraction of the captured environment with which a closure enters that\\n   765\\tcode. Code is fetched, validated, and projected once per address, but every\\n   766\\tnewly discovered address-context pair is analyzed. Two closures over the\\n   767\\tsame address with different captured environments are therefore distinct\\n   768\\twork items. A full pass repeats whenever it adds a work item or any dependency\\n   769\\tfact, and convergence is reached only when a full pass adds neither:\\n   770\\t\\n   771\\t- **Values:** every encoded closure contributes its segment address and its\\n   772\\t  captured env's values and the work item\\n   773\\t  `[segment-address captured-env-context]`; every stream and cursor marker\\n   774\\t  contributes a stream identity; every `:yin.k/primitive` marker contributes\\n   775\\t  a name. A captured environment contributes reachable values, never name\\n   776\\t  discharge.\\n   777\\t- **Code:** every reachable segment's instructions are walked — as datoms\\n   778\\t  where the emitter holds the batch, as canonical tuples where only a vector\\n   779\\t  was fetched (§7.3.4) — including **`:store-get` and `:store-put`\\n   780\\t  operands**, which name store\\n   781\\t  keys directly and which the first draft missed entirely; every `:var`\\n   782\\t  name; every effect op (`:stream-*`, `:ffi-call`, and the effects of\\n   783\\t  `:effectful`-class primitives the walk finds); every `:resume` operand\\n   784\\t  (a parked id, §7.6.3). Discovering a new work item continues the fixed point\\n   785\\t  with that address and context even when the address was already walked for\\n   786\\t  another context.\\n   787\\t- **Names:** only a free `:var` name is an obligation. An obligation is\\n   788\\t  discharged by a matching key in the reachable store slice, or retained as\\n   789\\t  a required primitive or module export and checked by profile (§7.5.2). An\\n   790\\t  environment never discharges a name: captured environments contribute\\n   791\\t  values to their work item's reachability analysis, while lexical binding\\n   792\\t  determines whether a `:var` is free before it becomes an obligation. If no\\n   793\\t  store key or profiled primitive/module requirement can discharge an\\n   794\\t  obligation, discovery is `:incomplete`; after lowering, an unavailable\\n   795\\t  retained requirement is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` (§7.6.5).\\n   796\\t- **Modules:** a module's manifest declares its exported primitive profiles\\n   797\\t  and its **store footprint** (store keys and effect kinds its handlers may\\n   798\\t  touch). Until manifests carry footprints, any required module whose\\n   799\\t  footprint is undeclared sets `:yin.k/discovery :incomplete` — the honest\\n   800\\t  statement that closure could not be computed, never a silent\\n   801\\t  under-approximation. A module effect handler may read store keys the\\n   802\\t  static walk cannot see; a composition may supply extra slice keys\\n   803\\t  explicitly, and the value records that it did.\\n   804\\t\\n   805\\tBecause the computation is over data, the same query answers \\\"what does this\\n   806\\tprogram need?\\\" for a segment that has never run. And because discovery can\\n   807\\t*fail to terminate successfully* — a missing segment cannot be walked, an\\n   808\\tundeclared footprint cannot be closed — the result distinguishes\\n   809\\t`:complete` from `:incomplete` from `:blocked` (§7.6.5); a fixed point that\\n   810\\tcould not finish is reported as such, not passed off as a satisfied closure.\\n   811\\t\\n   812\\t### 7.6.2 The store slice, restored as an isolated store\\n   813\\t\\n   814\\tThe store S holds `def` results, stream handles, cursor entries, and the FFI\\n   815\\tpair (§4.1). None of it is global in the UCF sense; it is the emitter's. A\\n   816\\tcontinuation carries the **reachable slice**: every store key the fixed\\n   817\\tpoint of §7.6.1 can name — every free `:var` obligation for which the\\n   818\\temitter's store has a matching key, every `:store-get`/`:store-put` operand,\\n   819\\tevery stream/cursor cell — with values encoded by §7.5:\\n   820\\t\\n   821\\t```clojure\\n   822\\t:yin.k/store {counter 41\\n   823\\t              'log {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/stream …}}\\n   824\\t```\\n   825\\t\\n   826\\tThe FFI pair is never in the slice — a program that names the pair's store\\n   827\\tkeys directly holds handles, and the lift refuses it (§7.5.4). A resumer has\\n   828\\tits own pair; outstanding calls route per §7.4.3.\\n   829\\t\\n   830\\t**There is no merge.** The first draft said, in two places, both that the\\n   831\\tslice wins on collision and that the slice merges *under* the local store —\\n   832\\ta contradiction, and beneath it a real defect: resolution is env → store →\\n   833\\tprimitives (`resolve-var`), so a resumer whose local store happens to bind\\n   834\\t`+` would change what the migrated program resolves, without any collision\\n   835\\tat all. Merging in either order is the wrong shape. **The resumed task runs\\n   836\\tin an isolated execution store**: a fresh store whose contents are exactly\\n   837\\tthe carried slice, the remapped cells of §7.5.3, and the resumer's FFI pair\\n   838\\t— nothing else of the resumer's, nothing of any other task's. A name absent\\n   839\\tfrom the slice resolves through primitives and modules at the resumer\\n   840\\texactly as it did at the emitter, because there is no third store to\\n   841\\tintervene; a slice key cannot collide with a receiver task's key because no\\n   842\\treceiver key participates; resource entries are remapped to fresh local\\n   843\\tkeys (cursor cells per §7.5.3, stream handles per §7.4.3), and non-resource\\n   844\\tentries keep their keys verbatim. \\\"Unreachable store entries do not travel\\\"\\n   845\\tsurvives unchanged — anything the program did not observe is not the\\n   846\\tprogram's state — and nothing else shares state with the resumed task by\\n   847\\taccident.\\n   848\\t\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n40:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_19\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '1069,1148p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"  1069\\t### 7.7.5 Fencing effects: epochs, operation ids, and what exactly-once costs\\n  1070\\t\\n  1071\\t`dao.lease.md` states it plainly: **the absence of a `:lapsed` fact is\\n  1072\\tnever evidence of tenure.** The first draft's fencing — stamp effects with\\n  1073\\tthe lease id, let consumers consult the medium and drop effects whose\\n  1074\\tincarnation has a `:lapsed` record — fails in every direction the review\\n  1075\\tnamed: a partitioned consumer accepts stale effects (it cannot see the\\n  1076\\tlapse); a delayed legitimate effect is discarded after release; repeated\\n  1077\\teffects within one lease share one incarnation and are indistinguishable; a\\n  1078\\treplacement holder repeats an effect its predecessor committed; and apply\\n  1079\\tids correlate requests with responses, they do not suppress duplicates.\\n  1080\\tLapse-record filtering is correlation, not fencing. What UCF requires\\n  1081\\tinstead:\\n  1082\\t\\n  1083\\t**Authority epochs, checked atomically with commitment.** The occurrence's\\n  1084\\tledger carries an epoch datom, advanced by the grantor on every reclaim\\n  1085\\t(§7.7.3) — a monotone number the possessing boundary alone can write. A\\n  1086\\tconsumer that must be exactly-once (a `dao.space` transactor, an FFI callee\\n  1087\\twith side effects) admits a fenced effect only in the same act that reads\\n  1088\\tthe epoch: the check and the commit are one transition against the\\n  1089\\tadmission resource, never check-then-act. An effect whose incarnation's\\n  1090\\tepoch is not the current one is refused at commitment. This is\\n  1091\\t`dao.lease.md`'s \\\"fencing from the resource\\\" for durable resources, and it\\n  1092\\tis a composition duty that composition owes exactly once.\\n  1093\\t\\n  1094\\t**Stable operation ids, and durable dedup.** Every fenced effect carries\\n  1095\\t`:yin.k/op-id {:yin.k/occurrence O :yin.k/seq n}` — assigned by the fenced\\n  1096\\twriter in emission order. Op-ids are deterministic functions of the\\n  1097\\tcheckpoint and the operation sequence: a replacement holder resuming the\\n  1098\\t*same* occurrence replays the same op-ids, which is what makes\\n  1099\\tacross-incarnation dedup possible at all. Exactly-once consumers keep a\\n  1100\\tdurable `{op-id → committed result}` record, written in the same atomic\\n  1101\\ttransition as the epoch check; a replayed op-id returns the recorded result\\n  1102\\tand commits nothing. Within one incarnation and across incarnations, the\\n  1103\\tsame op-id is the same logical operation — the room for exactly-once\\n  1104\\tsemantics that lease identity alone cannot provide.\\n  1105\\t\\n  1106\\t**Where the stamp rides.** Program values are program data; UCF never\\n  1107\\tedits them. A resumed incarnation running under a lease emits through a\\n  1108\\t**fenced writer** the composition wires: a writer that wraps each appended\\n  1109\\tvalue in an envelope `{:yin.k/envelope … :yin.k/incarnation <lease>\\n  1110\\t:yin.k/op-id … :yin.k/value <the program value, verbatim>}`. Exactly-once\\n  1111\\tconsumers read stamped envelopes; a stream wired bare — no fence — is\\n  1112\\t*declared* unprotected, and a composition that needs exactly-once delivery\\n  1113\\tthrough it has wired the wrong thing. Fencing is opt-in per stream, exactly\\n  1114\\tas `dao.lease.md` places the burden: \\\"a holder needing exclusion obtains it\\n  1115\\tfrom the resource.\\\" No callback tells a lapsed holder to stop; it stops at\\n  1116\\tits own lease bound (`dao.lease.md`, *The holder*), and what it did past\\n  1117\\tthe bound is distinguishable after the fact by epoch and op-id.\\n  1118\\t\\n  1119\\t**Durable completion of a checkpoint.** Publication of a successor is not\\n  1120\\tcompletion. The holder's exit sequence is: append the successor value to\\n  1121\\tthe carrier, append `:yin.k/resumed` (evidence) to the arbitration medium,\\n  1122\\tappend `:dao.lease/released`. Completion is the **grantor's ledger\\n  1123\\ttransition** — observing the release (or the `:resumed` evidence followed\\n  1124\\tby release) and closing the occurrence's tenure. The crash windows are then\\n  1125\\tbounded and harmless: a crash after the successor append but before the\\n  1126\\trecords leaves an orphan successor — content-addressed, idempotent,\\n  1127\\tre-readable as evidence but granting nothing; the lease lapses by silence;\\n  1128\\tthe grantor re-grants the *last recorded* occurrence, and the orphan chain\\n  1129\\tis queryable history (§7.7.6). A crash mid-sequence never requires the\\n  1130\\tgrantor to trust a holder's report as proof of durable completion, because\\n  1131\\tthe report is evidence and the ledger is authority.\\n  1132\\t\\n  1133\\t**Partition policy, stated honestly.** A fenced consumer that cannot reach\\n  1134\\tthe authority (epoch unreadable) must **suspend protected effect\\n  1135\\tcommitment** — fail closed — or the composition has chosen at-least-once\\n  1136\\tdelivery with after-the-fact dedup reconciliation; which one is a declared\\n  1137\\tproperty of the consumer, and a consumer that neither checks nor declares\\n  1138\\tis not exactly-once and may not claim to be. A holder partitioned from the\\n  1139\\tarbitration medium runs to its own lease bound and its effects are\\n  1140\\tafter-the-fact distinguishable, which is the guarantee `dao.lease.md`\\n  1141\\tactually makes; UCF does not promise more. A grantor that lost its ledger\\n  1142\\treclaims and re-grants (`dao.lease.md`, *Restart*); **permanent** authority\\n  1143\\tloss breaks exclusive custody irreparably — the ledger was the resource —\\n  1144\\tand repair is a governance act outside this protocol. The arbitration\\n  1145\\tmedium's durability is therefore a composition duty owed before\\n  1146\\t`:yin.k/exclusive` is offered at all (`dao.lease.md`, *Composition\\n  1147\\tduties*: a judge whose durability matches the resource).\\n  1148\\t\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n42:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_20\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '1149,1236p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"  1149\\t### 7.7.6 Successors and chains\\n  1150\\t\\n  1151\\tA resumed continuation runs to its next safepoint and either halts or parks\\n  1152\\tagain. Either way it produces a successor value — a `:yin.k/continuation`\\n  1153\\tor a `:yin.k/result` — whose `:yin.k/origin` carries the predecessor's\\n  1154\\t`:yin.k/occurrence` and the lease it ran under. Each park is a **new\\n  1155\\toccurrence**: minted fresh by the running holder, chained to its\\n  1156\\tpredecessor, and itself the subject of the next custody cycle. The holder\\n  1157\\tappends the successor, records `:yin.k/resumed` with the successor's\\n  1158\\taddress, and releases. The chain of occurrences linked by `:yin.k/origin`\\n  1159\\tis the migratory computation's history, queryable on the arbitration\\n  1160\\tmedium: where it ran, under which grant, what it became. A rollback is a\\n  1161\\tre-offer of an earlier link; effects past that link need compensation, not\\n  1162\\trecomputation (`streams-all-the-way-down.md` §6.4) — and §7.7.5's op-ids\\n  1163\\tare what compensation reads to find what was done.\\n  1164\\t\\n  1165\\t### 7.7.7 Restart\\n  1166\\t\\n  1167\\tA grantor that lost its ledger reclaims and re-grants (`dao.lease.md`,\\n  1168\\t*Restart*: reclaim first, then grant afresh — a new grant alone does not\\n  1169\\tend the prior tenure). Its inventory of possessed resources is the set of\\n  1170\\toccurrence ledgers it transacts; its recoverable holder for each is the\\n  1171\\t`:dao.lease/holder` of the last grant, both of which are facts on (or the\\n  1172\\trecoverable state of) the space it possesses. Both are queries, which is\\n  1173\\twhy custody lives on a `dao.space` and not on the carrier stream.\\n  1174\\t\\n  1175\\t## §7.8 Lifecycle: lift on park, lower on resume\\n  1176\\t\\n  1177\\t**Lift** (emitter side; composition-driven, never automatic on every park):\\n  1178\\t\\n  1179\\t1. The machine is parked or blocked with a local record `r`, and its\\n  1180\\t   ready-queue is empty, else `:yin.k/not-quiescent`.\\n  1181\\t2. **Enter exporting** (§7.7.4): detach the wait set into the export\\n  1182\\t   record, neutralize direct `:resume`. From here the source is fenced; no\\n  1183\\t   poll, retry, or local resume can perform an effect.\\n  1184\\t3. `lift(export-record)`: confirm the record is at a safepoint (§7.4.1),\\n  1185\\t   else `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; canonicalize every referenced segment and\\n  1186\\t   compute its address (§7.3.2), else the refusal; encode (§7.5), else the\\n  1187\\t   refusal; compute `:yin.k/requires` and `:yin.k/store` by the fixed point\\n  1188\\t   (§7.6), recording the discovery status; stamp the contract (§7.3.3); on\\n  1189\\t   first export, mint the occurrence; mint `:yin.k/id`.\\n  1190\\t4. Append the canonical instruction vectors of every required segment to a\\n  1191\\t   `dao.jing` intake pool (the payloads *are* the vectors, §7.3.4), then\\n  1192\\t   the id-less value body to the carrier medium; observe `:dao.stream/ok`\\n  1193\\t   on each. `:yin.k/carried` may then be empty. Retry of any failed append\\n  1194\\t   reuses the occurrence (§7.7.4).\\n  1195\\t5. Under `:exclusive`, append `:yin.k/offered` to the arbitration medium.\\n  1196\\t   The export record stays for recovery; the emitter is now a candidate\\n  1197\\t   like any other, and wakes only on custody (a grant to itself), never on\\n  1198\\t   data.\\n  1199\\t\\n  1200\\t**Lower** (resumer side):\\n  1201\\t\\n  1202\\t1. Read the value; check `:yin.k/contract`, else `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`.\\n  1203\\t2. Verify `:yin.k/id` by re-hashing the body, else `:yin.k/hash-mismatch`.\\n  1204\\t3. Decode against the §7.5.1 grammar with full structural validation, else\\n  1205\\t   `:yin.k/undecodable` — a correct hash is not structural validation, and\\n  1206\\t   both checks always run.\\n  1207\\t4. Evaluate `:yin.k/requires` (§7.6.5); fetch what may be fetched (segments\\n  1208\\t   by address, verified by re-canonicalization); else\\n  1209\\t   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with the discovery status.\\n  1210\\t5. Under `:exclusive`, attach to the arbitration medium (an attachment\\n  1211\\t   failure here is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming it — the value declares its\\n  1212\\t   authority, §7.7.3), propose, then observe the grant, else\\n  1213\\t   `:yin.k/not-holder` with the lease state observed; while a proposal is\\n  1214\\t   unanswered the driver's step answers `:yin.k/awaiting-grant` — a\\n  1215\\t   grantor owes no answer and no bound applies (`dao.lease.md`,\\n  1216\\t   *Authority*).\\n  1217\\t6. Load every required segment by address into the per-stamp index,\\n  1218\\t   verifying each (§7.3.4).\\n  1219\\t7. **Restore into an isolated store** (§7.6.2): the slice, the remapped\\n  1220\\t   cells, the resumer's FFI pair. Attach to every stream descriptor in the\\n  1221\\t   frame, pending waits, and cells (§7.4.3); an attachment `not-found` is\\n  1222\\t   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream. **Any failure from this step\\n  1223\\t   onward — after custody was acquired — releases first**: the resumer\\n  1224\\t   appends `:dao.lease/released`, the occurrence returns to offered, and\\n  1225\\t   the failure outcome is returned. A resumer that dies without releasing\\n  1226\\t   is recovered by the lease's own lapse; nothing else is owed.\\n  1227\\t8. `lower(frame, layout)` into engine registers; re-mint every pending wait\\n  1228\\t   in the local wait set; seed `:id-counter` per §7.6.3; run — through a\\n  1229\\t   fenced writer under `:exclusive` (§7.7.5).\\n  1230\\t9. At the next safepoint or halt, produce the successor occurrence\\n  1231\\t   (§7.7.6), record `:yin.k/resumed`, release.\\n  1232\\t\\n  1233\\tNeither sequence contains a callback. Each is a step function a\\n  1234\\tcomposition-supplied driver repeats, as for every other interpreter in the\\n  1235\\tsystem.\\n  1236\\t\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n46:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_22\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '1,140p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"     1\\t# yin.vm.linker: the universal code linker over dao.stream\\n     2\\t\\n     3\\tStatus: design, proposed (2026-09-24, revision r11). Supersedes the\\n     4\\tnamespace placement and the two-backend scope of\\n     5\\t`yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md` (Phase B6); that document's fetch pipeline,\\n     6\\tformat records, refusal vocabulary, and same-root pairing are carried\\n     7\\there in substance and extended to all four `yin.vm` execution backends.\\n     8\\tRevision r2 reconciled the adversarial review\\n     9\\t`collab/1790250614225-reviewer-universal-linker-spec.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md`;\\n    10\\trevisions r3 to r11 reconcile the round-2 to round-10 consensus\\n    11\\treviews by `gpt-6-sol` (thread\\n    12\\t`01a0d340-f8e7-7e30-9b74-c0a0e6b636fb`). Section\\n    13\\t13 records each finding of every round and its resolution. Subordinate to\\n    14\\t`datom.world.md`, `dao.stream.md`, and `dao.jing.md`. Builds on the code\\n    15\\tidentity rules of `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` (UCF) section\\n    16\\t7.3, the addressing and derivation rules of `yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`\\n    17\\tsections 4 and 5, and the ledger of `yin.vm.ledger`.\\n    18\\t\\n    19\\tRelated documents:\\n    20\\t\\n    21\\t- `docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md` - the B6 predecessor (two\\n    22\\t  formats, one synchronous fetch)\\n    23\\t- `docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md` - governing decisions D5 to D16\\n    24\\t- `docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` - AST rows, segment vectors,\\n    25\\t  derivation records\\n    26\\t- `docs/design/yin.vm.dependency-completion.md` - the work-item fixed\\n    27\\t  point that computes a program's requirements\\n    28\\t- `docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` - code identity,\\n    29\\t  contract stamp, safepoints, pending waits, primitive profiles\\n    30\\t- `docs/design/dao.jing.md` - content-addressed storage and the stepped\\n    31\\t  remote client\\n    32\\t- `docs/design/dao.stream.md` - the stream contract, in particular OD-5\\n    33\\t\\n    34\\t## 1. Objective and architectural position\\n    35\\t\\n    36\\t`yin.vm` has four execution backends, all reachable from `yin.repl`\\n    37\\t(`src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc`, `vm-constructors`):\\n    38\\t\\n    39\\t| Backend       | Kernel                    | Code shape                   |\\n    40\\t|---------------|---------------------------|------------------------------|\\n    41\\t| `:ast-walker` | `yin.vm.ast-walker`       | universal AST row set        |\\n    42\\t| `:semantic`   | `yin.vm.semantic`         | positional instruction vector|\\n    43\\t| `:stack`      | `yin.vm.debruijn.stack`   | de Bruijn stack image (H)    |\\n    44\\t| `:register`   | `yin.vm.debruijn.register`| de Bruijn register image (R) |\\n    45\\t\\n    46\\tEach backend needs the same service: given an identity for some code, and\\n    47\\tnothing else, obtain the code, prove it is the code the identity names,\\n    48\\tprove it is well formed, prove the receiver can bind its dependencies\\n    49\\tidentically to the publisher, and hand it over for loading. Today that\\n    50\\tservice exists once, for two of the four backends, under a name that says\\n    51\\tit belongs to one of them: `yin.vm.debruijn-linker`.\\n    52\\t\\n    53\\tThis document makes two corrections and one unification.\\n    54\\t\\n    55\\t**Correction 1: the linker is universal.** The B6 fetch function has no\\n    56\\tde Bruijn branch in it. It is parameterized by a format record and runs\\n    57\\tthe same six steps for a stack image and a register image. The namespace\\n    58\\t`yin.vm.debruijn-linker` is therefore a misnomer that invites a second\\n    59\\tlinker for the semantic VM and a third for the walker. There is one\\n    60\\tlinker, `yin.vm.linker`, and every backend is a format record it takes.\\n    61\\t\\n    62\\t**Correction 2: local and remote linking are one mechanism.** B6 already\\n    63\\tstates this (`yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md` section 7.2: \\\"the boundary is\\n    64\\t`dao.stream`; local and remote resolution are the same transport-neutral\\n    65\\tmechanism\\\"). But `fetch` today reads through a synchronous `dao.jing`\\n    66\\thandle, and a local composition hands it an in-memory store whose `get` is\\n    67\\ta map lookup. That is a function call across what the axioms say is a\\n    68\\tstream boundary. This document makes the request and response a stream\\n    69\\texchange in every composition; what varies is the transport underneath\\n    70\\t(ring buffer, file, WebSocket, DHT behind a served boundary), never the\\n    71\\tlinker's shape.\\n    72\\t\\n    73\\t**Unification: `require` is linking.** Module loading in `yin.vm.module`\\n    74\\tresolves `(require 'foo)` against a dictionary the composition built by\\n    75\\thand at wiring time. Linking resolves an identity to content over a stream.\\n    76\\tThese are the same operation at two grains: a module name is an entry in a\\n    77\\tname environment whose value is a manifest address, and a manifest names\\n    78\\tthe images the linker fetches. `(require 'foo)` lowers to an effect that\\n    79\\tparks the VM and emits a link request; the module arrives as verified\\n    80\\tcontent on a response stream and is installed by a child evaluation the\\n    81\\tscheduler owns. The dictionary becomes a manifest registry: a value naming\\n    82\\tcontent addresses, never holding code that arrived by linking.\\n    83\\t\\n    84\\t### 1.1 What this document adds over B6\\n    85\\t\\n    86\\t1. The namespace `yin.vm.linker` and its migration from\\n    87\\t   `yin.vm.debruijn-linker` (section 9).\\n    88\\t2. Two new format records, `:yin.ast/code` and `:yin.semantic/code`, beside\\n    89\\t   the two B6 records (section 5).\\n    90\\t3. A `:parts-fn` slot in the format record so that a multi-row payload\\n    91\\t   (an AST tree) fetches through the same pipeline as a single vector,\\n    92\\t   with row-local validation and composition-supplied bounds before any\\n    93\\t   child is enqueued (section 4.2, step 2).\\n    94\\t4. Identity-directed matching: `:identity-matches-fn` replaces a bare\\n    95\\t   hash comparison, so storage-derived identities verify under the\\n    96\\t   algorithm the identity carries (section 4.1).\\n    97\\t5. Dependency obligations `{name kind expected}` replacing bare free\\n    98\\t   names, net of the module's own definitions, discharged by exact\\n    99\\t   profile or manifest equality at the receiver (section 4.2, step 5).\\n   100\\t6. The stepped link interface (`link-state`, `request-link`, `step`,\\n   101\\t   `abandon`) as the portable shape, with a plain-data wire request and\\n   102\\t   all indexes and capabilities held in linker-local state; the\\n   103\\t   synchronous `fetch` is reclassified as host policy over an explicit\\n   104\\t   link runtime (section 6).\\n   105\\t7. The `:module/require` effect lowered onto the linker: two wait\\n   106\\t   states, cursor-before-append, pair-scoped id correlation,\\n   107\\t   installation as a scheduler child task, and export relocation by\\n   108\\t   lift and lower with a portable module store and per-task lowering\\n   109\\t   (section 7).\\n   110\\t8. The module manifest, pinned to one canonical tree by content-addressed\\n   111\\t   derivation records verified by re-lowering or explicitly trusted,\\n   112\\t   and the manifest registry that replaces the in-memory module\\n   113\\t   dictionary (section 8).\\n   114\\t9. A fail-closed, proof-carrying name-authority policy for `dao.space`\\n   115\\t   assertions, an entry criterion for M4 (section 8.2).\\n   116\\t10. Eleven additional refusals (section 4.3).\\n   117\\t\\n   118\\t### 1.2 What this document does not add\\n   119\\t\\n   120\\t- No transport. `dao.stream` and its transports are the only medium.\\n   121\\t- No peer routing, caching, or responder. `dao.jing.remote` serves\\n   122\\t  content; `dao.jing.dht` finds it, behind that served boundary.\\n   123\\t- No global registry, loader, or callback. Every handle, index, stream,\\n   124\\t  and format record is an explicit argument or a value in VM state.\\n   125\\t- No execution inside the linker. The linker fetches and verifies. Loading\\n   126\\t  an image into a kernel and running a module body belong to the VM's\\n   127\\t  scheduler.\\n   128\\t- No change to `image-hash`, `register-hash`, `segment-key`, the opcode\\n   129\\t  tables, or any kernel's step function.\\n   130\\t\\n   131\\t## 2. Governing invariants\\n   132\\t\\n   133\\tEvery rule below derives from `datom.world.md`. The derivations are named\\n   134\\tonce here so later sections can cite them.\\n   135\\t\\n   136\\t**I1. Code at rest is content in `dao.jing`.** An image, a row, a vector,\\n   137\\ta manifest, a derivation record: each is an opaque value at its\\n   138\\t`segment-key`. `dao.jing` assigns identity and retrieval and nothing else\\n   139\\t(`dao.jing.md`, *Definition*). The linker never asks the store what a\\n   140\\tvalue means.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n50:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_24\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '246,419p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"   246\\t## 4. The fetch pipeline\\n   247\\t\\n   248\\t### 4.1 The format record\\n   249\\t\\n   250\\tA format record is a plain map held in linker-local state. B6 fixed three\\n   251\\tfunction slots; this revision replaces one, renames one, and adds four:\\n   252\\t\\n   253\\t```clojure\\n   254\\t{:format              :yin.debruijn.code      ; the dispatch key\\n   255\\t :contract            \\\"b1\\\"                    ; contract revision name\\n   256\\t :identity-fn         (fn [value] identity)   ; mint: publish! only\\n   257\\t :identity-matches-fn (fn [identity value] boolean) ; step 3\\n   258\\t :row-defect-fn       (fn [value] defect-or-nil)    ; step 2, per part\\n   259\\t :validate-fn         (fn [value] defect-or-nil)    ; step 4, whole\\n   260\\t :obligations-fn      (fn [value] records)           ; step 5a, below\\n   261\\t :definitions-fn      (fn [value] records)           ; step 5a, below\\n   262\\t :applications-fn     (fn [value] records)           ; step 5a, below\\n   263\\t :parts-fn            (fn [value] [address ...])}   ; step 2 worklist\\n   264\\t```\\n   265\\t\\n   266\\t- `:identity-fn` mints an identity from a payload. It is used by\\n   267\\t  `publish!` and never by verification.\\n   268\\t- `:identity-matches-fn` is the step-3 check, directed by the identity.\\n   269\\t  For H and R it is `(= identity (image-hash value))`, a contract-pinned\\n   270\\t  hash with one algorithm. For the storage-derived formats it is\\n   271\\t  `(jing/segment-matches? identity value)`, so a value minted under\\n   272\\t  `:sha256` while the default is `:blake3` verifies under the algorithm\\n   273\\t  the identity names and never fails spuriously.\\n   274\\t- `:row-defect-fn` validates one fetched part on its own, before any\\n   275\\t  address it references is enqueued: tag, arity, slot kinds, saturation.\\n   276\\t  For single-payload formats it is the whole validator, run early.\\n   277\\t- `:parts-fn` returns the storage addresses a fetched payload references\\n   278\\t  and without which it is incomplete. For the three single-payload\\n   279\\t  formats it returns nothing. For `:yin.ast/code` it returns the child\\n   280\\t  row ids of a row body.\\n   281\\t- `:obligations-fn`, `:definitions-fn`, and `:applications-fn` are\\n   282\\t  **position-bearing scanners** with one shared result shape:\\n   283\\t\\n   284\\t  ```clojure\\n   285\\t  ;; :obligations-fn  value -> [{:name sym :at position :in-body? b}]\\n   286\\t  ;; :definitions-fn  value -> [{:name sym :at position\\n   287\\t  ;;                             :conditional? b}]\\n   288\\t  ;; :applications-fn value -> [{:at position}]\\n   289\\t  ;; position: a tree occurrence path [root-address path] for\\n   290\\t  ;;           :yin.ast/code; a pc for the vector formats\\n   291\\t  ```\\n   292\\t\\n   293\\t  `:conditional?` is true when any `:if` branch, jump target range, or\\n   294\\t  lambda body encloses the definition; `:in-body?` is true when a\\n   295\\t  lambda body encloses the occurrence; an `:applications-fn` record is\\n   296\\t  one **application site**: a position at which this image may apply a\\n   297\\t  closure it creates -- a direct call site, a call of an imported\\n   298\\t  name, or a call of a primitive that receives a function argument,\\n   299\\t  because the callee may apply what it is handed. Dominance between\\n   300\\t  two positions is decided by the linker from these fields and the\\n   301\\t  format's order (step 5a). For the AST record all three scanners are\\n   302\\t  Datalog over the rows: `yin.vm/free-names` today returns a symbol\\n   303\\t  set, and is extended (or wrapped by an occurrence-scoped query over\\n   304\\t  the same rules) to return each occurrence's path; the definitions\\n   305\\t  scanner is the store-key query behind `yin.vm/ast-requirements`\\n   306\\t  with the binding row's path and its enclosing tags; the application\\n   307\\t  scanner is the application-row query over the same relation. For\\n   308\\t  the vector formats all three are operand scans by pc (`:var`\\n   309\\t  against `:store-put`, `:load-free` against the `yin/def` call\\n   310\\t  sites, the call opcodes for applications) with conditional target\\n   311\\t  ranges taken from the `:jump-if` and body operands. A format whose\\n   312\\t  scanner returns positions as `nil` is admitted and falls under the\\n   313\\t  conservative degradation of step 5a: every occurrence is retained\\n   314\\t  as an obligation, and with no application positions no occurrence\\n   315\\t  inside a lambda body is discharged at all.\\n   316\\t\\n   317\\t`fetch` and `step` contain no branch on `:format`. Adding a fifth backend\\n   318\\tis one more record.\\n   319\\t\\n   320\\t### 4.2 The six steps\\n   321\\t\\n   322\\tThe order is B6's, unchanged, and remains load-bearing for the reasons\\n   323\\tB6 gives. An admission check precedes step 1; step 2 is a bounded\\n   324\\tworklist; steps 1 and 2 are stream exchanges (section 6); step 5 has a\\n   325\\tlinker half and a receiver half.\\n   326\\t\\n   327\\t```text\\n   328\\t(link runtime format identity)\\n   329\\t\\n   330\\t0. admission     request malformed                  -> :invalid-request\\n   331\\t                 no format record for the format    -> :unsupported-format\\n   332\\t                 request contract differs from the record's\\n   333\\t                                                    -> :contract-mismatch\\n   334\\t1. address    <- (index identity)               linker-local index\\n   335\\t                 absent entry                       -> :absent\\n   336\\t2. payload    <- fetch address over dao.stream\\n   337\\t                 absent payload                     -> :absent\\n   338\\t                 (not (segment-matches? address v)) -> :address-mismatch\\n   339\\t                 ((:row-defect-fn format) v)        -> :descriptor-defect\\n   340\\t                 bounds exceeded                    -> :parts-limit\\n   341\\t                 enqueue ((:parts-fn format) v); repeat until empty\\n   342\\t3.               (not ((:identity-matches-fn format) identity root))\\n   343\\t                                                    -> :hash-mismatch\\n   344\\t4.               ((:validate-fn format) whole) returns a defect\\n   345\\t                                                    -> :descriptor-defect\\n   346\\t5a.              obligations <- join free names with declarations\\n   347\\t                 free name with no declaration      -> :undeclared-free\\n   348\\t5b.  (receiver)  each obligation discharged by exact equality\\n   349\\t                 profile or manifest differs        -> :unresolved-free\\n   350\\t                 name bound in free env or store    -> :shadowed-free\\n   351\\t6. return the verified image as data\\n   352\\t```\\n   353\\t\\n   354\\tStep by step, for all four formats:\\n   355\\t\\n   356\\t0. **Admit the request.** Before any lookup or fetch, the linker checks\\n   357\\t   the request's shape against the closed key set of section 6.3\\n   358\\t   (`:invalid-request`), that it holds a format record for the requested\\n   359\\t   format (`:unsupported-format`), and that the request's\\n   360\\t   `:yin.link/contract`, when present, equals the record's `:contract`\\n   361\\t   (`:contract-mismatch`). The contract check runs here, ahead of step\\n   362\\t   2's row-local grammar check, so no validator ever runs under the\\n   363\\t   wrong table; revision r2 placed it in step 4, after row-local\\n   364\\t   validation had already run, which was an ordering defect. For a\\n   365\\t   manifest-delivered image the same check runs again against the\\n   366\\t   manifest's per-format entry under `:yin.module/contracts`\\n   367\\t   immediately after the manifest is verified and before any derivation\\n   368\\t   or image is fetched (section 8.1). The manifest's own schema version\\n   369\\t   is a different thing and is checked by the manifest validator.\\n   370\\t1. **Resolve identity to storage address.** The index is linker-local\\n   371\\t   composition data: a map, or datoms `[identity attribute address]`\\n   372\\t   read through `index-from-datoms` from a `dao.space` source the linker\\n   373\\t   observes with its own cursor. An entry that has not been observed\\n   374\\t   yet is `:absent` now, which is the honest answer at this moment; a\\n   375\\t   later request may find it. Step 1 never waits and never queries a\\n   376\\t   remote index inline. For the storage-derived formats the identity is\\n   377\\t   its own address and the index is the identity function.\\n   378\\t2. **Fetch payload over `dao.stream`.** Each address is a\\n   379\\t   `:jing/get-content` request on the content request stream and a\\n   380\\t   correlated response on the content response stream (section 6). The\\n   381\\t   value is verified against the address it was requested at, using the\\n   382\\t   algorithm the address carries (`jing/segment-matches?`). Then, before\\n   383\\t   any child is enqueued, `:row-defect-fn` validates this part alone;\\n   384\\t   a malformed row is refused here and its slots are never followed.\\n   385\\t   Then `:parts-fn` names further addresses; each is fetched and\\n   386\\t   verified the same way; the worklist is breadth-first from the root\\n   387\\t   and visits each address once. The composition supplies bounds in the\\n   388\\t   runtime: `:max-parts`, `:max-depth`, `:max-bytes`; exceeding any is\\n   389\\t   `:parts-limit` naming the bound and the address at which it was hit.\\n   390\\t   The result of step 2 is the root value plus, for a multi-part\\n   391\\t   format, the map of every fetched part by address.\\n   392\\t3. **Verify format identity.** `(:identity-matches-fn format)` over the\\n   393\\t   requested identity and the root value. For H and R this is a\\n   394\\t   different hash under a different preimage from step 2 and catches a\\n   395\\t   stale or swapped index entry, a descriptor disagreement, or a\\n   396\\t   contract version disagreement (all `:hash-mismatch`, as in B6). For\\n   397\\t   the storage-derived formats it is `segment-matches?` under the\\n   398\\t   identity's own algorithm; it still runs, because it is the only check\\n   399\\t   that the *index* did not point the identity at a different, correctly\\n   400\\t   stored payload.\\n   401\\t4. **Validate structural and liveness invariants.** The format's\\n   402\\t   validator runs over the complete value: for a tree, over the\\n   403\\t   assembled row set (`:id-resolves`, `:acyclic`, `:root-reachable` are\\n   404\\t   whole-tree rules that step 2's row-local check cannot run); for a\\n   405\\t   vector, over the vector; for a register image, including the\\n   406\\t   ascending, bounded, exact live-set rules. A validator that throws is\\n   407\\t   `:descriptor-defect` with rule `:validator-refused`.\\n   408\\t5. **Verify dependency closure.** Two halves.\\n   409\\t   - *5a, at the linker: obligations.* Two scans run over the verified\\n   410\\t     value. `:obligations-fn` yields the free-name occurrences, each\\n   411\\t     with its position: for a tree, the occurrence path and whether it\\n   412\\t     lies inside a `:lambda` body; for a vector, the pc and whether it\\n   413\\t     lies in a body rather than the main sequence. `:definitions-fn`\\n   414\\t     yields the **definition set** with positions: every constant store\\n   415\\t     key the image binds at module level (the section 7.7 store-key\\n   416\\t     query of `yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`: `:vm/store-put` rows,\\n   417\\t     `:store-put` operands, `yin/def` applications with a constant key),\\n   418\\t     each with the position of the binding in the main sequence.\\n   419\\t     A definition discharges an occurrence by **defined-before-use\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n52:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_25\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '1839,1971p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"  1839\\t## 9. Migration from `yin.vm.debruijn-linker`\\n  1840\\t\\n  1841\\tFive steps, each landing green on all three hosts before the next. The\\n  1842\\tpredecessor namespace is renamed, not aliased.\\n  1843\\t\\n  1844\\t```mermaid\\n  1845\\tflowchart TD\\n  1846\\t  M1[\\\"M1 rename\\\\nyin.vm.debruijn-linker -> yin.vm.linker\\\"]\\n  1847\\t  M2[\\\"M2 four format records\\\\nidentity-directed match, bounded parts\\\"]\\n  1848\\t  M3[\\\"M3 stepped core + link runtime\\\\nfetch = host policy; DHT behind server\\\"]\\n  1849\\t  M4[\\\"M4 require lowers to the linker\\\\nmanifests, authority, child install\\\"]\\n  1850\\t  M5[\\\"M5 yin.repl wires the four streams\\\\nper backend\\\"]\\n  1851\\t  M1 --> M2 --> M3 --> M4 --> M5\\n  1852\\t```\\n  1853\\t\\n  1854\\t**M1. Rename.** Move `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linker.cljc` to\\n  1855\\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc` with namespace `yin.vm.linker`; move the\\n  1856\\ttest likewise. Update the five design documents that name the old\\n  1857\\tnamespace (`yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md`, `yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`,\\n  1858\\t`dao.agent.md`, `dao.agent.harness.md`, `dao.agent.mcp.server.md`) and\\n  1859\\tthe orchestrator log. Mark `yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md` as superseded by\\n  1860\\tthis document in its status line. No behavior changes. The B6 test\\n  1861\\tsuite passes unchanged apart from the require alias.\\n  1862\\t\\n  1863\\t**M2. Four format records.** Replace `:hash-fn` with `:identity-fn` and\\n  1864\\t`:identity-matches-fn`; rename `:free-names-fn` to `:obligations-fn`;\\n  1865\\tadd `:contract`, `:row-defect-fn`, and `:parts-fn`; add `ast-format`,\\n  1866\\t`semantic-format`, `semantic-free-names`, and `row-local-defect`. Make\\n  1867\\tstep 2 the bounded worklist. `yin.vm.content` keeps `materialize-tree!`\\n  1868\\tand `materialize-vector!` as the mint side (they are `publish!` for those\\n  1869\\tformats); `load-rows` and `fetch-vector` are retired, and their callers\\n  1870\\tmove to `fetch` with the new records. Tests, beyond B6's matrix for the\\n  1871\\ttwo new formats: a tree with one absent child row is `:absent` naming\\n  1872\\tthat row; a tree with one corrupt child row is `:address-mismatch`\\n  1873\\tnaming it; a tree whose root is well-formed but whose child has a bad\\n  1874\\ttag is `:descriptor-defect` with no fetch of that child's slots; a tree\\n  1875\\texceeding `:max-parts` is `:parts-limit`; a vector whose index entry\\n  1876\\tpoints at a different valid vector is `:hash-mismatch`; a vector minted\\n  1877\\tunder `:sha256` verifies against its own address while the default is\\n  1878\\t`:blake3`.\\n  1879\\t\\n  1880\\t**M3. Stepped core and link runtime.** Implement `link-state`,\\n  1881\\t`request-link`, `step`, `abandon` over `dao.jing.remote.step` (or, for a\\n  1882\\tpurely local composition, over `dao.stream.rpc` on a ring-buffer pair\\n  1883\\tserved by `dao.jing.remote/serve-content!`'s handlers). Reimplement\\n  1884\\t`fetch` as the blocking driver over a link runtime; export `verify` and\\n  1885\\t`discharge`. Tests: the full refusal matrix through `step` over ring\\n  1886\\tbuffers on all three hosts; the JVM WebSocket path from B6 completion\\n  1887\\tcriterion 1; a `:pending` sequence where the content server answers one\\n  1888\\tpart per step; a request carrying a function or a handle is\\n  1889\\t`:invalid-request`; the local-fetch traffic test of section 6.4 with a\\n  1890\\t`get`-counting server handle and a handle-free linker state; the DHT\\n  1891\\thandle behind `serve-content!` answering a link over ring buffers with\\n  1892\\tthe server driven by the test.\\n  1893\\t\\n  1894\\t**M4. `require` lowers to the linker.** Entry criterion: the section 8.2\\n  1895\\tassertion policy is implemented and its tests pass (a signed assertion\\n  1896\\tby a declared principal resolves; a bare `:asserted-by` with no proof is\\n  1897\\t`:unauthenticated`; a bad signature is `:unauthenticated`; an attested\\n  1898\\tlog's assertion copied onto another stream is `:unauthenticated`; an\\n  1899\\tundeclared principal is ignored; a signed retraction bound to an\\n  1900\\tassertion's id removes that assertion only, and a retraction naming no\\n  1901\\tassertion or signed by another principal is discarded; an exact\\n  1902\\tduplicate envelope is honored once and never counted as equivocation;\\n  1903\\ta replayed sequence and a distinct equivocating pair are discarded; two\\n  1904\\tproven assertions refuse `:ambiguous-name`; snapshot advance). Then:\\n  1905\\tthe manifest and derivation\\n  1906\\tformat records; `register-host-module` with profile enforcement;\\n  1907\\t`require-handler` minting, parking, and emitting; the two wait states\\n  1908\\tthrough `check-wait-set` with id correlation; the install child in the\\n  1909\\tscheduler with the section 7.3 phases; `link-module`; the UCF table\\n  1910\\tamendments. Tests, per backend: a require of an unlinked module over\\n  1911\\tring buffers completes and the program resumes with its exports bound;\\n  1912\\ta manifest declaring a different name is `:module-name-mismatch`; a\\n  1913\\tmanifest whose semantic, H, or R derivation leads from a different tree\\n  1914\\tis `:derivation-mismatch` (three swapped-image tests, and a fourth\\n  1915\\twhere the `:yin.ast/code` tree is not the manifest's tree); a\\n  1916\\tsame-named primitive of different profile at the receiver is\\n  1917\\t`:unresolved-free`; a shadowed export name in the requiring program's\\n  1918\\tstore is `:shadowed-free`; a free name the manifest never declares is\\n  1919\\t`:undeclared-free`; two outstanding requires on one response stream\\n  1920\\teach restore on their own id; a response landing before the entry would\\n  1921\\thave been polled is not skipped; a module requiring another links\\n  1922\\ttransitively while a third task keeps running; a require cycle is\\n  1923\\t`:require-cycle`; a module that binds a name and exports a closure\\n  1924\\treading it links, the closure reads the module's value in the\\n  1925\\tparent, a parent binding of the same name neither supplies nor\\n  1926\\tshadows it, and a write from one export is visible to the next\\n  1927\\tapplication in the same task and to no other task; a read inside a\\n  1928\\tbody applied before its definition retains its obligation and is\\n  1929\\t`:undeclared-free` unless the manifest declares it; an export-only\\n  1930\\timage with no application site produces no obligations; a parked\\n  1931\\tentry recorded before an attach restores after it at the same pcs\\n  1932\\twith `:segment` never assigned from the entry; a register call in\\n  1933\\tflight while a nested require attaches an image returns into the\\n  1934\\tgrown code space, the attachment intact after the return; two tasks\\n  1935\\tholding images of different lengths require the same module and each\\n  1936\\tapplies its export correctly; a `:store-put` executed inside an\\n  1937\\texported closure writes the module store, and a module closure\\n  1938\\tcalling another module's closure writes each module's own store; a\\n  1939\\tslice re-exporting a dependency closure carries the dependency's\\n  1940\\tstore snapshot with the child's mutations, and a task already\\n  1941\\tholding that dependency's store keeps its own instance; a module\\n  1942\\tforging a predictable resource key (`[:store-get :stream-0]`)\\n  1943\\tagainst a task with a live stream reads nothing on any backend; a\\n  1944\\tforged reference literal -- stream, cursor, or FFI cell id --\\n  1945\\tpassed to an effect is `:forged-resource-reference` while the\\n  1946\\ttask's own sealed references resolve, and a lifted-then-lowered\\n  1947\\treference works under the receiver's seal; a module exporting a\\n  1948\\tfabricated, unsealed reference literal that never passed an\\n  1949\\teffect in the child fails the export lift with\\n  1950\\t`:yin.k/non-portable` and\\n  1951\\t`:yin.k/kind :forged-resource-reference`; a module export\\n  1952\\tcarrying a stream or cursor reference lifts and\\n  1953\\tlowers, the attachment or cell landing in the receiver's private\\n  1954\\t`:resources` and only the remapped reference in program values;\\n  1955\\tREPL input following a module closure's tail-completed application\\n  1956\\tdefines into the task's own store, no stale module context; a\\n  1957\\twalker closure whose body node is shared by two `:lambda` rows\\n  1958\\twith different params lifts from its recorded source row and\\n  1959\\trefuses `:unrooted-body` when a fabricated `[node params]` pair\\n  1960\\tmatches no row; `(vm :stack)` and `(vm :register)` in `yin.repl` link\\n  1961\\tthe same manifest by H and R respectively and produce B0-equal results.\\n  1962\\t\\n  1963\\t**M5. `yin.repl` wiring.** The REPL composes the link pair per VM and\\n  1964\\tthe content pair per connection (`connect` already opens the RPC\\n  1965\\tclient). `(require 'foo)` at the prompt exercises the whole path.\\n  1966\\t\\n  1967\\tCoexistence during M4: host modules are entered by\\n  1968\\t`register-host-module` from the first commit of M4, so every existing\\n  1969\\tcomposition keeps working while linked modules are added. There is no\\n  1970\\tperiod in which two registry shapes coexist.\\n  1971\\t\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n56:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_27\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '2028,2183p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"  2028\\t## 11. Completion criteria\\n  2029\\t\\n  2030\\tStructure:\\n  2031\\t\\n  2032\\t1. One `fetch` and one `step` serve all four formats with no\\n  2033\\t   format-specific branching; a fifth format is one record.\\n  2034\\t2. No global loader, registry, callback, or cache; every input is an\\n  2035\\t   argument or VM-state value; no function or handle ever appears in a\\n  2036\\t   value on the link or content streams.\\n  2037\\t3. Tri-host parity (JVM, Node, ClojureDart), 0 kondo errors, clean\\n  2038\\t   cljstyle.\\n  2039\\t4. This file and `yin.vm.linker` are pure ASCII with every line at most\\n  2040\\t   80 columns.\\n  2041\\t\\n  2042\\tVerification:\\n  2043\\t\\n  2044\\t5. Every B6 completion criterion (section 11 there) holds for all four\\n  2045\\t   formats where applicable, through both `fetch` and `step`.\\n  2046\\t6. A payload whose address is valid but whose identity is not the one\\n  2047\\t   requested is refused for every format, including the two whose\\n  2048\\t   identity is a storage address, and under every registered algorithm.\\n  2049\\t7. A request naming a contract the format record does not implement is\\n  2050\\t   `:contract-mismatch` before validation runs.\\n  2051\\t8. A malformed row is refused before any of its children is requested,\\n  2052\\t   and every composition bound produces `:parts-limit`.\\n  2053\\t9. Local `fetch` produces exactly the content-pair traffic the stepped\\n  2054\\t   path produces and holds no content handle.\\n  2055\\t\\n  2056\\tRequire:\\n  2057\\t\\n  2058\\t10. `(require 'foo)` on each of the four backends, over ring buffers,\\n  2059\\t    parks, links, installs through the child phases, and resumes with\\n  2060\\t    `'foo`; the same over the JVM WebSocket content path.\\n  2061\\t11. The scheduler registry holds no function that arrived by linking\\n  2062\\t    and no lowered bindings, only the portable slice; every task-local\\n  2063\\t    `:bindings` was lowered from a slice an isolated child evaluation\\n  2064\\t    produced, which reached halt with every export bound.\\n  2065\\t12. UCF section 7.4.1 carries `:link-request`, `:link-response`, and\\n  2066\\t    `:install` safepoints, section 7.4.3 their pending variants, section\\n  2067\\t    7.5.1 the `:yin.k/binding` closure variants, the `:yin.k/store-of`\\n  2068\\t    key, and the re-sealed references of section 7.3, sections 7.5.1,\\n  2069\\t    7.5.3, and 7.6.2 the private engine-resource table of section\\n  2070\\t    7.3 and the resource decode targets and store model it moves\\n  2071\\t    out of the store, and a parked entry of each kind lifts and\\n  2072\\t    lowers through UCF's round-trip test.\\n  2073\\t13. Every swapped-image and swapped-name case in M4 refuses before load.\\n  2074\\t14. The assertion policy of section 8.2 refuses `:ambiguous-name`,\\n  2075\\t    discards unproven assertions as `:unauthenticated`, and ignores\\n  2076\\t    undeclared principals, at a fixed snapshot.\\n  2077\\t15. A module that defines a name and then reads it in the main\\n  2078\\t    sequence produces no obligation for that name; a main-sequence\\n  2079\\t    read before the definition is `:use-before-definition`; a read\\n  2080\\t    inside a body is free of obligation only when an unconditional\\n  2081\\t    definition dominates every application site, and retains it\\n  2082\\t    otherwise -- an application preceding the definition, a closure\\n  2083\\t    handed to an import, a scanner with no application positions --\\n  2084\\t    linking then only if the manifest declares it; an export-only\\n  2085\\t    image with no application site discharges body reads against its\\n  2086\\t    unconditional definitions; a name defined only inside an `:if`\\n  2087\\t    branch and read afterwards retains its obligation; a scanner\\n  2088\\t    with no positions retains every obligation; an export whose key\\n  2089\\t    is computed at run time is neither refused statically nor\\n  2090\\t    assumed, and is verified at `validated`.\\n  2091\\t16. Two install children and the root task, each with counter `0`,\\n  2092\\t    issue three requests on one link pair with three distinct ids, and\\n  2093\\t    each restores on its own.\\n  2094\\t17. On every backend, an exported closure applied in the parent after\\n  2095\\t    `linked` runs the same body it ran in the child; a stack and a\\n  2096\\t    register closure exported from a child whose image was appended at\\n  2097\\t    a nonzero offset in the parent resolve to the relocated pc; a\\n  2098\\t    walker closure lowers to a body structurally equal to the child's;\\n  2099\\t    on every backend, a parent parked mid-program at a nonzero pc with\\n  2100\\t    a non-empty stack, frames, and continuation resumes after\\n  2101\\t    `attach-image` at the same pc with the same stack, frames, and\\n  2102\\t    restore identities, and every register the loader would have reset\\n  2103\\t    is unchanged; the parked entry predates the attach, its recorded\\n  2104\\t    image is a row of the offset table, no restore writes `:segment`\\n  2105\\t    from an entry, and `:hash` is never a restore key; on the\\n  2106\\t    register backend, an image attached by a nested require while a\\n  2107\\t    call is in flight survives the callee's return: no frame carries\\n  2108\\t    or restores `:segment` or `:hash`, and the caller resumes in the\\n  2109\\t    grown code space at the same absolute `:return-pc`; a\\n  2110\\t    continuation lifted after an attach and lowered into a fresh\\n  2111\\t    kernel places every pc by the receiving offset table; two\\n  2112\\t    waiters holding images of different lengths each lower the same\\n  2113\\t    slice against their own coordinates and apply the export\\n  2114\\t    correctly.\\n  2115\\t18. Under `:derivation :verifying`, a manifest whose record names a\\n  2116\\t    profile the linker does not implement is `:unverified-derivation`,\\n  2117\\t    never installed; under `:trusted` the same manifest installs with\\n  2118\\t    `:trust :composition` in the outcome.\\n  2119\\t19. A request naming a mismatched contract is refused with no content\\n  2120\\t    request appended and no validator run.\\n  2121\\t20. A closure re-exported from a transitive dependency lifts with that\\n  2122\\t    dependency's image as origin, the parent loads that image before\\n  2123\\t    lowering, and an origin the scheduler never verified is\\n  2124\\t    `:foreign-image`; a positional marker offered to a named kernel is\\n  2125\\t    `:binding-mismatch`.\\n  2126\\t21. An exact duplicate of a valid envelope, appended any number of\\n  2127\\t    times, is honored exactly once and never marks its principal as\\n  2128\\t    equivocating; a replayed envelope with an old sequence, an envelope\\n  2129\\t    at or below a principal's floor, two distinct envelopes with one\\n  2130\\t    sequence, a retraction naming no assertion, and a retraction by\\n  2131\\t    another principal are each discarded with the stated kind; a\\n  2132\\t    retraction bound to an assertion's id removes that assertion only.\\n  2133\\t22. One four-way manifest links through all four kernels with no\\n  2134\\t    `:contract-mismatch`; a manifest of another schema version is\\n  2135\\t    `:descriptor-defect` with rule `:schema`; a derivation without a\\n  2136\\t    contract entry is `:descriptor-defect` with rule\\n  2137\\t    `:contract-missing`.\\n  2138\\t23. On every backend, a module that binds `(def x 1)` and exports a\\n  2139\\t    closure reading `x` links, and the closure applied in the parent\\n  2140\\t    reads the module's `x`, while a parent binding of the same name\\n  2141\\t    neither supplies nor shadows it; a write the closure makes to\\n  2142\\t    `x` through a direct `:store-put` instruction is visible to a\\n  2143\\t    sibling export applied afterwards in the same task and to no\\n  2144\\t    other task's module store; the same module linked by two tasks\\n  2145\\t    holds two isolated stores; a slice whose exports reach a\\n  2146\\t    dependency's store transitively carries that dependency's\\n  2147\\t    snapshot, including a mutation the child made through the\\n  2148\\t    dependency's closure, a missing snapshot is\\n  2149\\t    `:yin.k/non-portable` with `:yin.k/kind :missing-module-store`,\\n  2150\\t    and a receiving task that already holds the dependency's store\\n  2151\\t    keeps its own instance; a module closure calling another\\n  2152\\t    module's closure routes each body's reads and writes to its own\\n  2153\\t    module; in the child, a closure re-exported from a dependency\\n  2154\\t    reads that dependency's module store, not the child's ambient\\n  2155\\t    bindings; a module emitting `[:store-get :stream-0]` against a\\n  2156\\t    task with a live stream at that id reads nothing on any\\n  2157\\t    backend, because engine resources live in the private table no\\n  2158\\t    store instruction reads; a fabricated reference literal -- a\\n  2159\\t    `stream-ref`, a `cursor-ref`, or an FFI cell id, written by the\\n  2160\\t    program or by a linked module -- passed to any effect fails\\n  2161\\t    `:forged-resource-reference` on every backend, while the\\n  2162\\t    sealed reference the task itself was issued resolves, and a\\n  2163\\t    reference remapped by lower carries the receiver's seal; a\\n  2164\\t    module exporting a fabricated, unsealed reference literal\\n  2165\\t    that never passed an effect in the child fails the export\\n  2166\\t    lift with `:yin.k/non-portable` and\\n  2167\\t    `:yin.k/kind :forged-resource-reference`, so nothing is\\n  2168\\t    laundered; a\\n  2169\\t    module export whose captured\\n  2170\\t    environment carries a stream or a cursor reference lifts and\\n  2171\\t    lowers: the receiver's private `:resources` gains the\\n  2172\\t    attachment or cell, the export's value is the remapped,\\n  2173\\t    re-sealed reference, and no store key is created; after a\\n  2174\\t    module closure applied at the REPL and its tail call\\n  2175\\t    completed, new top-level input defining\\n  2176\\t    a name of its own binds the task's store, never the module's;\\n  2177\\t    a walker closure whose body node sits under two `:lambda` rows\\n  2178\\t    with different params lifts from its recorded source row --\\n  2179\\t    annotated on the decoded node at load, copied into the closure\\n  2180\\t    at evaluation -- and a fabricated closure with no recorded row\\n  2181\\t    resolves by the `[node params]` index or refuses\\n  2182\\t    `:unrooted-body`.\\n  2183\\t\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n59:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_28\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md | sed -n '366,448p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"   366\\t## 8. Owner ruling: static kinds, dynamic reasons\\n   367\\t\\n   368\\tChoose Option B. A static entry describes where an instruction may\\n   369\\tpark, not why this activation parked. UCF 7.4.1 distinguishes sent\\n   370\\tfrom retained FFI calls by the outcome of a stream operation. An\\n   371\\teffectful `:call` can yield different blocking effects at the same pc.\\n   372\\tNeither fact is a function of the canonical vector. UCF 7.4.2 puts\\n   373\\tdynamic state in the frame, and its static map does not specify a\\n   374\\t`:yin.safepoint/reasons` field. The Phase 1 field exceeds that map.\\n   375\\t\\n   376\\tThe reference wait set currently mints `:next` and `:put` only\\n   377\\t(`semantic.cljc:113,167,171,450`; `engine.cljc:383`). An explicit\\n   378\\t`:park` is a parked record, not a wait entry with `:reason :park`.\\n   379\\tFFI response waits use `:next`; retained requests can wait on `:put`.\\n   380\\tAn effectful `:call` parks according to the effect it produces. Thus\\n   381\\tthe current static vectors in `ucf.cljc:265-273` are design labels,\\n   382\\tnot observed reasons. They must not be copied into `:yin.k/reason`.\\n   383\\t\\n   384\\tM4 shall replace `:yin.safepoint/reasons` with\\n   385\\t`:yin.safepoint/kinds`, whose values are static possibilities from\\n   386\\tUCF 7.4.1. Use these names, one per table row:\\n   387\\t\\n   388\\t    :park        -> [:explicit-park]\\n   389\\t    :stream-next -> [:blocked-read]\\n   390\\t    :stream-put  -> [:blocked-write]\\n   391\\t    :ffi-call    -> [:ffi-sent :ffi-retained]\\n   392\\t    :call        -> [:effectful-call]\\n   393\\t\\n   394\\tThe two FFI values name possible outcomes, not two entries at a pc.\\n   395\\tThe `:call` value likewise does not select the effect or pending\\n   396\\tvariant. The static map stays a pure function of the vector. This is\\n   397\\ta naming correction to the Phase 1 safepoint API, not a new `v2`\\n   398\\texecution revision: `:yin.safepoint/reasons` was not a component named\\n   399\\tby UCF 7.3.3 or specified in UCF 7.4.2. Consumers must migrate the\\n   400\\tfield when M4 implements the lift driver; Phase 1 has no runtime\\n   401\\tconsumer, though `ucf_test.cljc` asserts its current values.\\n   402\\t\\n   403\\tThe lift driver must check the pc against the static safepoint set,\\n   404\\tthen inspect the actual parked record, wait entry, and effect outcome\\n   405\\tto construct `:yin.k/reason` and the corresponding UCF 7.4.3\\n   406\\t`:yin.k/pending` variant. It may translate `:next`/`:put` where their\\n   407\\tpending data suffices, but may not infer a reason from a kind alone.\\n   408\\tFor an effectful `:call`, the observed stream effect determines\\n   409\\t`:next` or `:put`; the instruction's kind does not force\\n   410\\t`:call-effect`. For FFI, the sent or retained state determines\\n   411\\t`:ffi` or `:ffi-request` on the wire, even though the local wait\\n   412\\tentry is `:next` or `:put`. Export must refuse when evidence is\\n   413\\tinsufficient. This ruling does not change the reference wait-set\\n   414\\tclassifier or mint new wait-entry reasons.\\n   415\\t\\n   416\\tUCF 7.4.1 lists `:park` and `:call-effect` in its frame enum, while\\n   417\\t7.4.3's claimed exhaustive pending examples show neither variant.\\n   418\\tM4 must reconcile those rows before exporting them: an explicit park\\n   419\\tneeds a specified no-wait representation, and any use of\\n   420\\t`:call-effect` needs a portable pending shape. This is a UCF protocol\\n   421\\tgap, not grounds to invent a dynamic reason from static code.\\n   422\\t\\n   423\\tUCF 7.11's safepoint harness must run each 7.4.1 row, including both\\n   424\\tFFI outcomes and effectful calls. It checks static kind membership,\\n   425\\tthe observed reason and pending variant, and lift/lower parity. The\\n   426\\tPhase 1 reviewer and architect identified this as a Phase 2 gate:\\n   427\\t`collab/1790268690622-reviewer-ucf-phase1.glm-flash.findings.md`\\n   428\\tand `collab/1790280923723-architect-ucf-phase1-signoff.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log`.\\n   429\\t\\n   430\\t## 9. Source index\\n   431\\t\\n   432\\tDesign: `docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` (7.3,\\n   433\\t7.4.1-7.4.3, 7.11); `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md` (header, 2.2,\\n   434\\t2.4, 7 pointer); `docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` (4.1, 7.2);\\n   435\\t`docs/design/dao.jing.md` (open items); `docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md`\\n   436\\t(5.2, 9, 11 item 12, 12).\\n   437\\t\\n   438\\tCollab: `1790243232166-vm-engineer-ucf-phase1.prompt.md` (phase 1\\n   439\\tbrief); `1790268690622-reviewer-ucf-phase1.glm-flash.findings.md`;\\n   440\\t`1790280923723-architect-ucf-phase1-signoff.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log`;\\n   441\\t`1790320431837-architect-linker-m2-gate.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log`;\\n   442\\t`1790320567120-compiler-engineer-ucf-revisions.prompt.md` (this\\n   443\\ttask's brief).\\n   444\\t\\n   445\\tCommits: 27fc0f9a, 394940e6, 2ce4451d, 3325815d, 3e987123,\\n   446\\tae76fe0d, b82813d8, 66d55abd, a76674a5, 0aa710e7, f05599f7,\\n   447\\t3ddaa21b, e149aa31 (merge), f51077f2, c2b110a6 (merge), 1f7990d5.\\n   448\\t\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n62:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_30\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md | sed -n '134,279p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"   134\\t## 4. In-tree publication and amendments\\n   135\\t\\n   136\\tP-1. semantic.md indexes the contract (3e987123, 2026-09-17\\n   137\\t21:47:13 +0700, \\\"publish the ast-to-bytecode lowering profile (D1)\\\").\\n   138\\tsemantic.md 2.4 gains \\\"The `\\\"v2\\\"` execution contract, published\\\":\\n   139\\teach of the components of section 2 here indexed to its home, \\\"the\\n   140\\twhole `\\\"v2\\\"` contract by reference, not a duplicate of it\\\", and the\\n   141\\tdocument header gains its first revision line. What changed: the\\n   142\\tcontract's name and homes became published text; a sequence of\\n   143\\trevisions did not. Thus its header's claim to have closed UCF 7.11\\n   144\\twas premature (section 7.1). The commit also corrected the opcode\\n   145\\talias, defaults, and well-formedness description. Sections amended:\\n   146\\tsemantic.md 2.4, 2.6, and header. Images: none existed. The header\\n   147\\tself-dates 2026-09-18; the commit is dated 2026-09-17 (section 7.2).\\n   148\\t\\n   149\\tP-2. UCF baseline committed (ae76fe0d, 2026-09-18 10:57:45 +0700,\\n   150\\t\\\"U12: Commit UCF baseline\\\"). First committed carrier of UCF\\n   151\\t7.3.1-7.3.4 and 7.4 as current text: the resolved-interpretation\\n   152\\tinput rule (a repeated single-valued attribute keeps its last value;\\n   153\\t\\\"the load-bearing choice\\\"), saturation of defaulted operands,\\n   154\\tadmissible slots, the no-ordering rule, the address\\n   155\\t`(dao.jing/segment-key vector)`, the addressed object being the\\n   156\\tvector itself, the two loading paths, the three-step resolution\\n   157\\torder, and two-check verification (hash, then grammar). What changed\\n   158\\trelative to D-3: nothing in form -- this is the first durable\\n   159\\tcarrier. Sections amended: none. Images: none existed.\\n   160\\t\\n   161\\tP-3. Direct path made primary (b82813d8, 2026-09-18 11:00:43 +0700,\\n   162\\t\\\"U12: Amend UCF r3 for D6 resolutions\\\"). UCF 7.3.4: the projection\\n   163\\tpath is no longer the reference -- `yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` section\\n   164\\t7.2 (\\\"Conformance: both paths, one validator, one image\\\") supersedes\\n   165\\tthat designation; \\\"the direct path is primary and is the reference;\\n   166\\tthe projection path is derived from it. Both invoke the same\\n   167\\tvalidator.\\\" Sections amended: UCF 7.3.4 (loading); the stamp's named\\n   168\\tcomponents are untouched. Images: none existed.\\n   169\\t\\n   170\\tP-4. Split parked-id ruling (66d55abd, 2026-09-18 12:26:28 +0700,\\n   171\\t\\\"U14\\\"). Amends UCF 7.6.3 (referenced parked records): a missing id\\n   172\\treferenced as a code operand is reported `:yin.k/unsatisfied`; one\\n   173\\treferenced by an active value refuses the lift\\n   174\\t(`:yin.k/non-portable`, kind `:foreign-parked-ref`). Listed for\\n   175\\tcompleteness only: outside the stamp's referent.\\n   176\\t\\n   177\\tP-5. Editorial (a76674a5, 2026-09-20 17:05:41 +0700, \\\"W5\\\"). Two lines\\n   178\\tof the UCF doc rewritten to name the engine for the woken-writer\\n   179\\tretry. No contract semantics changed; the scheduler-semantics\\n   180\\tcomponent (semantic.md 3.5) is unaffected on its own text.\\n   181\\t\\n   182\\t## 5. Implementation and integration records\\n   183\\t\\n   184\\tI-0. Address algorithm registry (0aa710e7, 2026-09-23 16:50:31\\n   185\\t+0700, H1; hardened by f05599f7, 17:35:50 +0700, H2).\\n   186\\t`dao.jing/segment-key` changed its implicit minting default from\\n   187\\tSHA-256 to BLAKE3 and introduced a closed algorithm registry.\\n   188\\tBefore the CBOR swap, existing SHA-256 addresses remained verifiable\\n   189\\tby their embedded algorithm, but re-minting with the new default earned\\n   190\\ta different address. `semantic.cljc` changed to verify a claimed\\n   191\\taddress by its algorithm. The `v2` vector and execution semantics\\n   192\\tdid not change; no UCF-stamped image yet existed. UCF 7.3.2's\\n   193\\tSHA-256 example and section 4.1 of `yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`\\n   194\\tdescribe the earlier address era, not the current default.\\n   195\\t\\n   196\\tI-1. Address basis: canonical CBOR (3ddaa21b, 2026-09-25 06:56:59\\n   197\\t+0700; merge e149aa31, \\\"dao-jing-cbor-swap\\\"). `dao.jing/segment-key`\\n   198\\tnow derives from `dao.jing.cbor/encode`; dao.jing.md's open item is\\n   199\\tclosed as \\\"Retired by the canonical CBOR codec (2026-09-24)\\\": the\\n   200\\ttransitional order-normalized printer and its residuals (symbol\\n   201\\tmetadata not address-significant, pathological print collisions,\\n   202\\tambient print-var leakage) are gone. What changed: the vector form\\n   203\\tand the stamp did not; the address function beneath `segment-key`\\n   204\\tdid -- exactly the event UCF 7.3.2 forecast: \\\"when the pinned byte\\n   205\\tencoding lands, `:yin.code/hash` values change with every other\\n   206\\tminted address.\\\" Sections amended: none in UCF; the inherited basis\\n   207\\tit cites. Image validity: addresses minted under the transitional\\n   208\\tprinter do not verify after the swap and must be re-minted.\\n   209\\tIn-tree, no UCF-stamped image predates the swap: the phase 1 commit\\n   210\\tlanded 21 seconds later, in the same merge window.\\n   211\\t\\n   212\\tI-2. UCF phase 1: the stamp becomes executable (f51077f2, 2026-09-25\\n   213\\t06:57:20 +0700; merge c2b110a6, \\\"ucf-phase1\\\"). Carriers and changes:\\n   214\\t\\n   215\\t- `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf.cljc` (new): `contract-stamp` (ucf.cljc:38-47)\\n   216\\t  is the first executable carrier of\\n   217\\t  `{:yin.code/contract \\\"v2\\\", :yin.k/version 0}`;\\n   218\\t  `batch->canonical-instruction-vector` implements UCF 7.3.2\\n   219\\t  (index-batch collapse, saturation defaults, refs resolved to pcs,\\n   220\\t  `:non-canonicalizable` refusals, and a `well-formed-vector?` check\\n   221\\t  so the function only yields vectors `load-vector` accepts);\\n   222\\t  `code-address` delegates to `(jing/segment-key v)`; `canonicalize`\\n   223\\t  bundles the UCF 7.9 outcome; `safepoints` derives the static half\\n   224\\t  of UCF 7.4.2, plus `:yin.safepoint/reasons` (ucf.cljc:351),\\n   225\\t  corrected by the section 8 ruling.\\n   226\\t- `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc` (+6/-12): a claimed\\n   227\\t  `:yin.code/hash` is now verified by re-canonicalizing the batch\\n   228\\t  through `ucf/batch->canonical-instruction-vector` -- the saturated\\n   229\\t  vector -- instead of the loader's former local, unsaturated\\n   230\\t  `canonical` fn (the reviewer cites the removed fn at semantic.cljc\\n   231\\t  585-599 pre-phase-1; the verification now sits at semantic.cljc\\n   232\\t  605-616). The reviewer verified this as \\\"the intended, documented\\n   233\\t  fix for the unsaturated local canonicalizer; the coupling is\\n   234\\t  justified.\\\"\\n   235\\t- `test/yin/vm/ucf_test.cljc` (new, 622 lines).\\n   236\\t\\n   237\\tSections amended: none in the design docs; this revision binds the\\n   238\\tpublished text to executable behavior for the first time. Image\\n   239\\tvalidity: phase 1 is the first carrier of the `v2` stamp, so there\\n   240\\tis no earlier stamped image to invalidate, and per UCF 7.3.3 stamped\\n   241\\tcode indexes start here. Holding the current `dao.jing` encoder and\\n   242\\talgorithm fixed, an unstamped batch stating every defaulted operand\\n   243\\tkeeps its former address; one omitting a defaulted operand now earns\\n   244\\tthe saturated address, so an address minted by the pre-phase-1\\n   245\\tunsaturated path for that batch no longer verifies. The architect\\n   246\\tsign-off records the intent: \\\"The loader's\\n   247\\texecutable image remains unchanged for accepted batches; claimed-\\n   248\\thash acceptance changes intentionally.\\\"\\n   249\\t\\n   250\\tGates recorded on I-2 (not yet discharged in-tree):\\n   251\\t\\n   252\\t- Reviewer verdict READY with seven P3s, none blocking\\n   253\\t  (`collab/1790268690622-reviewer-ucf-phase1.glm-flash.findings.md`),\\n   254\\t  including the `:reasons` finding (section 8 here) and the\\n   255\\t  saturation nil-vs-truthiness P3 at ucf.cljc:171.\\n   256\\t- Architect verdict GRANTED with Phase 2 gates\\n   257\\t  (`collab/1790280923723-architect-ucf-phase1-signoff.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log`):\\n   258\\t  P2 -- the revision history (this document); P3 -- `:reasons`\\n   259\\t  (section 8 here); P3 -- the transitional encoder's metadata-\\n   260\\t  bearing payloads (\\\"Storage owner: preserve or refuse metadata-\\n   261\\t  bearing payloads before UCF publishes them\\\"), which I-1's codec\\n   262\\t  swap subsequently closed on the encoder side per dao.jing.md; the\\n   263\\t  gate's owner action is not recorded as discharged anywhere\\n   264\\t  in-tree.\\n   265\\t\\n   266\\tI-3. Linker consumer specified (1f7990d5, 2026-09-25 11:42:15 +0700,\\n   267\\t\\\"add the yin.vm.linker master specification (r11 consensus)\\\"). The\\n   268\\tlinker spec's section 5.2 fixes the `:yin.semantic/code` format\\n   269\\trecord with `:contract \\\"v2\\\"`: \\\"a link request for this format\\n   270\\tcarries the stamp, and the receiver's index is keyed per stamp (UCF\\n   271\\tsection 7.3.3)\\\". The format field names the execution revision;\\n   272\\t`:yin.k/version 0` remains the UCF envelope version. Loading is\\n   273\\t`yin.vm.semantic/load-vector`. This is a design consumer, not a new\\n   274\\truntime implementation or a contract revision. The M2 attempt is gated\\n   275\\tREQUEST CHANGES / Sign-off DENIED\\n   276\\t(`collab/1790320431837-architect-linker-m2-gate.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log`,\\n   277\\t2026-09-25 07:17:54 GMT) on record-scanner, Dart-typing, and\\n   278\\tfetch-bounds P1s -- implementation defects, not contract revisions.\\n   279\\t\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n64:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_31\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md | sed -n '280,365p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"   280\\t## 6. Pending amendments named but not landed\\n   281\\t\\n   282\\t- Contract-pinned AST and semantic identities. From the linker\\n   283\\t  spec's open items: \\\"Pinning `:yin.ast/code` and\\n   284\\t  `:yin.semantic/code` identities to a contract hash, with explicit\\n   285\\t  identity-to-address indexes, is an amendment to UCF section 7.3.2\\n   286\\t  and `yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` section 4.1.\\\" Named, not landed; it\\n   287\\t  would amend the referent's address semantics. Section 4.1 concerns\\n   288\\t  the root-row grain of AST identity, not the UCF vector's grain;\\n   289\\t  the proposed index must keep those two distinct.\\n   290\\t- M4's \\\"the UCF table amendments\\\" (linker spec section 9) are the\\n   291\\t  portable-encoding amendments of linker spec section 11, item 12 --\\n   292\\t  UCF 7.5.1/7.5.3 material. Outside this stamp's named components,\\n   293\\t  but required before the M4 lift driver runs.\\n   294\\t- UCF 7.11's warning stands until each blocker closes with the tests\\n   295\\t  it requires. Publication is complete here; conformance is not.\\n   296\\t\\n   297\\t## 7. Source disagreements and owner rulings\\n   298\\t\\n   299\\t7.1 Publication status. semantic.md (3e987123) claimed publication:\\n   300\\t\\n   301\\t    Revision: 1 (2026-09-18) -- the `\\\"v2\\\"` execution contract this\\n   302\\t    document names is published in full by S2.4's \\\"v2 contract\\n   303\\t    revision history\\\" note, per yin.vm.universal-continuation-\\n   304\\t    format.md S7.11's contract-revision-publication blocker. Prior\\n   305\\t    text carried no revision line; this is the first.\\n   306\\t\\n   307\\tand, in the note itself: \\\"All seven are already written, here or by\\n   308\\tdirect citation.\\\" Against it, UCF 7.11 (in-tree today) still\\n   309\\trequires \\\"a published revision history naming the complete execution\\n   310\\tcontract (S7.3.3) ... written into `yin.vm.semantic.md` S2.4 beside\\n   311\\tthe opcode table\\\", and the architect sign-off (2026-09-24 20:18 GMT,\\n   312\\ti.e. after 3e987123) still ruled:\\n   313\\t\\n   314\\t    P2, Phase 2 gate | ucf.cljc:38 | The `v2` stamp names a contract\\n   315\\t    whose complete revision history is not yet published, as S7.11\\n   316\\t    requires. | Architecture owner: publish the revision before\\n   317\\t    cross-host lowering.\\n   318\\t\\n   319\\tRuling: the sign-off and UCF 7.11 stood at the time. semantic.md 2.4\\n   320\\tpublished the component index and its then-current substance, but no\\n   321\\tchronological revision history. Its header overstated that publication.\\n   322\\tThis document now supplies the missing history, with semantic.md 2.4\\n   323\\tremaining the index of each component's operative definition. The\\n   324\\tamended semantic.md header points here. The publication gate is now\\n   325\\tclosed; M4 still must demonstrate the separate UCF conformance gates.\\n   326\\t\\n   327\\t7.2 Dates. The semantic.md header self-dates its revision line\\n   328\\t\\\"2026-09-18\\\"; its carrier commit 3e987123 is dated 2026-09-17\\n   329\\t21:47:13 +0700. The UCF header dates r2 and r3 \\\"2026-09-14\\\"; their\\n   330\\tfirst in-tree carrier ae76fe0d is dated 2026-09-18. Cited as\\n   331\\trecorded; no ordering is asserted beyond the commits' own dates.\\n   332\\t\\n   333\\t7.3 Absent artifacts. Two artifacts named by in-tree text were never\\n   334\\tcommitted to this repository (verified across all refs): the Phase 0\\n   335\\tfindings note (collab/1789221648668-architect-semantic-vm-v2-design\\n   336\\t.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md, named by the semantic.md header) and\\n   337\\tthe 2026-09-14 UCF architectural review (collab/1789387292000-\\n   338\\tarchitect-ucf-review.gpt-6-astra.findings.md, named by the UCF\\n   339\\theader). The D-0/D-1/D-2 history therefore rests on the surviving\\n   340\\tdocuments' own testimony of them.\\n   341\\t\\n   342\\t7.4 The static reasons field. UCF 7.4.2's published static map lists\\n   343\\t`:yin.safepoint/segment`, `/engine`, `/pc`, `/stack-effect`,\\n   344\\t`/lexically-required`, `/layout` -- no reasons field. Phase 1's\\n   345\\t`safepoints` publishes `:yin.safepoint/reasons` (ucf.cljc:351)\\n   346\\tanyway, described as \\\"the reasons under which the machine parks\\n   347\\tthere\\\" (ucf.cljc:339-340). Section 8 rules it a Phase 1 naming\\n   348\\terror and specifies its M4 replacement.\\n   349\\t\\n   350\\t7.5 Saturation boundary. semantic.md 2.4 says only `:gensym` prefix\\n   351\\tand `:stream-make` buffer default. The executable loader also treats\\n   352\\tan absent `:call` `:yin.code/tail?` as false\\n   353\\t(`semantic.cljc:576-580`); Phase 1 materializes that false in its\\n   354\\tvector (`ucf.cljc:77-78`). This is existing `v2` behavior, not a new\\n   355\\trevision. The semantic.md 2.4 index omits that default; this history\\n   356\\tcorrects the referent without moving the operand's home. Conversely,\\n   357\\tUCF 7.3.2 and `ucf.cljc:77` mention a default of zero for an absent\\n   358\\t`:ffi-call` argc, but semantic.md 2.6 rule 7 and `code.cljc`'s\\n   359\\t`:negative-argc` reject such a batch first. The fallback is\\n   360\\tunreachable for well-formed `v2` input. The Phase 1 reviewer flagged\\n   361\\tboth the dead FFI default and a separate falsy-prefix discrepancy:\\n   362\\tthe loader defaults `false` to `\\\"id\\\"`, while canonicalization refuses\\n   363\\tit. M4 must match the loader or narrow the claimed equivalence law\\n   364\\tbefore relying on that edge case for lift.\\n   365\\t\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n77:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_38\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md | sed -n '1192,1290p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"  1192\\t### 7.7 Dependency closure is Datalog over the tuples\\n  1193\\t\\n  1194\\tUCF §7.6.1 computes `:yin.k/requires` by a conservative fixed point over\\n  1195\\tvalues, code, names, and modules. The **code part** of that fixed point is\\n  1196\\tthe queries below, run over the flat rows of every reachable tree\\n  1197\\tand the segment-qualified rows (§6.2) of every reachable segment. The\\n  1198\\tvalue, name-satisfaction, and module parts remain the walk §7.6.1\\n  1199\\tspecifies; these queries supply their inputs and do not replace them.\\n  1200\\t\\n  1201\\tExtraction over trees (`$ast` is the union of flat rows of the reachable\\n  1202\\ttrees):\\n  1203\\t\\n  1204\\t```clojure\\n  1205\\t;; Free-name extraction is a query, not a tag match (§4.5): a :variable\\n  1206\\t;; occurrence's name is a free-name obligation unless it is bound by an\\n  1207\\t;; enclosing :lambda on ITS OWN occurrence path. This must be an\\n  1208\\t;; occurrence-relation query (§2.5's [origin root-address path]), not a\\n  1209\\t;; row-only one: content-addressed sharing means one :variable row can\\n  1210\\t;; have several occurrences, and a row-only rule that walks row parent\\n  1211\\t;; edges instead of per-occurrence paths can find a binder through one\\n  1212\\t;; occurrence's edge and wrongly call every occurrence of that row bound\\n  1213\\t;; — undercounting a real dependency, which UCF §7.6.1's conservative\\n  1214\\t;; fixed point must never do (§4.5). See `test/dao/space/query_test.cljc`'s\\n  1215\\t;; occurrence-aware rule set for the verified technique (not yet\\n  1216\\t;; root-scoped as written — §4.5 — a production query must additionally\\n  1217\\t;; constrain every rule to one root-address, since an unscoped rule set\\n  1218\\t;; can otherwise resolve a free name in one tree against a binder in a\\n  1219\\t;; different reachable tree); the shape here is illustrative, not the\\n  1220\\t;; exact syntax.\\n  1221\\t[:find ?name :in $ast $occ % ?root\\n  1222\\t :where [$occ ?root ?path ?v] [?v :variable ?name] (not (bound? ?root ?path ?name))]\\n  1223\\t\\n  1224\\t;; store keys read or written by code\\n  1225\\t[:find ?key :in $ast :where (or [$ast _ :vm/store-get ?key]\\n  1226\\t                                [$ast _ :vm/store-put ?key _])]\\n  1227\\t\\n  1228\\t;; FFI ops\\n  1229\\t[:find ?op :in $ast :where [$ast _ :dao.stream.apply/call ?op _]]\\n  1230\\t\\n  1231\\t;; parked ids named by resume\\n  1232\\t[:find ?pid :in $ast :where [$ast _ :vm/resume ?pid _]]\\n  1233\\t\\n  1234\\t;; syntax that raises an effect: the tag, to be normalized by the footprint table below\\n  1235\\t[:find ?tag :in $ast\\n  1236\\t :where [$ast _ ?tag & _]\\n  1237\\t        [(contains? #{:stream/make :stream/put :stream/cursor :stream/next\\n  1238\\t                      :stream/close :dao.stream.apply/call} ?tag)]]\\n  1239\\t```\\n  1240\\t\\n  1241\\tExtraction over segments (`$code` is the union of segment-qualified rows):\\n  1242\\t\\n  1243\\t```clojure\\n  1244\\t;; likewise over segment-qualified rows: derive free names from the\\n  1245\\t;; instruction vector's own scoping, no separate opcode needed. Unlike\\n  1246\\t;; AST rows, instructions are positionally addressed within one segment\\n  1247\\t;; (segment-address, pc), not content-shared sub-vector by sub-vector, so\\n  1248\\t;; the mixed-occurrence risk §4.5 documents for AST rows may not apply\\n  1249\\t;; here the same way — this is unverified. `linearize.cljc/lower` exists\\n  1250\\t;; today (`:162`) but takes `[e a v t m]` datoms, not the row sets this\\n  1251\\t;; design specifies (§9.1: lower must be adapted to read the row relation\\n  1252\\t;; instead); no test exercises this query against the row-set-based\\n  1253\\t;; lowering, so confirm this reasoning when that adaptation lands rather\\n  1254\\t;; than assuming it.\\n  1255\\t[:find ?name :in $code % :where [$code _ _ :var ?name] (not (bound? ?name))]\\n  1256\\t[:find ?key  :in $code :where (or [$code _ _ :store-get ?key]\\n  1257\\t                                  [$code _ _ :store-put ?key _])]\\n  1258\\t[:find ?op   :in $code :where [$code _ _ :ffi-call ?op _]]\\n  1259\\t[:find ?pid  :in $code :where [$code _ _ :resume ?pid]]\\n  1260\\t[:find ?mn   :in $code\\n  1261\\t :where [$code _ _ ?mn & _]\\n  1262\\t        [(contains? #{:stream-make :stream-put :stream-cursor :stream-next\\n  1263\\t                      :stream-close :ffi-call} ?mn)]]\\n  1264\\t```\\n  1265\\t\\n  1266\\tStore keys are extracted as values of the `key` kind (§2.2), whatever\\n  1267\\ttheir type, and are compared with the store slice's keys by value.\\n  1268\\t\\n  1269\\t#### 7.7.1 Effect normalization: the footprint table\\n  1270\\t\\n  1271\\tSyntax names effects by tag, segments by mnemonic, and profiles and\\n  1272\\t`:yin.k/requires` by **effect identifier** (`:stream/put`, UCF §7.6.1's\\n  1273\\t`:yin.k/effects`). The reference machine performs the mnemonic-to-effect\\n  1274\\tmapping in its hot loop (`semantic.cljc:360-403`: `:stream-make` raises\\n  1275\\t`{:effect :stream/make}`, `:stream-put` raises `{:effect :stream/put}`, and\\n  1276\\tso on; `:ffi-call` goes through the FFI pair, `semantic.cljc:405-420`).\\n  1277\\tThat mapping is lifted out of the machine into a **footprint table**,\\n  1278\\tversioned with the execution contract stamp (UCF §7.3.3) because the\\n  1279\\teffect a mnemonic raises is part of that contract. Extraction applies the\\n  1280\\ttable **before** any union with callable-profile effects, so the union is\\n  1281\\tover one vocabulary. The table for contract `\\\"v2\\\"`:\\n  1282\\t\\n  1283\\t+---------------+------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n  1284\\t| Syntax tag    | Mnemonic               | Effect identifiers | Other requirement contributed                                                                            |\\n  1285\\t|               |                        | contributed        |                                                                                                          |\\n  1286\\t+===============+========================+====================+==========================================================================================================+\\n  1287\\t| `:stream/make | `:stream-make`         | `#{:stream/make}`  | —                                                                                                        |\\n  1288\\t| `             |                        |                    |                                                                                                          |\\n  1289\\t+---------------+------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n  1290\\t| `:stream/put` | `:stream-put`          | `#{:stream/put}`   | —                                                                                                        |\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n80:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_39\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md | sed -n '1320,1443p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"  1320\\t| t k`          |                        |                    |                                                                                                          |\\n  1321\\t+---------------+------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n  1322\\t| `:vm/current- | `:current-continuation | `#{}`              | —                                                                                                        |\\n  1323\\t| continuation` | `                      |                    |                                                                                                          |\\n  1324\\t+---------------+------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n  1325\\t| `:literal`,   | `:const`, `:var`,      | `#{}` from syntax; | —                                                                                                        |\\n  1326\\t| `:variable`,  | `:closure`, `:push`,   | a call's effects   |                                                                                                          |\\n  1327\\t| `:lambda`,    | `:call`, `:return`,    | are its callee's   |                                                                                                          |\\n  1328\\t| `:application | `:jump`,               | profile effects    |                                                                                                          |\\n  1329\\t| `, `:if`      | `:branch-false`,       | (§7.7.2)           |                                                                                                          |\\n  1330\\t|               | `:halt`                |                    |                                                                                                          |\\n  1331\\t+---------------+------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n  1332\\t\\n  1333\\tEvery tag and every mnemonic has a row, so \\\"no external effect\\\" is an\\n  1334\\texplicit `#{}`, never an absence. `:variable`/`:var`'s \\\"Other requirement\\\"\\n  1335\\tcolumn is blank here because this table is unconditional per tag, and a\\n  1336\\t`:variable` row's contribution to the name obligation is conditional on\\n  1337\\twhether it is free (§4.5, §7.7.2) — that determination is a query over\\n  1338\\t`$ast`/`$code`, not a per-tag footprint fact, and is computed by the\\n  1339\\textraction queries in §7.7 rather than this table. The conformance\\n  1340\\tobligation: for every corpus tree, the requirement set computed from the\\n  1341\\ttree and the one computed from its lowered segment are **equal** in every\\n  1342\\tfield of `:yin.k/requires`. A tag or mnemonic outside the table is\\n  1343\\t`:yin.k/undecodable`, the same outcome the validators give it.\\n  1344\\t\\n  1345\\t#### 7.7.2 Name obligations are resolved per store slice, and completion is conservative\\n  1346\\t\\n  1347\\tA name extracted from code is an **obligation** when it is a **free**\\n  1348\\t`:variable` row's `name` — free per the query of §4.5, not per a\\n  1349\\tseparate tag. This design preserves named variables across the Semantic\\n  1350\\tTuple boundary, and keeps a single resolution path: the evaluator looks up\\n  1351\\tevery `:variable` name through `resolve-var`'s existing precedence, env →\\n  1352\\tstore → primitives → modules (`engine.cljc:46-58`, `ast_walker.cljc:361-363`,\\n  1353\\t`semantic.cljc:256-259`) — this is already what today's code does, and\\n  1354\\tthis design does not change it. What changes is only how the obligation\\n  1355\\tset is computed: not by scanning for a distinct tag, but by the §4.5/§7.7\\n  1356\\tquery over which `:variable` names are free.\\n  1357\\t\\n  1358\\t**Under-arity calls leave missing parameters bound to `nil`, not unbound.**\\n  1359\\tAn under-arity call leaves missing parameter names bound to `nil`; an\\n  1360\\tover-arity call drops extra arguments beyond the params list length. This\\n  1361\\twas a deliberate execution-contract change from the prior `zipmap`-based\\n  1362\\tbinding, which left a missing parameter name absent from the extended\\n  1363\\tenvironment rather than explicitly `nil` — so it could fall through to\\n  1364\\twhatever the closure's own captured environment (or, transitively, the\\n  1365\\tstore/primitives/modules chain) had under that name. Implemented as a\\n  1366\\tsingle shared helper, `yin.vm.engine/bind-params`, called from every\\n  1367\\tclosure-application site in both v2 evaluators\\n  1368\\t(`yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:192,513,555`, `yin/vm/semantic.cljc:200`);\\n  1369\\tthe v1 ast-walker this rule originally also described no longer exists,\\n  1370\\tdeleted by `yin.vm.v1-retirement.implementation-plan.md`. A missing\\n  1371\\tparameter is `nil` and shadows any such fallthrough within the closure's\\n  1372\\tbody. This is independent of §4.5's free/bound decision: it is about\\n  1373\\tparameter binding at closure application, not about resolving a name to\\n  1374\\ta value.\\n  1375\\t\\n  1376\\tEvery retained obligation is a required primitive or module export, checked by\\n  1377\\tprofile (UCF §7.5.2), and the **effects of a callable are read from its\\n  1378\\tprofile** (`:yin.k/effects`), never inferred from the name: `yin/def`\\n  1379\\tcontributes `:vm/store-put` and `require` contributes `:module/require`\\n  1380\\tbecause their profiles say so. Where a retained name has no profile at the\\n  1381\\temitter, discovery is `:incomplete`; conservatively retaining every\\n  1382\\tpossible primitive and module requirement is always admissible and never\\n  1383\\tmakes a `:complete` result wrong.\\n  1384\\t\\n  1385\\tThis rule is the amendment UCF §7.6.1's *Names* paragraph needs: its\\n  1386\\tsentence that a name \\\"is satisfied if it is bound in any environment the\\n  1387\\tvalue carries\\\" is the unsound test, and this document states the\\n  1388\\tcorrect rule in its place; UCF must be amended to say the same\\n  1389\\t(§10.8).\\n  1390\\t\\n  1391\\t#### 7.7.3 The fixed point converges over work items, not addresses\\n  1392\\t\\n  1393\\tThe unit of analysis is a **work item** `[code-address context]`, not an\\n  1394\\taddress. Two closures sharing one segment address with different captured\\n  1395\\tenvironments are two work items, because each enters the segment with its\\n  1396\\town environment (`semantic.cljc:199-205`) and so reaches different values (closures, streams, cursors, parked records), which\\n  1397\\tcontribute different work items; the name obligations of the segment are the same for both. The **context** of a work item is a finite abstraction: the\\n  1398\\tclosure's captured environment, and nothing else. This context\\n  1399\\tcontributes values (such as closures or streams bound within it), not\\n  1400\\tname discharge. Because values are finite, the set of contexts is finite\\n  1401\\tand the iteration terminates.\\n  1402\\t\\n  1403\\t- **Code once, contexts each.** Code is fetched, validated, and projected\\n  1404\\t  once per address; every newly discovered `[address context]` pair is\\n  1405\\t  analyzed, whether or not the address was seen before.\\n  1406\\t- **Parked ids.** Each extracted parked id must name a record in the\\n  1407\\t  carried `:parked` slice (UCF §7.6.3). A missing pid referenced as a\\n  1408\\t  code operand is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the id (an unsupplied\\n  1409\\t  requirement). A missing pid referenced by an active value (e.g., a\\n  1410\\t  captured parked continuation) refuses the lift (`:yin.k/non-portable`,\\n  1411\\t  `:foreign-parked-ref`), as it represents an unresolvable graph cycle.\\n  1412\\t- **Values.** Closures, streams, and cursors reachable from the frame, the\\n  1413\\t  K frames, parked records, the store slice, and the pending wait\\n  1414\\t  contribute work items and stream identities as §7.6.1 specifies; a\\n  1415\\t  closure contributes `[its segment address, its captured-env context]`.\\n  1416\\t- **Modules.** A required module with an undeclared footprint sets\\n  1417\\t  `:yin.k/discovery :incomplete`, as §7.6.1 already rules.\\n  1418\\t\\n  1419\\t**Convergence** is reached when one full pass adds nothing to any of:\\n  1420\\tthe work-item set, the obligation set, the discovered\\n  1421\\tvalue set (closures, streams, cursors, parked records), the store-key\\n  1422\\trequirement, the FFI-op requirement, the effect set after normalization\\n  1423\\t(§7.7.1), and the callable and module footprints. A pass that adds a new\\n  1424\\tcontext, a new profile fact, or a new footprint continues the iteration\\n  1425\\teven when no new address appeared.\\n  1426\\t\\n  1427\\tDiscovery is `:complete` only when every reachable work item was\\n  1428\\tanalyzed, every free-`:variable` obligation was discharged by the store slice by key or retained\\n  1429\\tas a profiled primitive or module requirement, every parked id resolved, and every module\\n  1430\\tfootprint was declared. It is `:blocked` when an address cannot be\\n  1431\\tfetched and `:incomplete` otherwise. Recasting the walk as Datalog changes\\n  1432\\twhere the facts come from, not the conservatism of the closure.\\n  1433\\t\\n  1434\\tUCF §7.6.1 has the same defect in its own terms: \\\"repeats until nothing\\n  1435\\tnew is reachable\\\" is stated over segments and values, and its walk of \\\"a\\n  1436\\tnewly fetched segment\\\" is per address. It needs this same amendment\\n  1437\\t(§10.8).\\n  1438\\t\\n  1439\\tBecause the queries are over data, they answer for a tree that has never\\n  1440\\trun, which is the property §7.6.1 names.\\n  1441\\t\\n  1442\\t---\\n  1443\\t\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n86:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_42\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '1350,1493p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"  1350\\t    receiver binding of the same name can neither supply nor shadow\\n  1351\\t    the module's value.\\n  1352\\t  - *Run time.* While a closure carrying `:store-of m` executes, the\\n  1353\\t    module store of m is its **active store**: free reads resolve\\n  1354\\t    through it first and the receiver's free env, primitives, and\\n  1355\\t    registry second; the ambient store does not participate. Direct\\n  1356\\t    store instructions route by the same context -- `:store-put`\\n  1357\\t    (and the `:vm/store-put` effect) writes the active module\\n  1358\\t    store, and `:store-get` reads it with **no ambient fallback**:\\n  1359\\t    a key the module store lacks is absent, exactly as a top-level\\n  1360\\t    read of an absent key is; engine resources are unreachable\\n  1361\\t    either way, by the private-resource rule below. The context\\n  1362\\t    threads through calls: a frame records the caller's active\\n  1363\\t    `:store-of` (or none), applying a closure makes that closure's\\n  1364\\t    `:store-of` active, and returning restores the frame's, so a\\n  1365\\t    module closure calling another module's closure routes each\\n  1366\\t    body's reads and writes to its own module. A tail call that\\n  1367\\t    empties the continuation -- the closure's body was the whole\\n  1368\\t    computation -- clears the active context with the last frame,\\n  1369\\t    and the VM admits new top-level input only with no active\\n  1370\\t    context, which `yin.repl`'s append asserts, so input after a\\n  1371\\t    module closure applied never routes through a stale module\\n  1372\\t    store. Sibling exports in one task share one store instance,\\n  1373\\t    so a write one makes is visible to the next; each task holds\\n  1374\\t    its own instances, so writes never cross tasks. A declared\\n  1375\\t    obligation the store does not hold falls through to the\\n  1376\\t    receiver's bindings exactly as step 5b checked them; a\\n  1377\\t    conditional definition that ran overwrites the module's own\\n  1378\\t    slot, which is the publisher's semantics, not a shadow.\\n  1379\\t  - *In the child.* The same discipline holds transitively: a\\n  1380\\t    dependency's closure runs against the dependency's store\\n  1381\\t    instance in the child's state, its mutations are what the lift\\n  1382\\t    snapshots above, and an export a child re-exports carries that\\n  1383\\t    dependency's `:store-of`, never the child's ambient bindings.\\n  1384\\t\\n  1385\\t  **Private engine resources (r8).** r7 let `:store-get` fall back\\n  1386\\t  to the ambient store for engine-filed resource cells. That\\n  1387\\t  fallback was a forging vector: `engine/gensym` mints resource\\n  1388\\t  keys from the VM's counter (`:stream-0`, `:cursor-1`), the ids\\n  1389\\t  are predictable, and a linked module could emit\\n  1390\\t  `[:store-get :stream-0]` and read a host handle no publisher\\n  1391\\t  handed it. The split is therefore physical, not conventional:\\n  1392\\t  stream handles, cursor cells, and the FFI pair live in a private\\n  1393\\t  `:resources` table in VM state; the engine's own machinery\\n  1394\\t  (wait-set resolution, handle creation and polling, resume) reads\\n  1395\\t  and writes only that table; and no user store instruction and no\\n  1396\\t  `resolve-var` step consults it, at top level or inside a module\\n  1397\\t  closure. The ids may stay predictable -- they name nothing a\\n  1398\\t  store instruction can reach. This amends the store model UCF\\n  1399\\t  section 7.6.2 itself states -- that section opens by listing what\\n  1400\\t  the store S holds (listed with the other amendments in section\\n  1401\\t  11, item 12; r8's citation of a \\\"UCF 4.1\\\" named no such section,\\n  1402\\t  corrected r10): a migration slice still carries resource\\n  1403\\t  cells, remapped at resume, but beside the program store, never\\n  1404\\t  inside it.\\n  1405\\t\\n  1406\\t  **Resource lowering (r9).** The continuation format's own decode\\n  1407\\t  rules move with the split, or the private table is bypassed at\\n  1408\\t  every boundary crossing: UCF section 7.5.1's table decodes a\\n  1409\\t  `:yin.k/stream` marker to \\\"an attached handle, under a fresh\\n  1410\\t  store key\\\" and a `:yin.k/cursor-ref` to \\\"a store cursor entry\\\",\\n  1411\\t  and section 7.5.3 lowers each logical cell to \\\"a fresh store\\n  1412\\t  cursor entry\\\". From r9 those decode targets are the private\\n  1413\\t  table, as an amendment to UCF sections 7.5.1 and 7.5.3 (added to\\n  1414\\t  the list in section 11, item 12): lowering installs an attached\\n  1415\\t  handle under a fresh **resource id** in `:resources`, lowers a\\n  1416\\t  logical cell to a cursor entry in `:resources` seeded with its\\n  1417\\t  carried position, and remaps every `:yin.k/cursor-ref` to that\\n  1418\\t  entry; two refs to one cell still share one entry. Program\\n  1419\\t  values keep only the non-forgeable references -- the opaque\\n  1420\\t  `stream-ref` and `cursor-ref` ids, exactly as a running program\\n  1421\\t  holds them -- and no store key is ever created. A module export\\n  1422\\t  carrying a stream or cursor reference therefore lifts and lowers\\n  1423\\t  through the same rules, the lift authenticating the reference\\n  1424\\t  first as the sealed-references rule below requires: the marker\\n  1425\\t  crosses in the slice, the\\n  1426\\t  receiver's `:resources` gains the attachment or cell, and the\\n  1427\\t  export's value in the receiver is the remapped reference,\\n  1428\\t  indistinguishable from one the receiver made itself.\\n  1429\\t\\n  1430\\t  **Sealed references (r10).** r9 moved resources out of the store,\\n  1431\\t  but a reference is still a plain map with a predictable id\\n  1432\\t  (`{:type :stream-ref, :id :stream-0}`), and an effect carries\\n  1433\\t  whatever value the program supplies: a program or linked module\\n  1434\\t  can write the literal itself and name a resource it was never\\n  1435\\t  handed -- another task's stream, a cursor cell, or the FFI pair's\\n  1436\\t  ids. Shape checks are not the answer (UCF 7.5.1 refuses every\\n  1437\\t  \\\"looks like data\\\" rule); unguessability is. Each task's resources\\n  1438\\t  are bound to a **task-scoped capability secret**, minted once by\\n  1439\\t  the composition at task creation from its own random source and\\n  1440\\t  held in VM state -- never in a program value, never on a stream,\\n  1441\\t  never counter-derived. Every reference the engine issues -- a\\n  1442\\t  `stream-ref`, a `cursor-ref`, an FFI cell id -- carries a seal\\n  1443\\t  over its id under that secret, and every effect dispatch that\\n  1444\\t  resolves a program-supplied reference verifies the seal against\\n  1445\\t  the active task's secret before touching `:resources`. A literal\\n  1446\\t  with a wrong or missing seal fails closed with\\n  1447\\t  `:forged-resource-reference`, naming the effect and the id it\\n  1448\\t  named: the effect boundary, not the store instruction, was the\\n  1449\\t  last read path, and r10 closes it. Lift and lower re-seal rather\\n  1450\\t  than carry, and **lift authenticates before it encodes** (r11):\\n  1451\\t  stripping a seal unexamined would launder one, because r10\\n  1452\\t  verified seals only at dispatch -- a module could export a\\n  1453\\t  fabricated reference literal that never passed an effect in the\\n  1454\\t  child, and a lift that merely dropped the seal would emit a\\n  1455\\t  portable marker the lower then mints into a valid,\\n  1456\\t  receiver-sealed capability. From r11 the lift, before emitting\\n  1457\\t  a `:yin.k/stream` or `:yin.k/cursor-ref` marker, verifies the\\n  1458\\t  reference's seal and resource kind against the emitter task's\\n  1459\\t  own capability secret; an invalid, unsealed, or forged reference\\n  1460\\t  refuses the export lift immediately, as `:yin.k/non-portable`\\n  1461\\t  with `:yin.k/kind :forged-resource-reference`. Only an\\n  1462\\t  authenticated reference is encoded without its seal (the marker\\n  1463\\t  is the portable encoding), and the lower, having installed the\\n  1464\\t  attachment or cell in the receiver's `:resources`, issues a\\n  1465\\t  fresh reference sealed under the receiver's secret -- which is\\n  1466\\t  what r9's \\\"remapped reference\\\" now means. One task's literals\\n  1467\\t  cannot guess another task's secret, so cross-task forgery and\\n  1468\\t  the export-laundering path fail with the same refusal. This\\n  1469\\t  amends UCF section 7.5.1's reference markers (added to the list\\n  1470\\t  in section 11, item 12).\\n  1471\\t  Executing exports inside the owning child instead was considered and\\n  1472\\t  rejected: it keeps a child VM alive for the parent's lifetime, and\\n  1473\\t  every application of an export becomes a function call across a VM\\n  1474\\t  boundary, which is the coupling the axioms forbid. Relocation costs\\n  1475\\t  one lift and one lower per install and nothing afterwards, and it is\\n  1476\\t  the same machinery UCF already needs, exercised in-process. The\\n  1477\\t  offset table it needs is one vector of `[identity offset length]` per\\n  1478\\t  positional kernel, appended to by `attach-image`; `yin.repl` already\\n  1479\\t  computes the offset and discards it, so this is recording a fact the\\n  1480\\t  loader has in hand, not a new computation. `attach-image` itself is\\n  1481\\t  the one new kernel operation this section asks for, and it is the\\n  1482\\t  append half of an operation `yin.repl` already performs.\\n  1483\\t- **`refused` restores every waiter with the refusal** as the effect's\\n  1484\\t  error, and the install entry is removed so a later require may try\\n  1485\\t  again.\\n  1486\\t\\n  1487\\tThe alternative, per-format export locators in the manifest (a body pc\\n  1488\\tfor stack images, a segment and pc for semantic ones), was considered\\n  1489\\tand rejected: it persists four descriptions of a fact that one\\n  1490\\tevaluation derives, and it would make the manifest's shape depend on\\n  1491\\twhich backends exist. This is *derive, don't persist* at the module\\n  1492\\tgrain.\\n  1493\\t\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n89:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_44\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"I found a correctness gap in the ownership argument: the draft treats `(occurrence, sequence)` as a stable operation identity across a re-grant, but a second run can take a different path before that sequence number. I’m testing that claim against the draft’s crash recovery and input rules; it may change the 7.7 verdict.\"}}\n90:{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_45\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -n 'Retention and Gaps|gap|kept cursor|cursor profile|portable' docs/design/dao.stream.md | head -70\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n91:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_45\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -n 'Retention and Gaps|gap|kept cursor|cursor profile|portable' docs/design/dao.stream.md | head -70\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"43:4. A **portable descriptor** is reachability data used to attach to an existing\\n49:identity. A handle is not portable identity. A cursor is not handle-owned\\n58:| `attach!`    | portable descriptor    | handle outcome     |\\n78:- Retention may be bounded. A cursor over evicted history receives a gap\\n115:  operation returns data: closed, full, blocked, end, gap, not found,\\n215:- `attach!` consumes a portable descriptor and returns a handle on an\\n257:The **portable descriptor** is inspired by CORBA's interoperable object\\n353:When a creation specification or portable descriptor arrives dynamically —\\n392:Retention and Gaps sections govern handles with a reader surface only.\\n449:reports `gap`); another policy answers the same condition, named (a ring buffer\\n450:evicts rather than refusing, so pressure surfaces as a `gap` to a cursor that\\n502:  an evicting transport advances as values are lost. A `gap` is reported to a\\n507:  declared retention (see *Retention and Gaps*), never from an anchor.\\n513:  `gap`. It cannot be minted afterwards and cannot be reconstructed: a consumer\\n518:  a successor from `next`, or recovered from a gap outcome. Consumers never\\n547:| `:dao.stream/gap`             | This position was evicted.                                                    | `:dao.stream/cursor` (earliest retained position, or the tail when nothing is retained) |\\n565:| `:dao.stream/full`            | The transport does not accept the value; nothing was appended. Whether this is transient or permanent is the transport's declared nature (see Retention and Gaps). | —             |\\n604:what *this handle* is on. (Not every outcome is: `gap` is retention-relative\\n611:not to a handle, so a kept cursor resumes through a newly attached handle.\\n654:## Retention and Gaps\\n661:`full` on append, `gap` on read — and whether its `full` is transient or\\n672:receives a `gap`.\\n675:gap outcome from the Reading table. The stream reports honestly and decides\\n676:nothing: an interpreter replaying a log may treat a gap as fatal; a live\\n680:handle: it is precisely what a kept cursor is worth, and it is why a consumer\\n690:**A transport that excludes `gap` gives completeness.** This is the existing\\n692:the transport's nature, an unbounded log never evicts, so never reports `gap`\\n701:`gap`, and the consumer aborts or recovers on its own policy. It does not make\\n724:`gap` is impossible and is excluded for that reason.\\n760:behind is evicted past and told so with a `gap`, and the reader holding it\\n784:  kept cursors cover repositioning.\\n869:Two related gaps compound it. `append!`'s `ok` carries no correlation value, so\\n912:TBD\\\", while *Explicitly Absent* removes `seek`: a kept cursor is therefore the\\n918:invalidating every kept cursor; a durable consumer resuming after restart;\\n972:   cursor into the dropped prefix receives `gap` exactly as under eviction, and\\n1046:   > The IO model is inherited. An interpreter's portable interface is\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n93:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_46\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '654,707p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"   654\\t## Retention and Gaps\\n   655\\t\\n   656\\tRetention and overflow behavior are transport-owned and form part of the\\n   657\\tstream or writer surface's declared nature; a locally created stream records\\n   658\\tthem through its creation specification. A ring buffer retains a bounded window and evicts\\n   659\\tthe oldest; a file or append-only log retains everything; a network send\\n   660\\tbuffer refuses transiently. Which outcomes a given stream can produce —\\n   661\\t`full` on append, `gap` on read — and whether its `full` is transient or\\n   662\\tpermanent follow from the transport's declared nature. DaoStream names the\\n   663\\toutcomes and standardizes no policy.\\n   664\\t\\n   665\\t**Eviction never waits on a reader.** Retention is the stream's own declared\\n   666\\tpolicy and applies whatever any reader is doing — a stream holds no reader\\n   667\\tpositions, so it cannot be held back by a slow one. A transport that deferred\\n   668\\teviction until every reader had passed a position would make one reader's\\n   669\\tabsence every other reader's unbounded growth: a single stalled or departed\\n   670\\treader would hold the stream open forever, and a hostile one could do it\\n   671\\tdeliberately. Bounded retention is bounded, and a cursor that falls behind\\n   672\\treceives a `gap`.\\n   673\\t\\n   674\\tWhen bounded retention evicts a value, a cursor still pointing at it gets the\\n   675\\tgap outcome from the Reading table. The stream reports honestly and decides\\n   676\\tnothing: an interpreter replaying a log may treat a gap as fatal; a live\\n   677\\ttelemetry viewer may adopt the returned cursor and continue. Silently\\n   678\\tskipping evicted history is forbidden — a reader holding a cursor across an\\n   679\\teviction is told it missed values. This is a promise to a **cursor**, not to a\\n   680\\thandle: it is precisely what a kept cursor is worth, and it is why a consumer\\n   681\\tthat must know mints one at origin rather than asking later (see *Cursors*).\\n   682\\t\\n   683\\t### Complete history\\n   684\\t\\n   685\\tSome consumers require *complete* history rather than *retained* history: an\\n   686\\tinterpreter that derives state by replaying a log from its beginning is wrong,\\n   687\\tnot merely stale, if it replays a suffix. The contract already carries what\\n   688\\tsuch a consumer needs, in two mechanisms that answer different questions.\\n   689\\t\\n   690\\t**A transport that excludes `gap` gives completeness.** This is the existing\\n   691\\tdeclaration in its existing form — the outcome's precondition is impossible by\\n   692\\tthe transport's nature, an unbounded log never evicts, so never reports `gap`\\n   693\\t(see *Surfaces*). Retention is declared through the transport's nature and, for\\n   694\\ta locally created stream, through its creation specification; it is\\n   695\\tconfiguration provenance. There is no way to ask a handle about it and none is\\n   696\\tadded. A consumer requiring complete history therefore requires *a transport of\\n   697\\tthat declared nature*, and the composition that wires it is what supplies one.\\n   698\\t\\n   699\\t**A kept origin cursor gives detection.** On a transport that can evict, an\\n   700\\torigin cursor turns a loss that would otherwise be invisible into a reported\\n   701\\t`gap`, and the consumer aborts or recovers on its own policy. It does not make\\n   702\\tthe history complete; it makes incompleteness observable.\\n   703\\t\\n   704\\tBoth stand, and they are not substitutes. Where completeness is a correctness\\n   705\\trequirement, only the declared transport delivers it, and a kept origin cursor\\n   706\\tis the honest failure mode that remains when completeness was not wired. Where\\n   707\\ta consumer can proceed on a suffix but must know that it is on one, the origin\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n94:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_47\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-25 08:42:08 GMT  \\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-25 15:42:08 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\\n\\n**Verdict:** The absence of a shipping heterogeneous continuation migrator does not establish an engineering impossibility. UCF is viable as a protocol for *conforming* engines, but the present exclusive-custody contract overclaims correctness after a crash. The proposed status remains appropriate: passing linker milestones does not accept UCF; every acceptance row, including the later custody gates, must pass first. [UCF §7.1, lines 71–74](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:71) · [UCF §7.11.1, lines 1350–1359](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1350)\\n\\n1. **7.3 Code identity — FEASIBLE-WITH-CHANGES.** *Failure case:* the contract says canonicalization and execution read the same resolved value, yet the revision history records a falsy-prefix case where the loader defaults `false` and canonicalization refuses it. That breaks the claimed equivalence at an accepted input boundary. *Ruling:* use one normalization rule in both paths, or narrow admissible `v2` input and publish the resulting contract decision. The addressed vector, separate stamp, hash check, grammar check, and common validator provide a credible identity mechanism once that discrepancy and the cross-host tests close. [UCF §7.3.2, lines 160–170](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:160) · [Revisions §7.5, lines 350–364](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md:350) · [UCF §7.3.4, lines 292–321](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:292)\\n\\n2. **7.4 Safepoint reconstruction — FEASIBLE-WITH-CHANGES.** *Failure case:* a pc alone cannot recover an activation’s stack, captured environment, or actual wait reason; the current static `:reasons` field also describes possibilities as though they were observed waits. Explicit park and `:call-effect` still lack complete wire pending shapes, and cross-host kept cursors depend on a transport profile. *Ruling:* carry dynamic state from the observed parked record, replace static reasons with the ratified `:kinds` field, define or remove those two pending variants, and demonstrate a portable kept-cursor transport. A foreign engine must prove frame round trips at every declared parking transition; a same-engine transfer between hosts is insufficient evidence for that claim. [UCF §7.4.2, lines 408–452](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:408) · [Revisions §8, lines 366–425](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md:366) · [UCF §7.4.3, lines 504–519](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:504) · [UCF §7.11.1, lines 1418–1426](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1418)\\n\\n3. **7.5 Recursive portable encoding — FEASIBLE-WITH-CHANGES.** *Failure case:* the disjoint marker grammar solves literal-map ambiguity, but the current decode targets put handles and cursors in the program store, where predictable keys and forged references can expose resources. *Ruling:* land the linker’s private `:resources` table and authenticate references **before** lift, then re-seal them on lower. Keep the existing whole-value refusal for unsupported host objects and cycles. These are required protocol amendments, not optional hardening. [UCF §7.5.1, lines 541–593](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:541) · [Linker §7.3, lines 1385–1469](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:1385) · [Linker §11, lines 2065–2072](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2065)\\n\\n4. **7.6 Dependency closure — FEASIBLE-WITH-CHANGES.** *Failure case:* an address-only walk misses distinct captured environments; an undeclared module footprint or a receiver store binding can make a supposedly complete continuation run with different dependencies. A static inventory also cannot certify arbitrary future effects. *Ruling:* close the stated work-item fixed point, require module footprints and isolated task/module stores, and scope `:complete` to dependencies needed for this restore and the reachable code analyzed under declared profiles. Check newly obtained modules and effects at their later boundaries. Report `:incomplete` or refuse when the analysis cannot justify completeness. [UCF §7.6.1, lines 761–810](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:761) · [UCF §7.6.2, lines 830–847](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:830) · [Code as tuples §7.7.3, lines 1391–1432](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1391) · [Linker §8.1, lines 1624–1632](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:1624)\\n\\n5. **7.7 Ownership arbitration — INFEASIBLE-AS-SPECIFIED.** *Failure case:* the contract says that on re-grant the same `(occurrence, sequence)` identifies “the same logical operation.” That sentence cannot hold for all allowed executions. A kept cursor can report a `gap` after eviction; a re-granted run can therefore take a different branch and issue a different first effect with the same operation id. Durable dedup would return the former effect’s result for the latter. Separately, an FFI consumer cannot atomically check a remote authority’s epoch and commit an independent local side effect without a shared transactional admission boundary. *Ruling:* limit exactly-once claims to explicitly enrolled consumers whose epoch check, operation-intent check, dedup record, and side-effect commitment share an atomic boundary. For crash re-grants, durably record nondeterministic inputs or refuse a divergent operation id; otherwise declare at-least-once or fail-stop behavior. The exporting transition is still necessary and well specified, but it alone does not close this cliff. [UCF §7.7.5, lines 1083–1117](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1083) · [UCF §7.4.1, lines 369–374](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:369) · [DaoStream, *Retention and Gaps*, lines 654–702](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:654) · [UCF §7.7.4, lines 1019–1057](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1019)\\n\\n**Residual risk, highest first:** 7.7 effect fencing; 7.4 foreign-engine frame and pending-wait reconstruction; 7.6 completeness; 7.5 encoding and resource authority; 7.3 identity. The correctness cliff is **7.7**: a failed lift or missing dependency refuses work, while a duplicated or misidentified committed effect changes the outside world. The draft itself acknowledges that an unfenced stream has at-least-once delivery and schedules exclusive custody after M5. [UCF §7.9, lines 1244–1253](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1244) · [UCF §7.11.1, lines 1457–1480](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1457)\\n\\n**Strategic judgment:** Yes, as a bounded design bet for long-lived work whose completed prefix is costly to replay and which genuinely needs more than one execution backend. It avoids replaying the prefix before a checkpoint; it does **not** eliminate re-execution after a crash before the next checkpoint, as the draft’s re-grant sequence shows. Unison supports content-addressed code and stack-oriented lowering as an analogy, not this migration guarantee. [UCF §7.7.5, lines 1119–1131](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1119) · [De Bruijn stack §7.1, lines 724–743](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md:724)\\n\\nThe acceptance matrix is the right mechanism **if treated as a release gate**. Add an early, independent two-engine round trip with a nontrivial call stack, closure, and blocked effect; then test crash re-grants against an enrolled transactional consumer. M3/M4’s three-host tests and in-process module relocation are useful steps, but the matrix explicitly leaves cross-host cursors, full dependency closure, and exclusive fencing for later. If the bet is wrong, the evidence should appear as pervasive `:non-portable`/`:incomplete` refusals, inability to reconstruct frames in a second engine, or fencing that costs as much as durable replay. In that case, retain portable code and checkpointed application state while withdrawing live heterogeneous resumption. [UCF §7.11.1, lines 1393–1426](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1393) · [UCF §7.11.1, lines 1476–1509](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1476) · [Linker §9, lines 1880–1911](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:1880)\\n\\nFeasibility: VIABLE WITH CHANGES (recorded)\"}}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '595,620p' docs/design/dao.stream.md; sed -n '665,690p' docs/design/dao.stream.md; sed -n '900,930p' docs/design/dao.stream.md; sed -n '345,366p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '595,620p' docs/design/dao.stream.md; sed -n '665,690p' docs/design/dao.stream.md; sed -n '900,930p' docs/design/dao.stream.md; sed -n '345,366p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md\"","aggregated_output":"the transport as the logical stream's owner closes that logical stream; a\nserver-side accepted-connection handle is an attachment and does not gain\nownership merely by being on the serving host. An attached\nhandle is on an attachment: closing it ends that attachment only — the\nstream persists, its descriptor remains valid, and `attach!` may rejoin\nlater. Which scope a transport's handles carry is part of its declared\nnature.\n\n`:dao.stream/closed` and `:dao.stream/end` are handle-relative: they speak of\nwhat *this handle* is on. (Not every outcome is: `gap` is retention-relative\nand `cursor-mismatch` is logical-stream-relative.) After an attachment\ncloses, the handle's `append!` answers `closed`; `descriptor` still answers\n`ok` with reachability and identity — both outlive any attachment; a reader surface, where the\nhandle has one, answers `end` from `next` once observation through this\nhandle is exhausted, and `closed` from `cursor` — no new cursors are\nminted through a closed attachment. Cursors bind to the logical stream,\nnot to a handle, so a kept cursor resumes through a newly attached handle.\n\nClosing an attachment freezes what that handle can still observe: values\nappended to the logical stream afterwards are not observable through it, so\n`end` is reached at the position the stream had reached when the attachment\nclosed. And a logical stream that has itself been closed remains attachable —\n`attach!` answers `ok` and the new handle reads retained history to `end` —\nbecause close changes future availability, not identity or reachability.\n\nClosing the logical stream changes future availability only:\n**Eviction never waits on a reader.** Retention is the stream's own declared\npolicy and applies whatever any reader is doing — a stream holds no reader\npositions, so it cannot be held back by a slow one. A transport that deferred\neviction until every reader had passed a position would make one reader's\nabsence every other reader's unbounded growth: a single stalled or departed\nreader would hold the stream open forever, and a hostile one could do it\ndeliberately. Bounded retention is bounded, and a cursor that falls behind\nreceives a `gap`.\n\nWhen bounded retention evicts a value, a cursor still pointing at it gets the\ngap outcome from the Reading table. The stream reports honestly and decides\nnothing: an interpreter replaying a log may treat a gap as fatal; a live\ntelemetry viewer may adopt the returned cursor and continue. Silently\nskipping evicted history is forbidden — a reader holding a cursor across an\neviction is told it missed values. This is a promise to a **cursor**, not to a\nhandle: it is precisely what a kept cursor is worth, and it is why a consumer\nthat must know mints one at origin rather than asking later (see *Cursors*).\n\n### Complete history\n\nSome consumers require *complete* history rather than *retained* history: an\ninterpreter that derives state by replaying a log from its beginning is wrong,\nnot merely stale, if it replays a suffix. The contract already carries what\nsuch a consumer needs, in two mechanisms that answer different questions.\n\n**A transport that excludes `gap` gives completeness.** This is the existing\nreach the same logical stream through different endpoints\". *Cursors* also says\n\"a cursor's representation belongs to the transport\". Together these hold only\nif a logical stream lives on exactly one transport for its whole life.\n\n**Evidence.** Cursor maps are keyed in the minting transport's namespace\n(`:dao.stream.ringbuffer/identity` and `/position`,\n`:dao.stream.memory-log/identity` and `/position`), so a cursor from one\ntransport is `invalid-cursor` on any other, whatever identity it names. Across\nhosts the promise has never been exercised: `ws` has no reader surface, so\ninbound values are deposited on a local stream with its own identity and its\nown positions. What a remote host reads is a copy, not the stream. Finally,\n\"whether a cursor survives serialization to another host is transport-owned and\nTBD\", while *Explicitly Absent* removes `seek`: a kept cursor is therefore the\nonly checkpoint a consumer has, and whether it can be written down is\nundecided.\n\n**What breaks.** Replication and DHT replicas (which replica's positions are\nthe stream's?); re-homing a stream from memory to a durable medium without\ninvalidating every kept cursor; a durable consumer resuming after restart;\ncomparing two replicas to detect a forked history.\n\n**Proposed resolution.** Two decisions, the first a choice:\n\n1. **Choose what a logical identity ranges over.** Either\n   (a) *one origin, one transport*: a logical stream is the sequence one\n   transport instance owns. A copy on another host or medium is a different\n   logical stream with its own identity; that it mirrors the first is a fact for an\n   interpreter to record. This is how the code already behaves:\n   `dao.stream.forward` copies into a destination with its own identity. The\n   sentence\n   about different descriptors reaching one stream narrows to \"different\n`safepoints` publishes `:yin.safepoint/reasons` (ucf.cljc:351)\nanyway, described as \"the reasons under which the machine parks\nthere\" (ucf.cljc:339-340). Section 8 rules it a Phase 1 naming\nerror and specifies its M4 replacement.\n\n7.5 Saturation boundary. semantic.md 2.4 says only `:gensym` prefix\nand `:stream-make` buffer default. The executable loader also treats\nan absent `:call` `:yin.code/tail?` as false\n(`semantic.cljc:576-580`); Phase 1 materializes that false in its\nvector (`ucf.cljc:77-78`). This is existing `v2` behavior, not a new\nrevision. The semantic.md 2.4 index omits that default; this history\ncorrects the referent without moving the operand's home. Conversely,\nUCF 7.3.2 and `ucf.cljc:77` mention a default of zero for an absent\n`:ffi-call` argc, but semantic.md 2.6 rule 7 and `code.cljc`'s\n`:negative-argc` reject such a batch first. The fallback is\nunreachable for well-formed `v2` input. The Phase 1 reviewer flagged\nboth the dead FFI default and a separate falsy-prefix discrepancy:\nthe loader defaults `false` to `\"id\"`, while canonicalization refuses\nit. M4 must match the loader or narrow the claimed equivalence law\nbefore relying on that edge case for lift.\n\n## 8. Owner ruling: static kinds, dynamic reasons\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '485,538p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '680,718p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '1290,1315p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '1075,1148p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '485,538p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '680,718p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '1290,1315p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '1075,1148p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md\"","aggregated_output":"\n;; FFI call, sent (:ffi-call → ok)\n{:yin.k/reason     :ffi\n :yin.k/call-id    :call-7             ; the :dao.stream.apply/id correlation\n :yin.k/op         :op/add\n :yin.k/request    {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}   ; where the request went\n :yin.k/response   {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}   ; where the answer lands\n :yin.k/cell       :yin.k/c-23         ; the response cursor cell, §7.5.3, at its KEPT position}\n\n;; FFI call, retained (:ffi-call → full): the request is still in hand\n{:yin.k/reason     :ffi-request\n :yin.k/call-id    :call-7\n :yin.k/request-op :op/add\n :yin.k/request-args [<encoded> …]     ; enough to rebuild the envelope verbatim\n :yin.k/request    {identity/descriptor as above}\n :yin.k/response   {identity/descriptor as above}\n :yin.k/cell       :yin.k/c-23}        ; response cell at its kept position\n```\n\nThree rules make these resumable somewhere other than where they were minted:\n\n- **The response position is a kept cursor, never an anchor.** Reattaching\n  at `:oldest` replays responses that belong to other calls; attaching at\n  `:newest` skips past this call's own. The response cell therefore travels\n  at its *kept* position, and the whole task's waiters on that cell travel\n  with it (§7.6.3), so the round-order semantics of `check-wait-set` —\n  waiters sharing a cell consume successive values — are preserved on the\n  resumer exactly. A first poll that observes `gap` is the honest outcome\n  the contract already promises a kept cursor (`dao.stream.md`, *Retention\n  and Gaps*). This requires the response transport to accept a kept cursor\n  from another host, which `dao.stream.md` leaves transport-owned and TBD;\n  UCF therefore declares, per stream, a **portable cursor profile** in\n  `:yin.k/requires` (`:yin.k/cursor-profiles`), and a transport that does\n  not declare one cannot be a pending-response endpoint — the lift refuses\n  it as `:yin.k/unsatisfied` before the value is ever minted.\n- **Outstanding calls route to the emitter's pair; the resumer's pair is its\n  own.** The reference machine restores a `:request-sent` entry into a\n  response wait through its fixed local store keys (`vm/call-out-stream-key`\n  and friends). A resumer must *not* do that: the outstanding call's\n  response will arrive on the **emitter's** response stream, because that is\n  where the request was sent. The resumer re-establishes the wait against\n  the carried response descriptor and the cell's fresh local key (the cell\n  id remapped per §7.5.3), while calls the resumed program makes *after*\n  lowering use the resumer's own pair under its own keys. Pending-call\n  routing and future-call routing are separate stores of state; conflating\n  them strands the migrated call exactly as the review described.\n- **On resume, the resumer attaches and re-mints.** Attach to every\n  descriptor in the frame, pending, and cells; an attachment resolving to\n  `not-found` is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream identity, never a\n  silent `nil`; a `:put` wait retries its retained value and resumes with it\n  on `ok` (the engine, a woken writer's retry appends and stamps the\n  value); a `:next` wait polls its cell. No wait is silently dropped and no\n  retained value is recomputed — what parks is what resumes.\n\n\n**Cursor cells.** A cursor is *observable state with aliasing*: two distinct\ncursor refs may sit at the same position and yet be independently\nadvanceable store entries, while repeated refs to one cursor ref must\nadvance together — `check-wait-set` advances a shared cursor ref before\npolling the next waiter, so waiters on one ref read successive values, and\nwaiters on two refs at the same position read the same next value\n(`yin.vm.engine`, `check-wait-set`). A position-only encoding erases\nexactly this distinction: two cells at one position would collapse, and\ntheir independent advancement would be lost. The encoding therefore carries\n**logical cursor cells** separately from position content:\n\n```clojure\n:yin.k/cells {:yin.k/c-17 {:yin.k/stream   {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/stream …}\n                           :yin.k/position <opaque, exactly as the transport minted it>}}\n;; values reference cells, never positions:\n{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/cursor-ref :yin.k/cell :yin.k/c-17}\n```\n\nCell ids are minted by the emitter (stable within one lift; remapped on\nlowering) and are *not* content-addressed — a cell is a local identity, like\na tempid, and deduplicating cells by their contents is precisely the aliasing\nerasure this structure exists to prevent. On lowering, each cell becomes a\nfresh store cursor entry seeded with its carried position; every\n`:yin.k/cursor-ref` to it remaps to that entry; two refs to one cell share\none entry and one ref per cell keeps its own. The FFI response cell of\n§7.4.3 is a cell like any other.\n\nA stream reference is portable iff its transport produces a descriptor whose\n`attach!` can succeed somewhere other than the emitter's host. The encoder\ncannot know that; it encodes the descriptor and lets the resumer's `attach!`\nreport `not-found` as data. What the encoder *can* refuse is a handle whose\n`descriptor` call fails, which cannot happen under the contract, and a raw\nin-memory handle admitted by an in-process stream's admission declaration\n(`dao.stream.md`, *Creation and Attachment*), which it treats as a host\nobject.\n\n### 7.5.4 Failure is total and names its place\n\nthe review's named architectural obligations; they are blockers to\n*accepting the design*, not merely work items.\n\n- **Authority grounding and wiring.** The arbitration `dao.space`, its\n  transactor as grantor, the occurrence ledger with epochs, and the\n  attribution resolver `dao.lease.md` requires, are composition duties; the\n  first composition to ship UCF is `yin.repl.core`'s handoff demo, and\n  its wiring becomes the worked example. A composition without a\n  transactable space offers `:yin.k/fork` only.\n- **Execution identity.** Occurrence minting, the `:yin.k/origin` chain,\n  retry idempotence (one occurrence per park), and successor completion as\n  a grantor ledger transition need conformance tests, including the crash\n  windows of §7.7.5.\n- **Pending-state completeness.** Every pending variant of §7.4.3 must\n  round-trip and resume *elsewhere*: retained writes, sent and retained FFI\n  calls with both endpoints and the kept response cursor, and cursor cells\n  through the aliasing scenarios of `check-wait-set`. This depends on a\n  **portable cursor profile** in at least one transport, which\n  `dao.stream.md` leaves TBD — an external dependency UCF inherits and\n  cannot close itself.\n- **Enforceable fencing.** Epoch-checked, atomic-with-commitment admission\n  and durable op-id dedup at consumers: which consumers check, and how a\n  `dao.space` transactor consults the epoch without a callback, is a design\n  item for `dao.space.transactor.md`. Until it lands, `:yin.k/exclusive`\n  custody is at-least-once and must say so.\n- **Canonical byte encoding.** `:yin.code/hash` and `:yin.k/id` are exactly\nnamed: a partitioned consumer accepts stale effects (it cannot see the\nlapse); a delayed legitimate effect is discarded after release; repeated\neffects within one lease share one incarnation and are indistinguishable; a\nreplacement holder repeats an effect its predecessor committed; and apply\nids correlate requests with responses, they do not suppress duplicates.\nLapse-record filtering is correlation, not fencing. What UCF requires\ninstead:\n\n**Authority epochs, checked atomically with commitment.** The occurrence's\nledger carries an epoch datom, advanced by the grantor on every reclaim\n(§7.7.3) — a monotone number the possessing boundary alone can write. A\nconsumer that must be exactly-once (a `dao.space` transactor, an FFI callee\nwith side effects) admits a fenced effect only in the same act that reads\nthe epoch: the check and the commit are one transition against the\nadmission resource, never check-then-act. An effect whose incarnation's\nepoch is not the current one is refused at commitment. This is\n`dao.lease.md`'s \"fencing from the resource\" for durable resources, and it\nis a composition duty that composition owes exactly once.\n\n**Stable operation ids, and durable dedup.** Every fenced effect carries\n`:yin.k/op-id {:yin.k/occurrence O :yin.k/seq n}` — assigned by the fenced\nwriter in emission order. Op-ids are deterministic functions of the\ncheckpoint and the operation sequence: a replacement holder resuming the\n*same* occurrence replays the same op-ids, which is what makes\nacross-incarnation dedup possible at all. Exactly-once consumers keep a\ndurable `{op-id → committed result}` record, written in the same atomic\ntransition as the epoch check; a replayed op-id returns the recorded result\nand commits nothing. Within one incarnation and across incarnations, the\nsame op-id is the same logical operation — the room for exactly-once\nsemantics that lease identity alone cannot provide.\n\n**Where the stamp rides.** Program values are program data; UCF never\nedits them. A resumed incarnation running under a lease emits through a\n**fenced writer** the composition wires: a writer that wraps each appended\nvalue in an envelope `{:yin.k/envelope … :yin.k/incarnation <lease>\n:yin.k/op-id … :yin.k/value <the program value, verbatim>}`. Exactly-once\nconsumers read stamped envelopes; a stream wired bare — no fence — is\n*declared* unprotected, and a composition that needs exactly-once delivery\nthrough it has wired the wrong thing. Fencing is opt-in per stream, exactly\nas `dao.lease.md` places the burden: \"a holder needing exclusion obtains it\nfrom the resource.\" No callback tells a lapsed holder to stop; it stops at\nits own lease bound (`dao.lease.md`, *The holder*), and what it did past\nthe bound is distinguishable after the fact by epoch and op-id.\n\n**Durable completion of a checkpoint.** Publication of a successor is not\ncompletion. The holder's exit sequence is: append the successor value to\nthe carrier, append `:yin.k/resumed` (evidence) to the arbitration medium,\nappend `:dao.lease/released`. Completion is the **grantor's ledger\ntransition** — observing the release (or the `:resumed` evidence followed\nby release) and closing the occurrence's tenure. The crash windows are then\nbounded and harmless: a crash after the successor append but before the\nrecords leaves an orphan successor — content-addressed, idempotent,\nre-readable as evidence but granting nothing; the lease lapses by silence;\nthe grantor re-grants the *last recorded* occurrence, and the orphan chain\nis queryable history (§7.7.6). A crash mid-sequence never requires the\ngrantor to trust a holder's report as proof of durable completion, because\nthe report is evidence and the ledger is authority.\n\n**Partition policy, stated honestly.** A fenced consumer that cannot reach\nthe authority (epoch unreadable) must **suspend protected effect\ncommitment** — fail closed — or the composition has chosen at-least-once\ndelivery with after-the-fact dedup reconciliation; which one is a declared\nproperty of the consumer, and a consumer that neither checks nor declares\nis not exactly-once and may not claim to be. A holder partitioned from the\narbitration medium runs to its own lease bound and its effects are\nafter-the-fact distinguishable, which is the guarantee `dao.lease.md`\nactually makes; UCF does not promise more. A grantor that lost its ledger\nreclaims and re-grants (`dao.lease.md`, *Restart*); **permanent** authority\nloss breaks exclusive custody irreparably — the ledger was the resource —\nand repair is a governance act outside this protocol. The arbitration\nmedium's durability is therefore a composition duty owed before\n`:yin.k/exclusive` is offered at all (`dao.lease.md`, *Composition\nduties*: a judge whose durability matches the resource).\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
